M1 = "Physical Quantities, Dimensional Analysis & Uncertainties"
M2 = "Kinematics, Linear Dynamics & Collisions"
M3 = "Statics, Work, Energy, Circular & Rotational Motion"

LESSONS = []

# ================================================================ M1 / Base and derived units
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Base and derived units",
    title="SI base units, derived units and prefixes",
    slug="phl1-si-base-and-derived-units",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Every physical quantity is a number times a unit. Learn the seven SI base units, build derived units such as the newton, joule and volt from them, and convert between prefixes.",
    objectives=[
        "State the seven SI base quantities and their base units",
        "Express a derived unit (N, Pa, J, W, C, V, ohm) in terms of base units",
        "Use prefixes from pico to tera and convert between units, including units of area and volume",
        "Write answers in standard form with the correct unit",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Physical quantity", "SI base unit", "Derived unit", "Prefix", "Unit conversion"],
    blocks=[
        H("Why units matter"),
        T(r"A trader in Kumba sells cocoa by the kilogram. A bus leaves Douala at 90 km/h. A phone charger delivers 2 A at 5 V. In every case a **number** is useless on its own: 90 what? Physics reports every measurement as a **physical quantity**: a number multiplied by a unit."),
        D(r"A **physical quantity** is a property that can be measured. Its value is written as a numerical magnitude multiplied by a unit.", title="Physical quantity",
          formula=r"\text{quantity} = \text{number} \times \text{unit}", note="Example: the length of a classroom is 8.0 m, that is, 8.0 times one metre."),
        T(r"Scientists everywhere use one system of units, the **SI** (from the French *Système International d'unités*). It is built from a small set of **base units**; every other unit is made from these."),
        H("The seven SI base quantities"),
        T(r"The SI has seven base quantities. All the measurements in this A Level course can be built from them. You must know the name and symbol of each base unit."),
        CODE("""Base quantity          Unit        Symbol
length                 metre       m
mass                   kilogram    kg
time                   second      s
electric current       ampere      A
thermodynamic temp.    kelvin      K
amount of substance    mole        mol
luminous intensity     candela     cd""", "text", title="The SI base units"),
        T(r"Notice two things. First, the base unit of mass is the **kilogram**, not the gram: it is the only base unit that already contains a prefix. Second, temperature is measured in **kelvin**. A temperature *difference* of 1 K is the same size as a difference of $1\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}$, but 0 K is absolute zero, about $-273.15\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}$."),
        TIP(r"Since 2019 the SI defines its base units from fixed values of constants of nature (for example the speed of light and Planck's constant). You do not need the exact wording of the definitions for this course: check your textbook for how much detail your teacher expects."),
        H("Derived quantities and derived units"),
        D(r"A **derived unit** is made by multiplying and dividing base units. A derived quantity is any quantity whose unit is a derived unit.", title="Derived unit",
          note="Speed = distance / time, so its unit is m / s, written m s⁻¹."),
        T(r"Some derived units are used so often that they have their own name, such as the newton or the joule. A named unit is only a shortcut for a combination of base units. To find the base units of a derived quantity, start from an equation that defines it and replace each symbol by its unit."),
        CODE("""Quantity        Unit (symbol)   In base units
force           newton (N)      kg m s^-2
pressure        pascal (Pa)     kg m^-1 s^-2
energy, work    joule (J)       kg m^2 s^-2
power           watt (W)        kg m^2 s^-3
charge          coulomb (C)     A s
potential diff. volt (V)        kg m^2 s^-3 A^-1
resistance      ohm             kg m^2 s^-3 A^-2
frequency       hertz (Hz)      s^-1""", "text", title="Named derived units in terms of base units"),
        EX("Worked example 1: base units of pressure", intro=r"Pressure is force per unit area. Express the pascal in SI base units.", steps=[
            S("Write the defining equation", math=r"p = \frac{F}{A}"),
            S("The newton is mass times acceleration, from $F = ma$", math=r"\mathrm{N} = \mathrm{kg}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Area is length squared", math=r"[A] = \mathrm{m^2}"),
            S("Divide", math=r"\mathrm{Pa} = \frac{\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-2}}}{\mathrm{m^2}} = \mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}\,s^{-2}}"),
        ], check=r"\rho g h:\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}\cdot\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}\cdot\mathrm{m} = \mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}\,s^{-2}}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: base units of the volt", intro=r"Electrical power is $P = IV$. Express the volt in SI base units.", steps=[
            S("Rearrange for $V$", math=r"V = \frac{P}{I}"),
            S("The watt is joule per second, and the joule is newton metre", math=r"\mathrm{W} = \frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{s}} = \frac{\mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-2}}}{\mathrm{s}} = \mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-3}}"),
            S("Divide by the ampere", math=r"\mathrm{V} = \mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-3}\,A^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        H("Prefixes"),
        T(r"Very large and very small numbers are written with **prefixes**, which multiply the unit by a power of ten."),
        CODE("""Prefix  Symbol  Factor        Prefix  Symbol  Factor
tera    T       10^12         centi   c       10^-2
giga    G       10^9          milli   m       10^-3
mega    M       10^6          micro   µ       10^-6
kilo    k       10^3          nano    n       10^-9
                              pico    p       10^-12""", "text", title="SI prefixes you need"),
        T(r"Because the base unit of mass is the kilogram, a gram is $10^{-3}$ kg. A prefix belongs to the unit that follows it: a megawatt is $10^6$ W, but a millimetre is $10^{-3}$ m."),
        EX("Worked example 3: conversions", intro=r"(a) Convert 108 km/h to m/s. (b) The density of aluminium is 2.7 g cm$^{-3}$. Convert it to kg m$^{-3}$. (c) A rice mill in Ndop uses 2.5 kW h of energy. Express this in joules.", steps=[
            S("(a) 1 km is 1000 m and 1 h is 3600 s", math=r"108\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}} = \frac{108 \times 1000\ \mathrm{m}}{3600\ \mathrm{s}} = 30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("(b) 1 g is $10^{-3}$ kg. 1 cm is $10^{-2}$ m, so 1 cm$^3$ is $(10^{-2})^3 = 10^{-6}$ m$^3$", math=r"2.7\ \frac{10^{-3}\ \mathrm{kg}}{10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^3}} = 2.7 \times 10^{3}\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}"),
            S("(c) 1 kW h is 1000 W for 3600 s", math=r"2.5\ \mathrm{kW\,h} = 2.5 \times 1000\ \mathrm{W} \times 3600\ \mathrm{s} = 9.0 \times 10^{6}\ \mathrm{J}"),
        ], check=r"1\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}} = 1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Converting areas and volumes like lengths", r"1 cm is $10^{-2}$ m, but 1 cm$^2$ is $10^{-4}$ m$^2$ and 1 cm$^3$ is $10^{-6}$ m$^3$. Raise the whole conversion factor to the power."),
        MISTAKE("Treating the gram as the base unit", r"In $F = ma$ the mass must be in kilograms. A 500 g ball has $m = 0.5$ kg. If you use 500 the force comes out 1000 times too large."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong symbols", r"Unit symbols are never plural (5 kg, not 5 kgs) and a unit named after a person has a capital letter only in the symbol (N, W, Pa) but not in the word (newton, watt, pascal). Take care: m is metre or milli, M is mega, so mW is milliwatt but MW is megawatt."),
        QUIZ(r"Which expression gives the unit of energy (the joule) in SI base units?",
             [r"$\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$\mathrm{kg\,m^{2}\,s^{-2}}$", r"$\mathrm{kg\,m^{2}\,s^{-3}}$", r"$\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$"], 1,
             r"Energy is force times distance: $\mathrm{N\,m} = \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-2}} \times \mathrm{m} = \mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-2}}$.",
             hint="Use work = force times distance.",
             feedback=[r"That is the newton, the unit of force. You forgot to multiply by the metre.", "", r"That is the watt (joule per second). Energy has one fewer power of $s^{-1}$.", r"That is the pascal, the unit of pressure."]),
        QUIZ(r"A copper wire has a cross-sectional area of $2.5\ \mathrm{mm^2}$. What is this in $\mathrm{m^2}$?",
             [r"$2.5\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m^2}$", r"$2.5\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm{m^2}$", r"$2.5\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$", r"$2.5\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$"], 2,
             r"$1\ \mathrm{mm} = 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m}$, so $1\ \mathrm{mm^2} = (10^{-3})^2 = 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$. Hence $2.5 \times 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$.",
             hint="Square the conversion factor for a length.",
             feedback=[r"You used the length factor $10^{-3}$ without squaring it.", r"You cubed the factor (that is for volume).", "", r"The area in $\mathrm{m^2}$ must be smaller than the number of mm$^2$, not larger."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Show that the unit of resistance, the ohm, can be written $\mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-3}\,A^{-2}}$.",
           r"$R = V/I$. From Worked example 2, $\mathrm{V} = \mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-3}\,A^{-1}}$. Dividing by $\mathrm{A}$: $\Omega = \mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-3}\,A^{-2}}$."),
        PQ(r"A bus travels at $72\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$. Express this speed in $\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and give the distance it covers in 15 s.",
           r"$72 \times 1000 / 3600 = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Distance $= 20 \times 15 = 300\ \mathrm{m}$."),
        PQ(r"The density of mercury is $13.6\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$. Express it in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and find the mass of $0.50\ \mathrm{L}$ of mercury (1 L $= 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m^3}$).",
           r"$13.6 \times 1000 = 1.36 \times 10^4\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$. Mass $= \rho V = 1.36\times10^4 \times 0.50\times10^{-3} = 6.8\ \mathrm{kg}$."),
        PQ(r"The frequency of a radio station is 96.5 MHz. Write this in hertz and give the period of one oscillation in nanoseconds.",
           r"$96.5\ \mathrm{MHz} = 9.65\times10^{7}\ \mathrm{Hz}$. Period $= 1/f = 1.04\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{s} = 10.4\ \mathrm{ns}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: units appear in Paper 1 (multiple choice, converting units or finding the unit of a derived quantity) and in every calculation on Paper 2, where a missing or wrong unit can lose marks."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"A physical quantity is a number times a unit; always state both.",
            r"The seven SI base units are the metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole and candela.",
            r"Derived units are products and quotients of base units, e.g. $\mathrm{N} = \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-2}}$ and $\mathrm{J} = \mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-2}}$.",
            r"Prefixes (T, G, M, k, c, m, micro, n, p) are powers of ten. Square or cube the factor for areas and volumes.",
            r"The kilogram, not the gram, is the base unit of mass."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M1 / Homogeneity of physical equations
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Homogeneity of physical equations",
    title="Dimensions and the homogeneity of equations",
    slug="phl1-dimensions-and-homogeneity",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use dimensions to check whether an equation can be right, to find the unit of an unknown constant, and to derive the form of a relationship up to a dimensionless constant.",
    objectives=[
        "Write the dimensions of a quantity in terms of M, L, T and I",
        "State and apply the principle of homogeneity to check an equation",
        "Find the dimensions and SI unit of a constant in an equation",
        "Derive the form of a formula by dimensional analysis, and state its limits",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Dimensions", "Homogeneity", "Dimensionless quantity", "Dimensional analysis", "Limits of dimensional analysis"],
    blocks=[
        H("Can you trust this formula?"),
        T(r"A technician in Limbe copies a formula from an old handbook for the speed of waves on a rope. A page is torn, and one symbol is unclear. Before he uses it, he wants a quick check that costs nothing: do the two sides of the equation even have the same *kind* of quantity? Physicists do this with **dimensions**."),
        D(r"The **dimensions** of a quantity show how it is built from the base quantities: mass $[\mathrm{M}]$, length $[\mathrm{L}]$, time $[\mathrm{T}]$ and electric current $[\mathrm{I}]$ (temperature $[\Theta]$ and amount of substance $[\mathrm{N}]$ are also base dimensions). We write the dimensions of a quantity $x$ as $[x]$.", title="Dimensions",
          formula=r"[\text{speed}] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-1}}, \qquad [\text{force}] = \mathrm{M\,L\,T^{-2}}",
          note="Dimensions are about the kind of quantity, not the unit: a speed has dimensions L T^-1 whether it is in m/s or km/h."),
        CODE("""Quantity            Dimensions
velocity            L T^-1
acceleration        L T^-2
force               M L T^-2
energy, work        M L^2 T^-2
power               M L^2 T^-3
pressure            M L^-1 T^-2
density             M L^-3
frequency           T^-1
charge              I T
angle, ratio        none (dimensionless)""", "text", title="Dimensions of common quantities"),
        T(r"A **dimensionless** quantity has no dimensions: an angle in radians (arc divided by radius), a ratio such as $v/c$, a refractive index, or a pure number such as $2\pi$ or $\tfrac{1}{2}$."),
        H("The principle of homogeneity"),
        D(r"In a correct physical equation every term has the same dimensions. Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added, subtracted or set equal. The argument of a function such as $\sin$, $\exp$ or $\ln$ must be dimensionless.", title="Principle of homogeneity",
          note="An equation that obeys this principle is called dimensionally homogeneous."),
        T(r"This gives a quick test. Find the dimensions of every term, and compare. If they differ, the equation is **certainly wrong**. If they agree, the equation *may* be right, but it is not proved."),
        EX("Worked example 1: checking equations", intro=r"Test whether (a) $s = ut + \tfrac{1}{2}at^2$ and (b) $v = u + at^2$ are homogeneous.", steps=[
            S("(a) Dimensions of each term", math=r"[s] = \mathrm{L},\quad [ut] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-1}\cdot T} = \mathrm{L},\quad [at^2] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-2}\cdot T^2} = \mathrm{L}"),
            S(r"The factor $\tfrac12$ is a pure number, so it does not change the dimensions", text="All three terms are L, so (a) is homogeneous."),
            S("(b) Dimensions of each term", math=r"[v] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-1}},\quad [u] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-1}},\quad [at^2] = \mathrm{L}"),
            S("Compare", text=r"$at^2$ is a length, but $v$ and $u$ are speeds. (b) is not homogeneous, so it is wrong. The correct form is $v = u + at$."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: the speed of sound", intro=r"A textbook gives the speed of sound in a gas as $v = \sqrt{\dfrac{\gamma p}{\rho}}$, where $p$ is pressure, $\rho$ is density and $\gamma$ is the (dimensionless) ratio of specific heat capacities. Show that it is homogeneous.", steps=[
            S(r"Dimensions of $p/\rho$", math=r"\frac{[p]}{[\rho]} = \frac{\mathrm{M\,L^{-1}\,T^{-2}}}{\mathrm{M\,L^{-3}}} = \mathrm{L^{2}\,T^{-2}}"),
            S("Take the square root", math=r"\left[\sqrt{\frac{\gamma p}{\rho}}\right] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-1}} = [v]"),
            S("Conclude", text="Both sides have the dimensions of speed, so the equation is homogeneous."),
        ], check=r"\gamma = 1.4,\ p = 1.0\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{Pa},\ \rho = 1.2\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}:\ v \approx 342\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        H("Finding the dimensions of a constant"),
        T(r"If an equation is known to be correct, homogeneity gives the dimensions (and hence the SI unit) of any constant in it. For Newton's law of gravitation $F = \dfrac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$, make $G$ the subject: $G = \dfrac{Fr^2}{m_1m_2}$. Then $[G] = \dfrac{\mathrm{M\,L\,T^{-2}}\cdot\mathrm{L^2}}{\mathrm{M^2}} = \mathrm{M^{-1}\,L^{3}\,T^{-2}}$, so the unit of $G$ is $\mathrm{kg^{-1}\,m^{3}\,s^{-2}}$."),
        H("Deriving a formula by dimensional analysis"),
        T(r"If you can list the quantities that a result depends on, you can find how it depends on each of them. Write the unknown quantity as a product of powers of the others, times a dimensionless constant $C$, then make the powers of M, L and T balance."),
        EX("Worked example 3: period of a mass on a spring", intro=r"A mass $m$ on a spring of stiffness $k$ (force per unit extension) oscillates with period $T$. Assume $T$ depends only on $m$ and $k$. Find how $T$ depends on them.", steps=[
            S("Write the assumed form", math=r"T = C\,m^{a}k^{b}"),
            S("Dimensions of $k$ (force per length)", math=r"[k] = \frac{\mathrm{M\,L\,T^{-2}}}{\mathrm{L}} = \mathrm{M\,T^{-2}}"),
            S("Equate dimensions of both sides", math=r"\mathrm{T} = \mathrm{M}^{a}\,(\mathrm{M\,T^{-2}})^{b} = \mathrm{M}^{a+b}\,\mathrm{T}^{-2b}"),
            S("Match the powers", math=r"\mathrm{M}:\ a + b = 0, \qquad \mathrm{T}:\ -2b = 1 \;\Rightarrow\; b = -\tfrac12,\ a = \tfrac12"),
            S("Result", math=r"T = C\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}}"),
        ], check=r"\text{Experiment and theory give } C = 2\pi:\ m = 0.50\ \mathrm{kg},\ k = 20\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}} \Rightarrow T = 0.99\ \mathrm{s}"),
        KEY("What dimensional analysis cannot do", r"It is a check and a guide, not a proof.", cards=[
            ["Numerical constants", r"It cannot give pure numbers such as $2\pi$ or $\tfrac12$. Only theory or experiment can."],
            ["Equal dimensions", r"It cannot tell apart quantities with the same dimensions, such as work and torque (both $\mathrm{M\,L^2\,T^{-2}}$)."],
            ["Sums of terms", r"It cannot find formulas with several terms, such as $s = ut + \tfrac12at^2$, nor functions like $\sin$ or $e^x$."],
            ["Missing variables", r"If you leave out a variable that matters, you will get a wrong but homogeneous answer."],
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Homogeneous does not mean correct", r"$s = ut + at^2$ is homogeneous but wrong (the $\tfrac12$ is missing). Passing the dimension test only means the equation is *possible*."),
        MISTAKE("Comparing units instead of dimensions", r"Write dimensions in terms of base quantities (M, L, T, I). Mixing prefixes or named units (for example $\mathrm{N}$ on one side and $\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-2}}$ on the other) makes comparisons confusing; convert to base units first."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that exponents and logs are dimensionless", r"In $N = N_0e^{-\lambda t}$ the product $\lambda t$ must be dimensionless, so $[\lambda] = \mathrm{T^{-1}}$. If you find a quantity with dimensions in an exponent, the equation is wrong."),
        QUIZ(r"A student writes the period of a simple pendulum as $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\dfrac{g}{l}}$. What does a dimensional check show?",
             [r"The equation is homogeneous, so it must be right.", r"The right-hand side has dimensions $\mathrm{T^{-1}}$, so the equation is wrong.", r"The $2\pi$ makes the two sides differ.", r"The right-hand side has dimensions $\mathrm{T^{2}}$, so the equation is wrong."], 1,
             r"$[g/l] = \mathrm{L\,T^{-2}}/\mathrm{L} = \mathrm{T^{-2}}$, and the square root has dimensions $\mathrm{T^{-1}}$, not $\mathrm{T}$. The correct form is $T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$.",
             hint="Find the dimensions of g/l, then take the square root.",
             feedback=[r"It is not homogeneous: the right side is $\mathrm{T^{-1}}$ while the left side is $\mathrm{T}$.", "", r"$2\pi$ is a pure number and has no dimensions.", r"You forgot the square root. $g/l$ is $\mathrm{T^{-2}}$ and its square root is $\mathrm{T^{-1}}$."]),
        QUIZ(r"Which statement about dimensional analysis is correct?",
             [r"It can prove that $E = \tfrac12mv^2$ is the correct formula for kinetic energy.", r"It can find the value of the constant $\tfrac12$ in the kinetic energy formula.", r"It can show that $E = mv^2$ and $E = \tfrac12mv^2$ are both dimensionally possible.", r"It can show that work and torque are different quantities."], 2,
             r"Both $mv^2$ and $\tfrac12mv^2$ have dimensions $\mathrm{M\,L^2\,T^{-2}}$. Dimensional analysis cannot find pure-number factors, nor separate quantities that share dimensions.",
             hint="What does a dimensionless number such as 1/2 do to the dimensions?",
             feedback=["It can only show that the formula is possible, not that it is right.", r"A pure number has no dimensions, so it cannot be found this way.", "", r"Work and torque both have dimensions $\mathrm{M\,L^2\,T^{-2}}$, so dimensional analysis cannot separate them."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Stokes' law gives the drag force on a small sphere of radius $r$ moving at speed $v$ through a fluid of viscosity $\eta$ as $F = 6\pi\eta rv$. Find the dimensions of $\eta$ and hence its SI unit in base units.",
           r"$[\eta] = \dfrac{[F]}{[r][v]} = \dfrac{\mathrm{M\,L\,T^{-2}}}{\mathrm{L\cdot L\,T^{-1}}} = \mathrm{M\,L^{-1}\,T^{-1}}$. The SI unit is $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ (equivalently Pa s)."),
        PQ(r"The speed $v$ of transverse waves on a stretched string depends on the tension $F$ in the string and its mass per unit length $\mu$. Use dimensional analysis to find the form of $v$.",
           r"Let $v = CF^a\mu^b$. $[\mu] = \mathrm{M\,L^{-1}}$. Then $\mathrm{L\,T^{-1}} = (\mathrm{M\,L\,T^{-2}})^a(\mathrm{M\,L^{-1}})^b$. Powers of M: $a + b = 0$. Of L: $a - b = 1$. Of T: $-2a = -1$. So $a = \tfrac12$, $b = -\tfrac12$ and $v = C\sqrt{F/\mu}$."),
        PQ(r"Bernoulli's equation for a flowing liquid contains the terms $p$, $\rho gh$ and $\tfrac12\rho v^2$. Show that all three terms have the same dimensions.",
           r"$[p] = \mathrm{M\,L^{-1}\,T^{-2}}$. $[\rho gh] = \mathrm{M\,L^{-3}\cdot L\,T^{-2}\cdot L} = \mathrm{M\,L^{-1}\,T^{-2}}$. $[\rho v^2] = \mathrm{M\,L^{-3}\cdot L^2\,T^{-2}} = \mathrm{M\,L^{-1}\,T^{-2}}$. All three are the dimensions of pressure, so they can be added."),
        PQ(r"A liquid drop oscillates with frequency $f$ which depends on its surface tension $\sigma$ (force per unit length), density $\rho$ and radius $r$. Find the form of $f$.",
           r"$f = C\sigma^a\rho^b r^c$ with $[\sigma] = \mathrm{M\,T^{-2}}$. Powers of M: $a + b = 0$. Of L: $-3b + c = 0$. Of T: $-2a = -1$. So $a = \tfrac12$, $b = -\tfrac12$, $c = -\tfrac32$ and $f = C\sqrt{\sigma/(\rho r^3)}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: homogeneity and unit-finding questions are common in Paper 1 (multiple choice) and as a short part of a Paper 2 structured question, usually worth a few marks: show the dimensions of each term, then compare them."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Dimensions describe the kind of quantity using M, L, T, I; they are written $[x]$.",
            r"In a correct equation every term has the same dimensions; arguments of $\sin$, $\exp$, $\ln$ are dimensionless.",
            r"A failed dimension check proves an equation wrong; a passed check does not prove it right.",
            r"Homogeneity gives the dimensions and SI unit of constants such as $G$ and $\eta$.",
            r"Dimensional analysis can give the form of a formula, but not pure-number constants like $2\pi$."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M1 / Systematic and random errors
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Systematic and random errors",
    title="Systematic errors, random errors, accuracy and precision",
    slug="phl1-systematic-and-random-errors",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Tell systematic errors from random errors, link them to accuracy and precision, correct zero errors, and reduce each kind of error in a real experiment.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish systematic errors from random errors and give examples of each",
        "Distinguish accuracy from precision, and error from uncertainty",
        "Correct a reading for a zero error",
        "Estimate the uncertainty in a single reading and in a set of repeated readings",
        "Suggest ways to reduce each type of error",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Systematic error", "Random error", "Accuracy", "Precision", "Zero error", "Uncertainty", "Resolution"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction: a scale that lies"),
        T(r"A trader in a market in Bafoussam weighs tomatoes on a spring balance. The pointer rests at 20 g when nothing is on the pan. Weigh the same bag of tomatoes ten times and you get a reading about 20 g too high every time. Now suppose a second, perfectly set balance, but its pointer jiggles in the wind: you get 502 g, 497 g, 500 g, 504 g. These two faults are of different kinds, and in physics we deal with them in different ways."),
        D(r"No measurement is perfect. The **error** in a measurement is the difference between the measured value and the true value. We can seldom know the true value, so a scientist also states an **uncertainty**: the range within which the true value is expected to lie.", title="Error and uncertainty",
          note="Error is about the difference from the true value. Uncertainty is the stated size of our doubt."),
        H("Random errors"),
        D(r"A **random error** varies unpredictably from one reading to the next. Repeated readings are scattered on both sides of the true value.", title="Random error",
          note="Examples: judging when a pendulum passes a mark, small changes of eye position, fluctuating mains voltage, air currents on a balance."),
        T(r"Random errors cannot be removed, but they can be **reduced** by taking many readings and calculating the **mean**. The positive and negative errors tend to cancel. They also give a **spread** in the readings, and this spread gives us an estimate of the uncertainty."),
        H("Systematic errors"),
        D(r"A **systematic error** shifts every reading by the same amount in the same direction. It does not show up as scatter, and averaging does not reduce it.", title="Systematic error",
          note="Examples: a zero error, a wrongly calibrated instrument, a metre rule with a worn end, always reading a scale from the same wrong angle."),
        T(r"Because the readings agree with each other, a systematic error can pass unnoticed. To find it, compare with a different instrument or method, check the zero of the instrument before use, or look at a graph: if theory says a line must pass through the origin but it has an intercept, something is wrong."),
        D(r"A **zero error** is a systematic error where the instrument does not read zero when the true value is zero. It is corrected by subtracting it from every reading.", title="Zero error",
          formula=r"\text{true reading} = \text{measured reading} - \text{zero error}",
          note="A zero error can be positive (instrument reads above zero) or negative (reads below zero)."),
        H("Accuracy and precision"),
        D(r"**Accuracy** describes how close a measurement is to the true value. **Precision** describes how close repeated measurements are to each other.", title="Accuracy and precision",
          note="Small systematic error means accurate. Small random error means precise."),
        IMG("al-phl1-accuracy-precision.svg", alt="Four targets showing repeated measurements: tightly grouped on the centre, tightly grouped away from the centre, scattered around the centre, and scattered and off-centre",
            caption="Figure 1. Precision is the size of the scatter. Accuracy is how close the average is to the true value (the centre)."),
        CODE("""Type of error   Effect on readings         Reduced by
random          scatter, both directions   repeat and take the mean;
                                           use a better method or
                                           a finer instrument
systematic      same shift every time      check the zero; calibrate;
                                           use a different method;
                                           correct the readings""", "text", title="The two kinds of error compared"),
        KEY("Resolution and uncertainty in one reading", r"The **resolution** of an instrument is the smallest change it can show. This sets a minimum uncertainty.", cards=[
            ["Analogue scale", r"Take about half the smallest division as the uncertainty (a ruler marked in mm: $\pm 0.5\ \mathrm{mm}$ at each end of what you read)."],
            ["Digital meter", r"Take the smallest digit as the uncertainty (a display that reads $0.01\ \mathrm{A}$ gives $\pm0.01\ \mathrm{A}$)."],
            ["Repeated readings", r"Take half the range: $\dfrac{x_{\max} - x_{\min}}{2}$, if that is larger than the resolution."],
        ]),
        TIP("Conventions differ a little between textbooks (for example, using the full smallest division for a digital meter or the standard deviation of repeated readings). Use the one your teacher or textbook states, and say what you did."),
        EX("Worked example 1: repeated readings", intro=r"A student times 20 oscillations of a pendulum five times: 31.4 s, 31.6 s, 31.2 s, 31.5 s, 31.3 s. Find the period and its uncertainty.", steps=[
            S("Mean time for 20 oscillations", math=r"\bar t = \frac{31.4 + 31.6 + 31.2 + 31.5 + 31.3}{5} = 31.4\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Uncertainty from the spread (half the range)", math=r"\Delta t = \frac{31.6 - 31.2}{2} = 0.2\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Divide both by 20 to get one period", math=r"T = \frac{31.4}{20} = 1.570\ \mathrm{s}, \qquad \Delta T = \frac{0.2}{20} = 0.010\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("State the result", math=r"T = (1.57 \pm 0.01)\ \mathrm{s}"),
        ]),
        TIP("Timing many oscillations makes the random error of reaction time at the start and end a much smaller fraction of the result. This is why we time 20 swings, not one."),
        EX("Worked example 2: zero errors", intro=r"(a) A micrometer screw gauge reads $+0.03\ \mathrm{mm}$ when the jaws are closed. A wire reads $4.37\ \mathrm{mm}$. Find its true diameter. (b) A different micrometer reads $-0.02\ \mathrm{mm}$ when closed and gives $4.35\ \mathrm{mm}$ for the wire. Find the true diameter.", steps=[
            S("(a) Subtract the zero error", math=r"d = 4.37 - (+0.03) = 4.34\ \mathrm{mm}"),
            S("(b) The zero error is negative. Subtracting a negative number adds", math=r"d = 4.35 - (-0.02) = 4.37\ \mathrm{mm}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: classify the error", intro=r"Say whether each fault mainly produces a random or a systematic error, and how the student could reduce it. (a) An ammeter reads $0.02\ \mathrm{A}$ with no current. (b) A student reads the level in a measuring cylinder from varying eye positions. (c) A ruler is cut off so that its first mark is really at 1 mm. (d) The mains voltage drifts while a heater is timed.", steps=[
            S("(a) Systematic", text="The same shift every time (a zero error). Subtract 0.02 A from each reading, or adjust the zero screw."),
            S("(b) Random", text="Parallax errors vary both ways. Read at eye level to the bottom of the meniscus and repeat the reading."),
            S("(c) Systematic", text="Every length is 1 mm too small (or too large). Do not use the end of the rule: measure from a full mark, or use a different rule."),
            S("(d) Random", text="The drift is unpredictable. Use a stabilised supply or a datalogger, and repeat the run."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Thinking that averaging removes all errors", r"A mean reduces random error only. If the instrument has a zero error, every reading has it, and so does the mean."),
        MISTAKE("Swapping accuracy and precision", r"A set of readings can be very close together (precise) and still far from the true value (inaccurate). Precision is about scatter; accuracy is about closeness to the true value."),
        MISTAKE("Writing 'human error'", r"Examiners want the **specific** cause: for example 'parallax when reading the scale' or 'delay in starting the stopwatch'. Also, a blunder (misreading a scale, a calculator slip) is a mistake, not an error in this sense: repeat the measurement."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong sign when correcting a zero error", r"Always subtract the zero error, with its sign. A micrometer that reads $-0.02\ \mathrm{mm}$ when closed under-reads, so $0.02\ \mathrm{mm}$ must be **added**."),
        QUIZ(r"A student measures the same current five times with an ammeter and gets $0.52, 0.52, 0.53, 0.52, 0.52\ \mathrm{A}$. The true value is $0.60\ \mathrm{A}$. Which description fits?",
             ["Accurate and precise", "Precise but not accurate", "Accurate but not precise", "Neither accurate nor precise"], 1,
             r"The readings are very close together (precise) but they all lie about $0.08\ \mathrm{A}$ below the true value (not accurate). This suggests a systematic error such as a zero error.",
             hint="Compare the scatter with the distance from the true value.",
             feedback=["They are not close to 0.60 A, so they are not accurate.", "", "The readings are tightly grouped, so they are precise.", "The tight grouping means they are precise."]),
        QUIZ(r"Which change would **reduce the random error** in finding the time for a trolley to roll down a slope?",
             ["Subtract the zero error of the stopwatch", "Repeat the timing several times and take the mean", "Replace the stopwatch with an identical, correctly set one", "Add 0.2 s to every reading"], 1,
             "Random errors scatter the readings on both sides of the true value, so averaging many readings reduces them.",
             hint="Which action uses several readings?",
             feedback=["That would correct a systematic error, not a random one.", "", "A change of instrument may remove a systematic error but does nothing about random scatter.", "A fixed correction can only deal with a systematic error."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A student measures the diameter of a wire at five places with a micrometer: $0.52, 0.54, 0.53, 0.52, 0.54\ \mathrm{mm}$. The micrometer reads $-0.02\ \mathrm{mm}$ when the jaws are closed. Find the corrected mean diameter and the uncertainty from the spread.",
           r"Mean reading $= 2.65/5 = 0.53\ \mathrm{mm}$. Correct for the zero error: $0.53 - (-0.02) = 0.55\ \mathrm{mm}$. Uncertainty $= (0.54 - 0.52)/2 = 0.01\ \mathrm{mm}$. Diameter $= (0.55 \pm 0.01)\ \mathrm{mm}$."),
        PQ(r"Two students each measure $g$ four times (true value $9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$). Student A gets $9.2, 9.3, 9.1, 9.2$. Student B gets $9.5, 10.1, 9.9, 10.3$. Calculate each mean and range, and say which student is more accurate and which is more precise.",
           r"A: mean $9.2$, range $0.2$. B: mean $9.95$, range $0.8$. A is more precise (smaller spread) but less accurate (mean $0.6$ below the true value, which suggests a systematic error). B is less precise (larger spread) but more accurate (mean only $0.14$ above the true value)."),
        PQ(r"A student plots the potential difference $V$ across a resistor against the current $I$. The points lie close to a straight line, but the line cuts the $V$ axis at $+0.15\ \mathrm{V}$ instead of passing through the origin. Suggest a cause, say whether the error is random or systematic, and describe how to check and correct it.",
           r"The points lie close to a line (small random error) but all are shifted the same way, so the error is **systematic**. A likely cause is a zero error on the voltmeter (it reads $0.15\ \mathrm{V}$ at zero current), or a contact resistance. Check by disconnecting the circuit and reading the voltmeter: a non-zero reading is the zero error. Subtract it from every reading. The gradient is not affected by a constant shift, so the resistance obtained from the gradient is still reliable."),
        PQ(r"State two ways of reducing the effect of reaction time when timing a falling object with a hand-held stopwatch.",
           r"Increase the time measured (use a taller drop or time many swings or bounces so that the reaction time is a smaller fraction of the total). Repeat and average. Use light gates or a datalogger, which remove the human reaction time altogether. Any two are acceptable."),
        TIP("In the exam: errors are examined in Paper 2 (planning and analysis questions) and in the practical Paper 3, where you may be asked to identify the sources of error, correct a zero error, or say how to improve an experiment. Link each improvement to the specific error it reduces."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A random error varies unpredictably and shows as scatter; repeating and averaging reduces it.",
            "A systematic error shifts all readings the same way; averaging does not reduce it, but checking zeros, calibration and method does.",
            "Accuracy is closeness to the true value; precision is closeness of repeated readings to each other.",
            r"Correct a zero error by subtracting it with its sign: true reading $=$ measured reading $-$ zero error.",
            r"One reading: uncertainty is about half the smallest division (analogue) or one smallest digit (digital); repeated readings: half the range."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M1 / Uncertainty propagation 1: the rules
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Uncertainty propagation",
    title="Combining uncertainties: sums, products and powers",
    slug="phl1-combining-uncertainties",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Write results as value plus or minus uncertainty, and combine absolute and percentage uncertainties for sums, differences, products, quotients and powers.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish absolute, fractional and percentage uncertainty",
        "Add absolute uncertainties for sums and differences",
        "Add percentage uncertainties for products and quotients, and multiply by the power for powers",
        "Work a full multi-step uncertainty calculation and quote the result with a sensible number of figures",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Absolute uncertainty", "Fractional uncertainty", "Percentage uncertainty", "Combining uncertainties", "Significant figures in a result"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction: how many tiles?"),
        T(r"A builder in Yaoundé measures a room as $4.20\ \mathrm{m}$ by $3.10\ \mathrm{m}$ with a tape that can be read to about $\pm0.01\ \mathrm{m}$. The floor area is $4.20 \times 3.10 = 13.02\ \mathrm{m^2}$. But each length could be a little bigger or smaller than measured. How uncertain is the area? Should he order tiles for $13.02\ \mathrm{m^2}$ exactly? This lesson gives the rules for passing the uncertainty in each measurement into the final result. This is called **propagation of uncertainties**."),
        D(r"An **absolute uncertainty** $\Delta x$ is the uncertainty in the same unit as the quantity. The **fractional uncertainty** is $\dfrac{\Delta x}{x}$ and the **percentage uncertainty** is $\dfrac{\Delta x}{x}\times 100\%$. A result is written $x \pm \Delta x$.", title="Ways to state an uncertainty",
          formula=r"x \pm \Delta x, \qquad \text{percentage uncertainty} = \frac{\Delta x}{x}\times 100\%",
          note="Example: a length of (12.0 ± 0.1) cm has absolute uncertainty 0.1 cm and percentage uncertainty 0.83 %."),
        H("Rule 1: sums and differences"),
        D(r"When quantities are **added or subtracted**, add their **absolute** uncertainties.", title="Sums and differences",
          formula=r"y = a \pm b \;\Rightarrow\; \Delta y = \Delta a + \Delta b"),
        T(r"Why add, even for a difference? Take the worst case. For $y = a - b$, the largest possible $y$ is $(a + \Delta a) - (b - \Delta b) = (a - b) + (\Delta a + \Delta b)$. Subtracting the quantities does not cancel the uncertainties; it makes the *percentage* uncertainty larger when $a$ and $b$ are close in size."),
        EX("Worked example 1: temperature rise", intro=r"The temperature of water in a solar heater rises from $\theta_1 = (23.5 \pm 0.5)\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to $\theta_2 = (67.0 \pm 0.5)\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}$. Find the rise in temperature with its absolute and percentage uncertainty.", steps=[
            S("Subtract the values", math=r"\Delta\theta = 67.0 - 23.5 = 43.5\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}"),
            S("Add the absolute uncertainties", math=r"0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}"),
            S("State the result", math=r"\text{rise} = (43.5 \pm 1.0)\ {}^\circ\mathrm{C}"),
            S("Percentage uncertainty", math=r"\frac{1.0}{43.5}\times100\% = 2.3\%"),
        ]),
        H("Rule 2: products and quotients"),
        D(r"When quantities are **multiplied or divided**, add their **percentage (or fractional)** uncertainties.", title="Products and quotients",
          formula=r"y = \frac{ab}{c} \;\Rightarrow\; \frac{\Delta y}{y} = \frac{\Delta a}{a} + \frac{\Delta b}{b} + \frac{\Delta c}{c}",
          note="Even a quotient adds the percentage uncertainties: the uncertainty in the denominator also makes the answer uncertain."),
        T(r"Quick derivation: for $y = ab$, the largest value is $(a + \Delta a)(b + \Delta b) = ab + a\,\Delta b + b\,\Delta a + \Delta a\,\Delta b$. The last term is tiny and is ignored. So $\Delta y \approx a\,\Delta b + b\,\Delta a$. Divide by $y = ab$: $\dfrac{\Delta y}{y} = \dfrac{\Delta a}{a} + \dfrac{\Delta b}{b}$."),
        EX("Worked example 2: area of a rectangle", intro=r"A plate has length $L = (12.0 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{cm}$ and width $W = (8.0 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{cm}$. Find the area and its uncertainty.", steps=[
            S("Area", math=r"A = 12.0 \times 8.0 = 96.0\ \mathrm{cm^2}"),
            S("Percentage uncertainties", math=r"\frac{0.1}{12.0} = 0.83\%, \qquad \frac{0.1}{8.0} = 1.25\%"),
            S("Add them", math=r"0.83\% + 1.25\% = 2.08\%"),
            S("Convert back to an absolute uncertainty", math=r"\Delta A = 0.0208 \times 96.0 = 2.0\ \mathrm{cm^2}"),
            S("State the result", math=r"A = (96 \pm 2)\ \mathrm{cm^2}"),
        ], check=r"\text{largest area: } 12.1 \times 8.1 = 98.01;\ \text{smallest: } 11.9 \times 7.9 = 94.01"),
        H("Rule 3: powers"),
        D(r"For a quantity raised to a power $n$, **multiply** the percentage uncertainty by the size of the power (a root is a power of $\tfrac12$, so it halves the percentage uncertainty).", title="Powers",
          formula=r"y = a^{n} \;\Rightarrow\; \frac{\Delta y}{y} = |n|\,\frac{\Delta a}{a}",
          note="A square doubles the percentage uncertainty; a square root halves it. Pure numbers such as 2, ½ and π carry no uncertainty."),
        T(r"This is the rule for $a\times a$ again: the same quantity appears twice, and its uncertainty is **not** independent, so you add the two equal percentages, which is the same as doubling."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a full calculation", intro=r"A metal cylinder has mass $m = (86.4 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{g}$, diameter $d = (2.00 \pm 0.01)\ \mathrm{cm}$ and height $h = (10.0 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{cm}$. Find its density with the uncertainty.", steps=[
            S("Density formula", math=r"\rho = \frac{m}{V} = \frac{m}{\pi (d/2)^2 h} = \frac{4m}{\pi d^2 h}"),
            S("Value", math=r"\rho = \frac{86.4}{\pi \times 1.00^2 \times 10.0} = 2.750\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}"),
            S("Percentage uncertainties of each quantity", math=r"m: \frac{0.1}{86.4} = 0.12\%,\quad d: \frac{0.01}{2.00} = 0.50\%,\quad h: \frac{0.1}{10.0} = 1.0\%"),
            S("Combine: $d$ is squared, so its percentage is doubled", math=r"0.12\% + 2(0.50\%) + 1.0\% = 2.12\%"),
            S("Absolute uncertainty", math=r"\Delta\rho = 0.0212 \times 2.750 = 0.058\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}"),
            S("State the result, one or two significant figures in the uncertainty", math=r"\rho = (2.75 \pm 0.06)\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}} = (2750 \pm 60)\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}"),
        ]),
        TIP(r"In this example $d$ contributes $1.0\%$ (after doubling) and $h$ contributes $1.0\%$, while $m$ contributes only $0.12\%$. Improving the measurement of the mass would change almost nothing. You should improve the largest contribution."),
        H("Quoting the final result"),
        KEY("How many figures?", r"Do not claim more precision than you have.", cards=[
            ["Uncertainty", r"Round the uncertainty to **one** significant figure (two if the first digit is 1). Some textbooks use two: follow your teacher."],
            ["Value", r"Round the value to the **same decimal place** as the uncertainty: $(2.75 \pm 0.06)$, not $(2.7502 \pm 0.06)$."],
            ["Intermediate steps", r"Keep extra figures during the calculation and round only at the end."],
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Subtracting percentages in a quotient", r"For $y = a/b$ the percentage uncertainties **add**. An uncertainty can never reduce another one."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing absolute and percentage uncertainties", r"For sums use absolute; for products use percentage. Do not add a percentage to an absolute value."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to multiply by the power", r"If $V \propto r^3$ then the percentage uncertainty in $V$ is **three** times that in $r$, not equal to it."),
        MISTAKE("Quoting too many figures", r"An answer such as $(9.80217 \pm 0.134)$ is meaningless. Write $(9.80 \pm 0.13)$."),
        QUIZ(r"The percentage uncertainty in the radius $r$ of a circle is 3%. What is the percentage uncertainty in its area $A = \pi r^2$?",
             ["3 %", "6 %", "9 %", r"$3\pi\ \%$"], 1,
             r"The radius is squared, so the percentage uncertainty is doubled: $2 \times 3\% = 6\%$. The constant $\pi$ adds no uncertainty.",
             hint="Which rule applies when a quantity is squared?",
             feedback=["You forgot that the radius is squared.", "", "You cubed instead of squared (that is the rule for a volume).", r"The number $\pi$ is exact and does not multiply the percentage."]),
        QUIZ(r"A mass is $(50.0 \pm 0.5)\ \mathrm{g}$ and a second mass is $(20.0 \pm 0.4)\ \mathrm{g}$. What is their total, with its uncertainty?",
             [r"$(70.0 \pm 0.9)\ \mathrm{g}$", r"$(70.0 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{g}$", r"$(70.0 \pm 0.5)\ \mathrm{g}$", r"$(70.0 \pm 3.0)\ \mathrm{g}$"], 0,
             "For a sum, add the absolute uncertainties: $0.5 + 0.4 = 0.9\\ \\mathrm{g}$.",
             hint="Sums use absolute uncertainties.",
             feedback=["", "You subtracted the uncertainties. They always add.", "You used only the larger of the two.", "You added the percentage uncertainties (1% + 2% = 3%) and treated the result as grams."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The speed of a trolley is found from $v = s/t$ with $s = (2.50 \pm 0.01)\ \mathrm{m}$ and $t = (1.84 \pm 0.02)\ \mathrm{s}$. Find $v$ and its absolute uncertainty.",
           r"$v = 2.50/1.84 = 1.359\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Percentage uncertainties: $0.01/2.50 = 0.40\%$ and $0.02/1.84 = 1.09\%$. Sum $= 1.49\%$. $\Delta v = 0.0149 \times 1.359 = 0.020\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. $v = (1.36 \pm 0.02)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A resistor has $V = (6.0 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{V}$ across it and $I = (0.250 \pm 0.005)\ \mathrm{A}$ through it. Calculate $R$ and its uncertainty.",
           r"$R = 6.0/0.250 = 24.0\ \Omega$. Percentage uncertainties: $0.1/6.0 = 1.67\%$ and $0.005/0.250 = 2.0\%$. Total $3.67\%$. $\Delta R = 0.0367 \times 24.0 = 0.88\ \Omega$. $R = (24.0 \pm 0.9)\ \Omega$."),
        PQ(r"The kinetic energy $E = \tfrac12mv^2$ of a ball with $m = (0.250 \pm 0.005)\ \mathrm{kg}$ and $v = (3.20 \pm 0.05)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ is calculated. Find $E$ and its percentage and absolute uncertainty.",
           r"$E = \tfrac12 \times 0.250 \times 3.20^2 = 1.28\ \mathrm{J}$. Percentages: $m$: $2.0\%$; $v$: $0.05/3.20 = 1.56\%$, doubled to $3.13\%$. Total $5.1\%$. $\Delta E = 0.051 \times 1.28 = 0.066\ \mathrm{J}$. $E = (1.28 \pm 0.07)\ \mathrm{J}$ (about 5 %)."),
        PQ(r"The length of a spring is $(85.3 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{cm}$ when loaded and $(20.1 \pm 0.1)\ \mathrm{cm}$ when unloaded. Find the extension and its percentage uncertainty. Then comment on what would happen if the two lengths were $100.4$ cm and $99.8$ cm (each $\pm 0.1$ cm).",
           r"Extension $= 85.3 - 20.1 = 65.2\ \mathrm{cm}$; $\Delta = 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.2\ \mathrm{cm}$, so $(65.2 \pm 0.2)\ \mathrm{cm}$, i.e. $0.31\%$. For $100.4 - 99.8 = 0.6\ \mathrm{cm}$ the uncertainty is still $0.2\ \mathrm{cm}$, which is $33\%$. Subtracting two nearly equal measurements makes the percentage uncertainty large, so avoid it in an experiment where possible."),
        TIP("In the exam: uncertainty calculations appear in Paper 2 (data analysis and structured questions) and are central in the practical Paper 3. Show each percentage uncertainty, add them, and give the final absolute uncertainty to one significant figure."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Absolute uncertainty $\Delta x$ has the unit of $x$; percentage uncertainty is $\Delta x/x \times 100\%$.",
            r"Sum or difference: add the **absolute** uncertainties.",
            r"Product or quotient: add the **percentage** uncertainties.",
            r"Power $n$: multiply the percentage uncertainty by $|n|$.",
            "Quote the uncertainty to one significant figure and the value to the same decimal place."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M1 / Uncertainty propagation 2: experiments and graphs
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Uncertainty propagation",
    title="Uncertainties in experiments: error bars and best-fit lines",
    slug="phl1-uncertainties-in-experiments-and-graphs",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Carry uncertainties through a real experiment: error bars, the gradient and its uncertainty from steepest and shallowest lines, the intercept as a check for systematic error, and comparing a result with an accepted value.",
    objectives=[
        "Draw error bars and a best-fit line, and draw the steepest and shallowest acceptable lines",
        "Find a gradient and its uncertainty, and use them to find a physical constant with its uncertainty",
        "Use the intercept to look for a systematic error",
        "Decide whether a result agrees with an accepted value, and which measurement to improve",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Error bar", "Line of best fit", "Steepest and shallowest lines", "Gradient uncertainty", "Agreement with an accepted value"],
    blocks=[
        H("From readings to a conclusion"),
        T(r"A school laboratory in Buea uses a simple pendulum to find the acceleration of free fall $g$. For a pendulum of length $L$ the period is $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$, so $T^2 = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{g}\,L$. A graph of $T^2$ against $L$ is a straight line through the origin with gradient $\dfrac{4\pi^2}{g}$. The data, and how sure we are of them, must lead to a value of $g$ **with an uncertainty**."),
        H("The data"),
        T(r"Five pendulum lengths were used. For each, the time for 20 oscillations was measured several times and the period $T$ found, then squared. The spread of the repeated readings gave an uncertainty of about $\pm0.08\ \mathrm{s^2}$ in each value of $T^2$. The uncertainty in $L$ (about $\pm0.002\ \mathrm{m}$) is too small to show on the graph. These are teaching data, not a real experiment."),
        CODE("""L / m     T^2 / s^2     uncertainty in T^2
0.20      0.84          +/- 0.08
0.40      1.58          +/- 0.08
0.60      2.45          +/- 0.08
0.80      3.17          +/- 0.08
1.00      4.07          +/- 0.08""", "text", title="Teaching data: pendulum"),
        D(r"An **error bar** is a line through a plotted point whose length shows the uncertainty in that value (a vertical bar for the uncertainty in $y$, a horizontal bar for $x$). The true value probably lies somewhere along the bar.", title="Error bar"),
        H("Best-fit line and worst acceptable lines"),
        KEY("How to draw them", "Use a transparent ruler and a sharp pencil.", cards=[
            ["Best-fit line", r"A straight line with points balanced on both sides. For a line that should pass through the origin, check it does. It should pass close to the mean point of the data."],
            ["Steepest line", "The line of largest gradient that still passes through all the error bars."],
            ["Shallowest line", "The line of smallest gradient that still passes through all the error bars."],
            ["Gradient uncertainty", r"$\Delta m = \dfrac{m_{\text{steepest}} - m_{\text{shallowest}}}{2}$"],
        ]),
        IMG("al-phl1-error-bar-graph.svg", alt="Graph of T squared against L with five points and error bars; a solid best-fit line and two dashed lines, the steepest and shallowest that pass through all the error bars",
            caption="Figure 1. The best-fit line (solid) and the steepest and shallowest lines (dashed) that still pass through every error bar."),
        T(r"Use a large triangle on the lines to find gradients, taking points far apart on the line (not data points) so the reading error is small."),
        EX("Worked example 1: gradient with uncertainty, and the value of g", intro=r"Using Figure 1: the best-fit line has gradient $4.03\ \mathrm{s^2\,m^{-1}}$ (it passes through the points $(0.20,\ 0.81)$ and $(1.00,\ 4.03)$). The steepest line gives $4.15\ \mathrm{s^2\,m^{-1}}$ and the shallowest $3.88\ \mathrm{s^2\,m^{-1}}$. Find $g$ and its uncertainty.", steps=[
            S("Uncertainty in the gradient", math=r"\Delta m = \frac{4.15 - 3.88}{2} = 0.135 \approx 0.14\ \mathrm{s^2\,m^{-1}}"),
            S("State the gradient", math=r"m = (4.03 \pm 0.14)\ \mathrm{s^2\,m^{-1}}, \quad \frac{\Delta m}{m} = 3.5\%"),
            S(r"Use $m = 4\pi^2/g$, so $g = 4\pi^2/m$", math=r"g = \frac{4\pi^2}{4.03} = 9.80\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("$g$ is proportional to $1/m$, so it has the same percentage uncertainty", math=r"\Delta g = 0.035 \times 9.80 = 0.34\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("State the result", math=r"g = (9.8 \pm 0.3)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: checking for a systematic error", intro=r"The steepest line has equation $T^2 = 4.15L - 0.07$ and the shallowest $T^2 = 3.88L + 0.11$ (in $\mathrm{s^2}$, with $L$ in metres). Theory says the line must pass through the origin. Is there evidence of a systematic error?", steps=[
            S("Read the intercepts", math=r"c_{\text{steepest}} = -0.07\ \mathrm{s^2}, \qquad c_{\text{shallowest}} = +0.11\ \mathrm{s^2}"),
            S("The best-fit intercept is about $0.01\\ \\mathrm{s^2}$. The range of intercepts is about", math=r"0.02 \pm 0.09\ \mathrm{s^2}"),
            S("Compare with zero", text="Zero lies inside the range of acceptable intercepts, so the data show no evidence of a systematic error. A large intercept that excluded zero would suggest one, such as measuring the length to the top of the bob instead of its centre."),
        ]),
        H("Comparing with an accepted value"),
        D(r"A measured result **agrees** with an accepted value if the accepted value lies within the range of the measured value (its value plus or minus its uncertainty). The **percentage difference** is $\dfrac{|\text{measured} - \text{accepted}|}{\text{accepted}} \times 100\%$.", title="Agreement with an accepted value"),
        T(r"For our result, $g = (9.8 \pm 0.3)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ gives the range $9.5$ to $10.1\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The accepted value of $9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ is inside it, so the result agrees with it within the experimental uncertainty. If a range does not contain the accepted value, there is probably a systematic error that you have not found (or the uncertainty was underestimated)."),
        EX("Worked example 3: which measurement to improve?", intro=r"A single pendulum has $L = (0.800 \pm 0.002)\ \mathrm{m}$ and 20 oscillations took $(35.90 \pm 0.20)\ \mathrm{s}$. Find $g$ and its uncertainty, say which measurement limits the precision, and suggest an improvement.", steps=[
            S("Period and its uncertainty", math=r"T = \frac{35.90}{20} = 1.795\ \mathrm{s}, \qquad \Delta T = \frac{0.20}{20} = 0.010\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Value of $g$", math=r"g = \frac{4\pi^2 L}{T^2} = \frac{4\pi^2 \times 0.800}{1.795^2} = 9.80\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Percentage uncertainties; $T$ is squared", math=r"L:\ \frac{0.002}{0.800} = 0.25\%, \qquad T:\ 2 \times \frac{0.010}{1.795} = 1.11\%"),
            S("Add", math=r"0.25\% + 1.11\% = 1.36\%, \quad \Delta g = 0.0136\times 9.80 = 0.13\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("State and comment", text=r"$g = (9.80 \pm 0.13)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The timing contributes most (1.11 % against 0.25 %). Timing 50 oscillations, about 89.75 s with the same $\pm0.2\ \mathrm{s}$, would cut its contribution to $2\times0.2/89.75 = 0.45\%$."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using data points to find the gradient", r"Use two points far apart **on the line** (a large triangle), not two plotted points. Points that are not on the line carry their own scatter."),
        MISTAKE("Steepest and shallowest lines that miss the error bars", r"These lines must stay within **all** the error bars. A line through the first and last bar corners only may pass outside the middle bars."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to convert a gradient uncertainty to the final quantity", r"The gradient is $4\pi^2/g$, not $g$. Find $g$ from the gradient, and use the **percentage** uncertainty in the gradient for $g$ (the power is $-1$, so the percentage is unchanged)."),
        MISTAKE("Saying 'it agrees' with no reasoning", r"State the range of the result and say whether the accepted value lies within it."),
        QUIZ(r"The gradient of a $T^2$ against $L$ graph is $(4.0 \pm 0.2)\ \mathrm{s^2\,m^{-1}}$. What is the percentage uncertainty in $g = 4\pi^2/\text{gradient}$?",
             ["0.2 %", "5 %", "10 %", r"$4\pi^2 \times 5\ \%$"], 1,
             r"$\Delta m / m = 0.2/4.0 = 5\%$. Since $g \propto 1/m$ the percentage uncertainty stays 5%.",
             hint="g is proportional to 1 over the gradient. What does a power of minus 1 do to the percentage?",
             feedback=["That is the absolute uncertainty written as a percentage sign.", "", "You doubled it, which is only needed for a square.", r"The constant $4\pi^2$ is exact and does not multiply the percentage."]),
        QUIZ(r"A student finds $g = (9.4 \pm 0.3)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The accepted value is $9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. What is the best conclusion?",
             [r"The result agrees with the accepted value.", r"The result does not agree: 9.81 lies outside $9.1$ to $9.7$, so a systematic error is likely.", r"The result is exact.", r"The result cannot be compared with an accepted value."], 1,
             r"The range is $9.1$ to $9.7$. $9.81$ is outside it, so the result does not agree within the stated uncertainty. Look for a systematic error (or an underestimated uncertainty).",
             hint="Write the range first.",
             feedback=["9.81 is not between 9.1 and 9.7.", "", "No experimental result is exact.", "A comparison is exactly what scientists do with accepted values."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A spring obeys $F = kx$. A graph of $F$ (vertical) against extension $x$ has a best-fit gradient of $24.0\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$, a steepest line of gradient $25.2\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$ and a shallowest line of $22.8\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$. State $k$ with its uncertainty.",
           r"$\Delta k = (25.2 - 22.8)/2 = 1.2\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$. $k = (24.0 \pm 1.2)\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$, a 5 % uncertainty."),
        PQ(r"The same pendulum experiment gives a graph of $T^2$ against $L$ whose line cuts the $T^2$ axis at $0.35\ \mathrm{s^2}$. The error bars are $\pm0.08\ \mathrm{s^2}$ and the best-fit and worst lines all have intercepts between $0.28$ and $0.42\ \mathrm{s^2}$. Is there a systematic error? Suggest a cause.",
           r"Zero is well outside the range of acceptable intercepts ($0.28$ to $0.42$), so there is a systematic error. A likely cause is that $L$ was measured to the top of the bob (or to the point of suspension with a clamp offset) rather than to the centre of the bob, which makes every $L$ too small. The gradient can still be used to find $g$."),
        PQ(r"A student finds $\rho = (2.75 \pm 0.06)\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$ for a metal. Two data-book values for different metals are $2.70\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$ and $2.83\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$. For each, say whether the result agrees, and find the percentage difference.",
           r"Range: $2.69$ to $2.81\ \mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$. For $2.70$: inside the range, agrees; percentage difference $= 0.05/2.70 = 1.9\%$. For $2.83$: outside the range ($2.81 < 2.83$), so it does not agree; percentage difference $= 0.08/2.83 = 2.8\%$. The data suggest the first metal."),
        PQ(r"Show that doubling the number of oscillations timed from 20 to 40 halves the percentage uncertainty in $T$, assuming the same absolute uncertainty $\Delta t$ in the total time.",
           r"$T = t/n$ and $\Delta T = \Delta t/n$, so $\Delta T/T = \Delta t/t$. The total time $t$ is proportional to $n$, so doubling $n$ doubles $t$ while $\Delta t$ stays the same. Hence $\Delta t/t$, and therefore $\Delta T/T$, is halved."),
        TIP("In the exam: this is the heart of the practical Paper 3 and of analysis questions in Paper 2: tables with uncertainties, error bars, gradients and their uncertainties, and a conclusion about agreement. Always give a range, then compare."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Error bars show the uncertainty in each plotted value.",
            r"Gradient uncertainty $= \tfrac12(m_{\text{steepest}} - m_{\text{shallowest}})$, using lines that pass through all the error bars.",
            "A physical constant found from the gradient carries the gradient's percentage uncertainty (adjusted by any power).",
            "An intercept that is incompatible with theory indicates a systematic error.",
            "A result agrees with an accepted value if the value lies within the result's range; improve the measurement with the largest percentage contribution."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Projectile motion 1: horizontal launch
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Projectile motion",
    title="Projectile motion 1: independent horizontal and vertical motion",
    slug="phl1-projectile-horizontal-launch",
    minutes=22,
    summary="A projectile has constant horizontal velocity and constant downward acceleration. Treat the two directions separately to find time of flight, range, and the speed and direction at impact.",
    objectives=[
        "State the equations of motion for constant acceleration and apply them in one dimension",
        "Explain that horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent",
        "Solve problems on objects launched horizontally: time, range, impact velocity",
        "Derive the parabolic path equation of a horizontally launched projectile",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Projectile", "Independence of components", "Constant acceleration equations", "Time of flight", "Range", "Parabolic path"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"After a flood, a helicopter flying low over a river drops sacks of relief food to a village on the far bank. The sacks do not fall straight down: they keep moving forward with the helicopter's speed while falling. Where should the pilot release them so they land on the target? The same physics describes a ball kicked off a platform, a jet of water from a hole in a tank, or a stone thrown from a bridge. All are **projectiles**."),
        D(r"A **projectile** is an object that moves under gravity alone after being launched. If air resistance is ignored, its acceleration is constant: $g = 10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ vertically downwards (use $g = 10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ in this course unless stated).", title="Projectile",
          note="Air resistance is neglected throughout this lesson. For a dense, compact object moving slowly it is a good approximation."),
        H("Equations for constant acceleration"),
        T(r"For motion in a straight line with constant acceleration $a$, starting with velocity $u$, after time $t$ the velocity is $v$ and the displacement is $s$:"),
        F(r"v = u + at \qquad s = ut + \tfrac12 at^2 \qquad v^2 = u^2 + 2as \qquad s = \tfrac12 (u + v)\,t"),
        T(r"These apply **separately along each direction**. Choose a positive direction and keep to it: quantities pointing the other way are negative."),
        H("The key idea: independence of the two motions"),
        D(r"The horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent. Horizontally there is no force, so the velocity is **constant**. Vertically the acceleration is $g$ downwards.", title="Independence of components",
          formula=r"x = u_x t, \qquad y = \tfrac12 g t^2 \ \ (\text{for a launch with } u_y = 0)"),
        T(r"In the figure below, a ball is thrown horizontally (blue) at the same moment as a second ball is dropped (orange). At every instant they are at the same height, because the horizontal velocity of the first has no effect on how fast it falls. The horizontal spacing of the blue dots is constant, and the vertical spacing increases as the speed downwards increases."),
        IMG("al-phl1-projectile-horizontal.svg", alt="A ball thrown horizontally at 12 metres per second from a 45 metre cliff and a ball dropped at the same moment; the two are always at the same height at equal times",
            caption="Figure 1. A ball thrown horizontally at 12 m/s from a 45 m cliff, and a dropped ball. Both reach the ground after 3.0 s."),
        EX("Worked example 1: kicked off a platform", intro=r"A ball is kicked horizontally at $12\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ from the edge of a platform $45\ \mathrm{m}$ high. Find (a) the time to reach the ground, (b) the horizontal distance travelled, (c) the speed and direction of the ball as it lands.", steps=[
            S("(a) Vertical motion: $u_y = 0$, $s_y = 45$ m downwards, $a = g$", math=r"45 = \tfrac12 (10) t^2 \;\Rightarrow\; t = \sqrt{9.0} = 3.0\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("(b) Horizontal motion at constant velocity", math=r"x = u t = 12 \times 3.0 = 36\ \mathrm{m}"),
            S("(c) Vertical component of velocity at landing", math=r"v_y = g t = 10 \times 3.0 = 30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} \text{ downwards}"),
            S("Combine the components", math=r"v = \sqrt{12^2 + 30^2} = 32.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Direction below the horizontal", math=r"\tan\theta = \frac{30}{12} \Rightarrow \theta = 68.2{}^\circ"),
        ], check=r"v^2 = u^2 + 2gh = 12^2 + 2(10)(45) = 1044 \Rightarrow v = 32.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: relief supplies", intro=r"A helicopter flies horizontally at $50\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at a height of $500\ \mathrm{m}$ above flat ground. It releases a sack of food. (a) How far **before** the target must the sack be released? (b) What is the speed of the sack when it lands?", steps=[
            S("Time to fall 500 m (initial vertical velocity is zero)", math=r"500 = \tfrac12 (10) t^2 \;\Rightarrow\; t = 10\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("(a) Horizontal distance covered in that time (the sack keeps the helicopter's speed)", math=r"x = 50 \times 10 = 500\ \mathrm{m}"),
            S("(b) Vertical speed at landing", math=r"v_y = 10 \times 10 = 100\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Total speed", math=r"v = \sqrt{50^2 + 100^2} = 112\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        H("The path is a parabola"),
        T(r"Eliminate $t$ between $x = ut$ and $y = \tfrac12 gt^2$. From the first, $t = x/u$. Put this into the second:"),
        F(r"y = \frac{g}{2u^2}\,x^2"),
        T(r"Here $y$ is the distance fallen below the launch height. This is the equation of a parabola, so the path of a projectile launched horizontally is a parabola with its vertex at the launch point. A faster launch speed $u$ makes the parabola flatter."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a jet of water", intro=r"Water leaves a small hole in the side of a tank horizontally. The hole is $1.25\ \mathrm{m}$ above level ground and the jet hits the ground $2.0\ \mathrm{m}$ from the foot of the tank. Find the speed of the water as it leaves the hole.", steps=[
            S("Time to fall 1.25 m", math=r"1.25 = \tfrac12 (10) t^2 \;\Rightarrow\; t = 0.50\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Horizontal speed (constant)", math=r"u = \frac{x}{t} = \frac{2.0}{0.50} = 4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"y = \frac{g x^2}{2u^2} = \frac{10 \times 2.0^2}{2 \times 4.0^2} = 1.25\ \mathrm{m}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the horizontal speed to find the time of fall", r"The time of fall depends only on the vertical motion (height and $g$). Find $t$ from $h = \tfrac12gt^2$, then use it for the horizontal motion."),
        MISTAKE("Giving only $v_y$ as the impact speed", r"The speed at impact is the **resultant** of the horizontal and vertical components. Use Pythagoras and quote an angle."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a faster horizontal throw takes longer to land", r"It does not. A bullet fired horizontally and a bullet dropped from the same height reach the ground together (in the absence of air resistance and the Earth's curvature)."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the sign of $g$", r"Choose downwards as positive for a projectile launched horizontally and stay with it. Do not use $-10$ for $v_y = gt$ and also measure $s$ downwards."),
        QUIZ(r"A stone is thrown horizontally from a bridge at $6\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. A second stone is dropped from the same height at the same instant. Which statement is correct (ignore air resistance)?",
             ["The thrown stone hits the water first", "The dropped stone hits the water first", "They hit the water at the same time", "It depends on the mass of the stones"], 2,
             "The vertical motion is independent of the horizontal motion. Both start with zero vertical velocity and fall with acceleration $g$, so they reach the water at the same time.",
             hint="Compare only their vertical motions.",
             feedback=["The horizontal speed does not shorten the time of fall.", "The horizontal speed does not lengthen the time of fall.", "", "Without air resistance, mass does not affect the time of fall."]),
        QUIZ(r"The speed of a horizontally launched projectile is increased from $10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ to $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ from the same height. What happens to its range?",
             ["It is unchanged", "It is doubled", "It is quadrupled", "It is halved"], 1,
             r"The time of fall is unchanged (it depends only on the height). The range is $x = ut$, so doubling $u$ doubles $x$.",
             hint="Which quantity stays the same: time of fall or range?",
             feedback=["The range depends on the horizontal speed.", "", "That would be the case if the range were proportional to $u^2$, as for the speed after falling.", "A faster launch gives a longer range, not a shorter one."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A ball is thrown horizontally at $8.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ from a window $20\ \mathrm{m}$ above the ground. Find the time of flight, the horizontal range and the speed on impact.",
           r"$20 = \tfrac12(10)t^2 \Rightarrow t = 2.0\ \mathrm{s}$. Range $= 8.0 \times 2.0 = 16\ \mathrm{m}$. $v_y = 10 \times 2.0 = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, so $v = \sqrt{8.0^2 + 20^2} = 21.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $\tan^{-1}(20/8) = 68{}^\circ$ below the horizontal."),
        PQ(r"A rescue plane flying horizontally at $80\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at a height of $320\ \mathrm{m}$ releases a package. How long does it take to land, and how far ahead of the release point does it land?",
           r"$320 = 5t^2 \Rightarrow t = 8.0\ \mathrm{s}$. Horizontal distance $= 80 \times 8.0 = 640\ \mathrm{m}$ ahead of the release point (the plane is directly above the package at all times if it keeps its speed)."),
        PQ(r"A stone thrown horizontally from the top of a cliff lands $60\ \mathrm{m}$ from the foot of the cliff after $4.0\ \mathrm{s}$. Find the launch speed, the height of the cliff and the speed of impact.",
           r"$u = 60/4.0 = 15\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Height $= \tfrac12(10)(4.0)^2 = 80\ \mathrm{m}$. $v_y = 10 \times 4.0 = 40\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ so $v = \sqrt{15^2 + 40^2} = 42.7\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A projectile is launched horizontally at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Using $y = gx^2/(2u^2)$, how far has it fallen when it has moved $30\ \mathrm{m}$ horizontally? Check by calculating the time.",
           r"$y = \dfrac{10 \times 30^2}{2 \times 20^2} = \dfrac{9000}{800} = 11.25\ \mathrm{m}$. Check: $t = 30/20 = 1.5\ \mathrm{s}$ and $y = 5(1.5)^2 = 11.25\ \mathrm{m}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: projectile motion is common in Paper 1 (a quick time-of-fall or range calculation) and Paper 2 (structured questions with a sketch of the path). Always split the velocity into components first and write the time as the link between the two directions."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A projectile moves under gravity only: constant horizontal velocity, constant vertical acceleration $g$ downwards.",
            r"The time of flight depends only on the vertical motion; the horizontal motion uses that same time ($x = ut$).",
            r"For a horizontal launch: $y = \tfrac12 gt^2$, $v_y = gt$, and the path is the parabola $y = gx^2/(2u^2)$.",
            "Impact speed is the resultant of the two components; give a direction as an angle below the horizontal.",
            "A dropped object and a horizontally thrown object from the same height land together."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Projectile motion 2: angled launch
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Projectile motion",
    title="Projectile motion 2: launch at an angle, range and maximum height",
    slug="phl1-projectile-angled-launch",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Resolve the launch velocity into components, then find time of flight, maximum height, range and the velocity at any time; use the range formula, maximum range at 45 degrees and launches from a height.",
    objectives=[
        "Resolve an initial velocity into horizontal and vertical components",
        "Derive and use expressions for time of flight, maximum height and range on level ground",
        "Show that the range is greatest at 45 degrees and equal for complementary angles",
        "Solve problems with launch from a height, or with a wall or net to clear",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Resolving a velocity", "Time of flight", "Maximum height", "Range formula", "Complementary angles", "Trajectory equation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A goalkeeper in Garoua takes a goal kick: the ball leaves the ground at an angle, rises, and comes down far up the field. The ball spends time in the air because of its upward velocity; it travels forward because of its horizontal velocity. To predict where it lands, we resolve the launch velocity into components and treat each direction separately, as in the previous lesson."),
        H("Components of the launch velocity"),
        D(r"A projectile is launched with speed $u$ at an angle $\theta$ above the horizontal. Its initial velocity has components", title="Resolving the launch velocity",
          formula=r"u_x = u\cos\theta, \qquad u_y = u\sin\theta"),
        T(r"After time $t$ (upwards positive, $a_y = -g$):"),
        F(r"v_x = u\cos\theta, \qquad v_y = u\sin\theta - gt"),
        F(r"x = (u\cos\theta)\,t, \qquad y = (u\sin\theta)\,t - \tfrac12 g t^2"),
        IMG("al-phl1-projectile-angled.svg", alt="Path of a projectile launched at 30 metres per second at 53 degrees, with components 18 and 24 metres per second, maximum height 28.8 metres and range 86.4 metres",
            caption="Figure 1. A projectile launched at 30 m/s at 53 degrees. At the top, the velocity is horizontal (18 m/s) and the vertical component is zero."),
        H("Time of flight, maximum height and range"),
        T(r"For a launch from level ground, three results follow directly. At the **top** of the path $v_y = 0$, so $u\sin\theta - gt = 0$ and the time to reach the top is $t = \dfrac{u\sin\theta}{g}$."),
        T(r"The path is symmetrical, so the **total time of flight** is twice this. (Equivalently, put $y = 0$ in $y = (u\sin\theta)t - \tfrac12gt^2$ and take the non-zero root.) The **maximum height** is found from $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$ with $v_y = 0$. The **range** is the horizontal distance in the time of flight."),
        D(r"For launch speed $u$ at angle $\theta$ from level ground (no air resistance):", title="Standard results (level ground)",
          formula=r"T = \frac{2u\sin\theta}{g}, \qquad H = \frac{u^2\sin^2\theta}{2g}, \qquad R = \frac{u^2\sin 2\theta}{g}",
          note="The range formula uses $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = \\sin 2\\theta$. These results hold only if the landing level equals the launch level."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the 30 m/s launch", intro=r"A ball is launched with components $u_x = 18\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $u_y = 24\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ (a speed of $30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $53^\circ$ to the horizontal) from level ground. Find the time of flight, the maximum height and the range.", steps=[
            S("Time to reach the top ($v_y = 0$)", math=r"t_{\text{up}} = \frac{u_y}{g} = \frac{24}{10} = 2.4\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Total time of flight", math=r"T = 2 \times 2.4 = 4.8\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Maximum height", math=r"H = \frac{u_y^2}{2g} = \frac{24^2}{20} = 28.8\ \mathrm{m}"),
            S("Range", math=r"R = u_x T = 18 \times 4.8 = 86.4\ \mathrm{m}"),
        ], check=r"R = \frac{u^2 \sin 2\theta}{g} = \frac{900 \times 0.96}{10} = 86.4\ \mathrm{m}"),
        H("Maximum range and complementary angles"),
        T(r"For a fixed speed $u$, the range $R = \dfrac{u^2\sin2\theta}{g}$ is greatest when $\sin2\theta = 1$, that is when $\theta = 45^\circ$, and then $R_{\max} = \dfrac{u^2}{g}$. Also, $\sin2\theta = \sin(180^\circ - 2\theta) = \sin 2(90^\circ - \theta)$, so angles that add to $90^\circ$ (**complementary angles**) give the **same range**. A flat, long throw at $30^\circ$ and a high, lobbed throw at $60^\circ$ land at the same place."),
        T(r"For $u = 30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$: $R_{\max} = 900/10 = 90\ \mathrm{m}$ at $45^\circ$, and $R = 900\sin 60^\circ/10 = 77.9\ \mathrm{m}$ at both $30^\circ$ and $60^\circ$. The $60^\circ$ throw takes longer ($5.2\ \mathrm{s}$ against $3.0\ \mathrm{s}$)."),
        TIP("In real life air resistance reduces the range, and the angle for maximum range is a little less than 45 degrees. Sports with a launch from above ground (shot put) or with spin also differ. The 45 degree result is for the ideal case with no air resistance and launch and landing at the same height."),
        EX("Worked example 2: launch from a height", intro=r"A ball is thrown at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal from the top of a $10\ \mathrm{m}$ cliff. Find the time to reach the sea below, the horizontal distance from the cliff, and the speed on impact.", steps=[
            S("Components", math=r"u_x = 20\cos30^\circ = 17.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}, \qquad u_y = 20\sin30^\circ = 10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Vertical displacement is $-10$ m (below the start)", math=r"-10 = 10t - 5t^2 \;\Rightarrow\; t^2 - 2t - 2 = 0"),
            S("Solve the quadratic (take the positive root)", math=r"t = \frac{2 + \sqrt{4 + 8}}{2} = 1 + \sqrt{3} = 2.73\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Horizontal distance", math=r"x = 17.3 \times 2.73 = 47.3\ \mathrm{m}"),
            S("Speed on impact: $v_y = 10 - 10(2.73) = -17.3$", math=r"v = \sqrt{17.3^2 + 17.3^2} = 24.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"v^2 = u^2 + 2gh = 400 + 2(10)(10) = 600 \Rightarrow v = 24.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        T(r"Notice that the speed on impact could be found immediately from energy: the speed at a given height depends only on the starting speed and the height fallen, not on the launch angle."),
        EX("Worked example 3: clearing a wall", intro=r"A footballer kicks a ball at $22\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal from the ground. A wall $3.0\ \mathrm{m}$ high stands $20\ \mathrm{m}$ away on level ground. Does the ball clear it, and by how much?", steps=[
            S("Components", math=r"u_x = 22\cos30^\circ = 19.05\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}, \qquad u_y = 22\sin30^\circ = 11\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Time to cover 20 m horizontally", math=r"t = \frac{20}{19.05} = 1.05\ \mathrm{s}"),
            S("Height of the ball at that time", math=r"y = 11(1.05) - 5(1.05)^2 = 11.55 - 5.51 = 6.04\ \mathrm{m}"),
            S("Compare with the wall", text="$6.04\\ \\mathrm{m} > 3.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$, so the ball clears the wall by about $3.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the range formula for a launch from a height", r"$R = u^2\sin2\theta/g$ needs launch and landing at the same level. For a cliff or a raised platform, solve $y(t)$ for the time and use $x = u_xt$."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing sine and cosine", r"With $\theta$ measured **from the horizontal**, the horizontal component is $u\cos\theta$ and the vertical one $u\sin\theta$. If the angle is given from the vertical, swap them."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the velocity is zero at the top", r"Only the **vertical** component is zero at the top. The horizontal component $u\cos\theta$ remains, so the speed is $u\cos\theta$ there."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that the time of flight is twice the time up", r"The range is $u_x \times T$ with the **whole** flight time $T = 2u_y/g$, not the time to the top."),
        QUIZ(r"A ball is thrown at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $30^\circ$ from level ground. At what other angle would the same speed give the same range?",
             [r"$15^\circ$", r"$45^\circ$", r"$60^\circ$", r"$120^\circ$"], 2,
             r"The range is the same for angles that add to $90^\circ$: $30^\circ + 60^\circ = 90^\circ$. Both give $R = 400\sin 60^\circ/10 = 34.6\ \mathrm{m}$.",
             hint="Which angles make sin 2θ equal?",
             feedback=["That is half of 30 degrees, and gives a different range.", "That gives the maximum range, 40 m, not the same range.", "", "That is not a launch angle above the horizontal for a projectile moving forward."]),
        QUIZ(r"At the highest point of its path a projectile launched at $40\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $60^\circ$ has a speed of",
             [r"$0$", r"$20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$34.6\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$40\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$"], 1,
             r"At the top only the horizontal component remains: $40\cos60^\circ = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$.",
             hint="Which component of velocity is unchanged throughout the flight?",
             feedback=["Only the vertical component is zero there.", "", r"That is $40\sin60^\circ$, the initial vertical component.", "That is the launch speed; the speed at the top is less."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A ball is kicked from level ground at $25\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $40^\circ$ to the horizontal. Find the time of flight, the maximum height and the range.",
           r"$u_x = 25\cos40^\circ = 19.15$, $u_y = 25\sin40^\circ = 16.07\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. $T = 2u_y/g = 3.21\ \mathrm{s}$. $H = u_y^2/(2g) = 12.9\ \mathrm{m}$. $R = u_xT = 19.15 \times 3.21 = 61.6\ \mathrm{m}$. Check: $625\sin80^\circ/10 = 61.6\ \mathrm{m}$."),
        PQ(r"A projectile is fired at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Calculate the range for launch angles of $30^\circ$, $45^\circ$ and $60^\circ$ and comment on the results.",
           r"$R = 400\sin2\theta/10$. $30^\circ$: $40\sin60^\circ = 34.6\ \mathrm{m}$. $45^\circ$: $40\ \mathrm{m}$. $60^\circ$: $40\sin120^\circ = 34.6\ \mathrm{m}$. The ranges at $30^\circ$ and $60^\circ$ are equal (complementary angles) and $45^\circ$ gives the maximum."),
        PQ(r"A javelin is thrown from the ground at $45^\circ$. What launch speed is needed for it to land $40\ \mathrm{m}$ away on level ground (ignore air resistance)?",
           r"At $45^\circ$, $R = u^2/g$, so $u = \sqrt{Rg} = \sqrt{40 \times 10} = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"For the $30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ projectile at $53^\circ$ in Figure 1, find the speed and direction of the velocity $1.0\ \mathrm{s}$ after launch.",
           r"$v_x = 18\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $v_y = 24 - 10(1.0) = 14\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Speed $= \sqrt{18^2 + 14^2} = 22.8\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Direction: $\tan^{-1}(14/18) = 37.9^\circ$ above the horizontal."),
        TIP("In the exam: expect an angled-launch calculation in Paper 2 (time of flight, height, range, or whether the ball clears an obstacle) and conceptual items in Paper 1 (what is constant, speed at the top, complementary angles)."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Resolve: $u_x = u\cos\theta$, $u_y = u\sin\theta$. Horizontal velocity is constant; $v_y = u\sin\theta - gt$.",
            r"Level ground: $T = 2u\sin\theta/g$, $H = u^2\sin^2\theta/(2g)$, $R = u^2\sin2\theta/g$.",
            r"Range is greatest at $45^\circ$ ($R_{\max} = u^2/g$); complementary angles give equal ranges.",
            "At the top the vertical velocity is zero but the horizontal velocity remains.",
            "For launch from a height, solve the vertical displacement equation for the time (a quadratic) and then use the horizontal motion."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Newton's laws 1
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Newton's laws and linear dynamics",
    title="Newton's three laws, free-body diagrams and friction",
    slug="phl1-newtons-laws-free-body-friction",
    minutes=24,
    summary="State Newton's three laws precisely, draw free-body diagrams, resolve forces along and across a slope, and use limiting and kinetic friction in F = ma problems.",
    objectives=[
        "State Newton's three laws and identify third-law pairs",
        "Draw a free-body diagram showing only the forces on one body",
        "Apply F = ma along a chosen direction, including on a slope",
        "Use static and kinetic friction, F = mu N, and the condition for sliding",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Newton's first, second and third laws", "Resultant force", "Weight and mass", "Free-body diagram", "Normal reaction", "Friction", "Coefficient of friction"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A mini-bus climbs the road to Dschang. The engine pushes it forward, the road pushes back with friction, the air resists, gravity pulls it down the slope. Whether the bus speeds up, slows down or keeps a steady speed depends on the **resultant** of all these forces. Newton's three laws tell us how."),
        H("Newton's three laws"),
        D(r"A body remains at rest, or moves with constant velocity in a straight line, unless a resultant (net) force acts on it.", title="Newton's first law",
          note="If the resultant force is zero the velocity is constant (this includes being at rest). A force is needed to change velocity, not to maintain it."),
        D(r"The resultant force on a body is equal to the rate of change of its momentum, and acts in the direction of that change. For a body of constant mass this gives", title="Newton's second law",
          formula=r"\sum \mathbf{F} = \frac{d(m\mathbf{v})}{dt} = m\mathbf{a}",
          note="One newton is the force that gives a mass of 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m s⁻²."),
        D(r"If body A exerts a force on body B, then B exerts an equal and opposite force on A. The two forces are of the same type and act on **different** bodies.", title="Newton's third law"),
        T(r"**Mass** $m$ is the amount of matter in a body, a measure of its inertia (resistance to a change of velocity). It is measured in kilograms and is the same everywhere. **Weight** $W = mg$ is the gravitational force on the body, in newtons. A person of mass $60\ \mathrm{kg}$ weighs about $600\ \mathrm{N}$ on Earth, and less on the Moon, but still has a mass of $60\ \mathrm{kg}$."),
        H("Free-body diagrams"),
        D(r"A **free-body diagram** shows one body on its own, with arrows for **every force acting on that body** (and no forces that the body exerts on other things).", title="Free-body diagram"),
        KEY("Method for dynamics problems", "Follow the same steps every time.", cards=[
            ["1. Isolate", "Choose one body and draw it alone."],
            ["2. Forces", r"Draw weight (down), normal reaction (perpendicular to the surface), friction (along the surface, opposing the motion or tendency to move), and any push, pull or tension."],
            ["3. Axes", "Choose axes along and perpendicular to the likely motion, and resolve forces on them."],
            ["4. Equations", r"Along the motion: resultant $= ma$. Perpendicular to it: resultant $= 0$."],
        ]),
        IMG("al-phl1-fbd-incline.svg", alt="A block on a 30 degree rough slope with weight W downwards, normal reaction N perpendicular to the slope, friction F up the slope, and the weight resolved into mg sin theta along the slope and mg cos theta into the slope",
            caption="Figure 1. A block sliding down a rough slope: weight, normal reaction and friction, with the weight resolved along and perpendicular to the slope."),
        H("Friction"),
        D(r"**Friction** is the force between two surfaces in contact that opposes sliding, or the tendency to slide. When the surfaces are sliding, the **kinetic** friction is $F = \mu_k N$, where $N$ is the normal reaction. When the body is still, the **static** friction adjusts to balance the applied force up to a maximum, the **limiting friction** $F_{\max} = \mu_s N$.", title="Friction",
          formula=r"F_{\text{kinetic}} = \mu_k N, \qquad F_{\text{static}} \le \mu_s N",
          note="The coefficient of friction μ has no unit. Usually μs is a little larger than μk. For some pairs of surfaces textbooks differ, so use the values given in a question."),
        T(r"Note that $N$ is **not always** $mg$. On a slope it is $mg\cos\theta$; if a force pulls upwards at an angle, $N$ is reduced. Always find $N$ from the forces perpendicular to the surface."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a car on a level road", intro=r"A car of mass $1200\ \mathrm{kg}$ has a driving force of $3000\ \mathrm{N}$ and a total resistive force of $800\ \mathrm{N}$. Find its acceleration, its speed after $10\ \mathrm{s}$ from rest and the distance covered.", steps=[
            S("Resultant force", math=r"F = 3000 - 800 = 2200\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Newton's second law", math=r"a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{2200}{1200} = 1.83\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Speed after $10$ s", math=r"v = at = 1.83 \times 10 = 18.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Distance", math=r"s = \tfrac12 at^2 = \tfrac12(1.83)(10)^2 = 91.7\ \mathrm{m}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: pulling a box with a rope at an angle", intro=r"A $20\ \mathrm{kg}$ box is pulled along a level floor by a rope with tension $100\ \mathrm{N}$ at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction is $0.25$. Find the acceleration.", steps=[
            S("Perpendicular to the floor: forces balance", math=r"N + 100\sin30^\circ = mg \;\Rightarrow\; N = 200 - 50 = 150\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Kinetic friction", math=r"F = \mu_k N = 0.25 \times 150 = 37.5\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Along the floor", math=r"100\cos30^\circ - 37.5 = 20a \;\Rightarrow\; 86.6 - 37.5 = 20a"),
            S("Solve", math=r"a = \frac{49.1}{20} = 2.46\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
        ]),
        TIP("The upward component of the pull reduces the normal reaction (from 200 N to 150 N). Pulling at an angle reduces friction compared with a horizontal pull."),
        EX("Worked example 3: sliding down a slope", intro=r"A block slides down a rough slope inclined at $30^\circ$ to the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction is $0.20$. Find its acceleration down the slope.", steps=[
            S("Perpendicular to the slope", math=r"N = mg\cos30^\circ"),
            S("Friction up the slope", math=r"F = \mu N = 0.20\,mg\cos30^\circ"),
            S("Along the slope (down is positive)", math=r"mg\sin30^\circ - 0.20\,mg\cos30^\circ = ma"),
            S("The mass cancels", math=r"a = g(\sin30^\circ - 0.20\cos30^\circ) = 10(0.500 - 0.173) = 3.27\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
        ]),
        T(r"A block placed on a slope is just about to slide when the component of its weight along the slope equals the limiting friction: $mg\sin\theta = \mu_s mg\cos\theta$, so $\tan\theta = \mu_s$. This is a way to measure $\mu_s$: raise a slope slowly until the block just slips."),
        H("Third-law pairs"),
        T(r"A book rests on a table. The weight of the book (Earth pulling the book down) and the normal reaction of the table (table pushing the book up) are equal and opposite, but they are **not** a third-law pair: both act on the same body, and they are different types of force. The third-law partner of the Earth's pull on the book is the book's (equal) pull on the Earth. The partner of the table's push on the book is the book's push on the table."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Calling weight and normal reaction a third-law pair", r"A third-law pair acts on **two different bodies** and is of the **same type**. The weight and the normal reaction both act on the book."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the forces cancel so nothing moves", r"Third-law forces act on different bodies, so they never cancel each other. A horse moves a cart because the ground pushes the horse forward harder than the cart pulls it back."),
        MISTAKE("Using N = mg on a slope", r"On a slope $N = mg\cos\theta$. Using $mg$ makes the friction far too large."),
        MISTAKE("Adding forces that act on different bodies", r"In a free-body diagram show **only forces acting on the chosen body**. Do not include the force it exerts on something else."),
        QUIZ(r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley is pulled along a smooth horizontal track by a force of $6.0\ \mathrm{N}$. What is its acceleration?",
             [r"$0.33\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$12\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$8.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$"], 1,
             r"$a = F/m = 6.0/2.0 = 3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$.",
             hint="Use F = ma.",
             feedback=["You divided the mass by the force.", "", "You multiplied force by mass.", "You added the force and the mass."]),
        QUIZ(r"A skydiver falls at a constant (terminal) velocity. Which statement is correct?",
             ["The air resistance is greater than the weight", "The resultant force is zero", "The weight has become zero", "There are no forces acting"], 1,
             "A constant velocity means zero acceleration, so by Newton's first law the resultant force is zero: air resistance equals weight.",
             hint="What does constant velocity say about the acceleration?",
             feedback=["If drag were larger, the diver would be slowing down.", "", "Weight does not disappear.", "Weight and drag still act; they balance."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A car of mass $900\ \mathrm{kg}$ travelling at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ is brought to rest by a constant braking force of $4500\ \mathrm{N}$. Find the deceleration, the time to stop and the stopping distance.",
           r"$a = F/m = 4500/900 = 5.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ (deceleration). $t = 20/5.0 = 4.0\ \mathrm{s}$. $s = v^2/(2a) = 400/10 = 40\ \mathrm{m}$."),
        PQ(r"A $15\ \mathrm{kg}$ crate is pushed along a level floor by a horizontal force of $60\ \mathrm{N}$. The coefficient of kinetic friction is $0.30$. Find the acceleration.",
           r"$N = mg = 150\ \mathrm{N}$. $F = 0.30 \times 150 = 45\ \mathrm{N}$. $a = (60 - 45)/15 = 1.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$."),
        PQ(r"A block is on a rough plank. The plank is raised slowly, and the block just begins to slide when the plank is at $31^\circ$ to the horizontal. Find the coefficient of static friction.",
           r"At the point of slipping, $mg\sin\theta = \mu_s mg\cos\theta$, so $\mu_s = \tan\theta = \tan31^\circ = 0.60$."),
        PQ(r"A student says: 'A horse pulls a cart forward with a force, and by Newton's third law the cart pulls the horse back with an equal and opposite force. So the forces cancel and the cart can never move.' Explain the mistake.",
           r"The two forces act on different bodies (the horse pulls the cart; the cart pulls the horse), so they cannot cancel. For the cart, the forward pull of the horse is greater than the resistance, so it accelerates. For the horse, the forward push of the ground (friction on its hooves) is greater than the backward pull of the cart. The system moves forward because of the resultant force on each body, with the ground providing the external push."),
        TIP("In the exam: Newton's laws appear in almost every Paper 2 mechanics question and in Paper 1 (definition of the newton, third-law pairs, resultant force). State the law you use, draw the free-body diagram, and write 'resultant force = mass x acceleration' along the motion."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("First law: no resultant force means constant velocity. Second law: resultant force = rate of change of momentum, $F = ma$ for constant mass. Third law: equal and opposite forces on two different bodies.",
            r"Weight is $mg$ (a force in newtons); mass is a measure of inertia (in kg).",
            "A free-body diagram has only the forces on one body. Resolve along and perpendicular to the motion.",
            r"Friction: $F = \mu N$ while sliding; static friction can be at most $\mu_sN$; $N$ must be found from the forces perpendicular to the surface.",
            r"On a slope, the component of weight down the slope is $mg\sin\theta$ and $N = mg\cos\theta$."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Newton's laws 2
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Newton's laws and linear dynamics",
    title="Linear dynamics: connected bodies, lifts and terminal velocity",
    slug="phl1-connected-bodies-lifts-terminal-velocity",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Apply F = ma to two or more connected bodies, pulleys, tows and lifts, find tensions and apparent weight, and explain terminal velocity.",
    objectives=[
        "Write one equation of motion for each body in a connected system and solve them together",
        "Find the tension in a string or tow-bar and the force on a pulley support",
        "Calculate apparent weight in an accelerating lift",
        "Explain terminal velocity in terms of drag and weight",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Connected bodies", "Tension", "Light inextensible string", "Apparent weight", "Terminal velocity", "Drag"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A lorry pulls a trailer along the road to Bertoua. The coupling between them has a tension. Lifts in buildings in Douala make you feel heavier when they start going up. A water pump pulls a bucket up from a well over a pulley. In all of these, two or more bodies are linked, and Newton's second law must be applied to **each** body in turn."),
        D(r"If a **light, inextensible string** joins two bodies and passes over a smooth pulley, the tension $T$ is the same all along the string, and both bodies have the same **size** of acceleration.", title="Connected bodies: standard assumptions",
          note="Light: the string's mass is negligible. Inextensible: it does not stretch. Smooth pulley: no friction, so it does not change the tension."),
        KEY("Method for connected bodies", "Never skip a step.", cards=[
            ["Separate", "Draw each body in its own free-body diagram."],
            ["Same $a$, same $T$", "Same size of acceleration for bodies on a taut string; the same tension on both ends over a smooth pulley."],
            ["One equation each", "Resultant force on each body $= ma$ along its motion. Choose the direction of motion as positive for every body."],
            ["Solve together", "Add the equations (the tension cancels) to get $a$; then use one equation to get $T$."],
        ]),
        IMG("al-phl1-connected-bodies.svg", alt="A trolley of mass M on a smooth table joined by a string over a pulley to a hanging mass m; the string tension T acts on both, and both accelerate at a",
            caption="Figure 1. A trolley of mass M pulled by a hanging mass m. The tension T pulls the trolley forwards and the hanging mass upwards."),
        EX("Worked example 1: trolley and hanging mass", intro=r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley on a horizontal table is joined by a light string over a smooth pulley to a $0.50\ \mathrm{kg}$ mass hanging freely. Find the acceleration and the tension (a) when the table is smooth and (b) when the coefficient of friction between trolley and table is $0.10$.", steps=[
            S("(a) Trolley (to the right): the tension is the only horizontal force", math=r"T = 2.0a"),
            S("Hanging mass (downwards positive)", math=r"0.50(10) - T = 0.50a"),
            S("Add the two equations; $T$ cancels", math=r"5.0 = 2.5a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Tension", math=r"T = 2.0 \times 2.0 = 4.0\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S(r"(b) Friction on the trolley $= 0.10 \times 2.0 \times 10 = 2.0$ N. Trolley equation", math=r"T - 2.0 = 2.0a"),
            S("Add to the hanging-mass equation", math=r"5.0 - 2.0 = 2.5a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 1.2\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Tension", math=r"T = 0.50(10 - 1.2) = 4.4\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"T - 2.0 = 2.0(1.2) \Rightarrow T = 4.4\ \mathrm{N}"),
        T(r"Notice that the tension $4.0\ \mathrm{N}$ is **less than** the weight of the hanging mass ($5.0\ \mathrm{N}$). The hanging mass accelerates downwards, so the resultant force on it is downwards."),
        EX("Worked example 2: two masses over a pulley", intro=r"Masses of $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ and $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ hang from the two ends of a light string over a smooth pulley. They are released from rest. Find the acceleration, the tension, and the force the string exerts on the pulley.", steps=[
            S("Heavier mass (down positive)", math=r"3.0g - T = 3.0a"),
            S("Lighter mass (up positive)", math=r"T - 2.0g = 2.0a"),
            S("Add", math=r"g = 5.0a \;\Rightarrow\; a = 2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Tension from the lighter mass", math=r"T = 2.0(10 + 2.0) = 24\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Force on the pulley: two string sections pull down with $T$ each", math=r"2T = 48\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"3.0(10) - 24 = 6.0 = 3.0(2.0)"),
        H("Lifts and apparent weight"),
        D(r"The **apparent weight** of a person is the reaction force $R$ of the floor (or the reading of a scale) on them. It equals the true weight $mg$ only when the acceleration is zero.", title="Apparent weight",
          formula=r"R - mg = ma \quad (\text{upwards positive})",
          note="When the lift accelerates upwards, R > mg and you feel heavier. When it accelerates downwards, R < mg."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a lift", intro=r"A person of mass $70\ \mathrm{kg}$ stands on a weighing scale in a lift. What does the scale read when the lift (a) accelerates upwards at $1.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, (b) accelerates downwards at $1.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, (c) moves up at a constant speed, (d) is in free fall?", steps=[
            S("(a) Upwards positive", math=r"R - 700 = 70(1.5) \;\Rightarrow\; R = 805\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("(b) Acceleration is $-1.5$", math=r"R - 700 = 70(-1.5) \;\Rightarrow\; R = 595\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("(c) $a = 0$", math=r"R = 700\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("(d) $a = -g$", math=r"R = 70(10 - 10) = 0 \ (\text{apparent weightlessness})"),
        ], check=r"805/10 = 80.5\ \mathrm{kg}, \quad 595/10 = 59.5\ \mathrm{kg}\ (\text{apparent masses})"),
        TIP("A scale reads the force it feels. When it reads 805 N it does not mean you have gained mass; it means the scale pushes up on you with 805 N."),
        H("Terminal velocity"),
        T(r"A falling object also experiences **drag** (air resistance), a force that opposes the motion and grows with speed. For a skydiver or a raindrop, as the speed rises the drag rises, until it equals the weight. Then the resultant force is zero, the acceleration is zero and the speed stays constant."),
        D(r"The **terminal velocity** is the constant velocity reached when the drag (plus any upthrust) on a falling body equals its weight, so the resultant force is zero.", title="Terminal velocity",
          note="Opening a parachute increases the drag a great deal. The skydiver slows down until a lower terminal velocity is reached, at which drag again equals weight."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the tension as the weight of the hanging mass", r"If the hanging mass is accelerating, $T \neq mg$. Write the second-law equation for it."),
        MISTAKE("Giving the two bodies different signs for their acceleration", r"Pick the direction of motion as positive for each body. If the string is taut, the bodies share the same magnitude of acceleration."),
        MISTAKE("Calling the scale reading the 'real weight'", r"The reading is the reaction force. Your real weight $mg$ does not change when the lift accelerates."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking terminal velocity means no forces", r"At terminal velocity the forces are **balanced** (drag $=$ weight). The weight and drag are still acting."),
        QUIZ(r"Two masses, $m_1 = 4.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ and $m_2 = 1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$, hang from a light string over a smooth pulley. What is the acceleration?",
             [r"$6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$", r"$3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$"], 0,
             r"$a = \dfrac{(m_1 - m_2)g}{m_1 + m_2} = \dfrac{3.0 \times 10}{5.0} = 6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$.",
             hint="The driving force is the difference of weights; the mass moved is the sum.",
             feedback=["", "That is free fall. The lighter mass holds the heavier one back.", "You used 2 as the denominator instead of the total mass 5.0 kg.", "You used the difference of masses as the denominator."]),
        QUIZ(r"A lift moving upwards slows down before stopping at a floor (acceleration is downwards). A passenger's apparent weight is",
             ["greater than her true weight", "equal to her true weight", "less than her true weight", "zero"], 2,
             r"The acceleration is downwards, so $R - mg = -ma$ and $R = m(g - a) < mg$.",
             hint="Which way does the resultant force on the person point?",
             feedback=["That happens when the lift accelerates upwards.", "That needs a zero resultant force.", "", "That would need $a = g$ (free fall)."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A truck of mass $2000\ \mathrm{kg}$ pulls a trailer of mass $800\ \mathrm{kg}$. The driving force on the truck is $5600\ \mathrm{N}$. Resistive forces are $400\ \mathrm{N}$ on the truck and $200\ \mathrm{N}$ on the trailer. Find the acceleration and the tension in the tow-bar.",
           r"Whole system: $5600 - 400 - 200 = 5000\ \mathrm{N}$, mass $2800\ \mathrm{kg}$, so $a = 1.79\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. Trailer alone: $T - 200 = 800a$, so $T = 200 + 800(1.786) = 1630\ \mathrm{N}$. Check on the truck: $5600 - 400 - T = 2000a = 3571$, giving $T = 1629\ \mathrm{N}$."),
        PQ(r"A $4.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ block on a smooth slope inclined at $30^\circ$ is joined by a light string over a pulley at the top to a $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ mass hanging freely. Find the acceleration and the tension.",
           r"Along the slope the weight component is $4.0(10)\sin30^\circ = 20\ \mathrm{N}$ (down the slope). The hanging mass drags with $30\ \mathrm{N}$. Resultant $= 30 - 20 = 10\ \mathrm{N}$ on a total mass of $7.0\ \mathrm{kg}$: $a = 1.43\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. $T = 3.0(10 - 1.43) = 25.7\ \mathrm{N}$."),
        PQ(r"A $60\ \mathrm{kg}$ person on a scale in a lift sees it read $540\ \mathrm{N}$. Find the acceleration of the lift and say what the lift may be doing.",
           r"$R - mg = ma$: $540 - 600 = 60a$, so $a = -1.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ (downwards). The lift is either accelerating downwards at $1.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ or moving upwards and slowing down at that rate."),
        PQ(r"Explain, with reference to the forces, why a skydiver who has just jumped accelerates at first and then falls at a steady speed, and why opening the parachute makes her slow down.",
           r"At the start the speed is small, drag is small, so the resultant force (weight minus drag) is large and she accelerates. As speed rises, drag rises, the resultant force and acceleration fall, until drag equals weight: the resultant is zero, and she falls at constant terminal velocity. Opening the parachute suddenly increases the drag above the weight, so the resultant force is upwards and she decelerates. As speed falls, drag falls until it again equals weight, at a smaller terminal velocity."),
        TIP("In the exam: connected-body and lift problems are standard Paper 2 questions. Write one equation per body, show the tension cancelling, and check the answer with a second equation."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("For connected bodies, write one $F = ma$ equation for each body; a taut light string has the same tension throughout and the bodies have the same acceleration size.",
            "Add the equations to eliminate the tension, then substitute back to find it.",
            r"Apparent weight (the reaction $R$) is $m(g + a)$ for upward acceleration $a$; it is zero in free fall.",
            "Terminal velocity: drag equals weight, the resultant force is zero and the speed is constant.",
            "The tension in a string supporting an accelerating mass is not equal to its weight."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Conservation of linear momentum
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Conservation of linear momentum",
    title="Momentum, impulse and the conservation of linear momentum",
    slug="phl1-momentum-impulse-conservation",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Define momentum and impulse, derive the conservation of momentum from Newton's laws, and apply it to bodies that stick together, recoil and explode.",
    objectives=[
        "Define momentum and impulse and relate impulse to a force-time graph",
        "Derive the conservation of momentum from Newton's second and third laws",
        "Apply the principle with correct signs to collisions that stick together, and to recoil and explosions",
        "Explain how seat belts and crumple zones reduce the force in a crash",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Linear momentum", "Impulse", "Force-time graph", "Isolated system", "Conservation of momentum", "Recoil"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A fully loaded lorry and a bicycle can both move at $5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, but it is much harder to stop the lorry. The quantity that measures how hard it is to stop a moving body is its **momentum**. In this lesson we meet momentum, see how a force changes it (**impulse**), and then use the most useful rule in collision problems: when bodies interact without outside forces, their total momentum does not change."),
        D(r"The **linear momentum** of a body is the product of its mass and its velocity. It is a vector, in the same direction as the velocity. Unit: $\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$ (or N s).", title="Momentum",
          formula=r"\mathbf{p} = m\mathbf{v}"),
        H("Impulse"),
        T(r"Newton's second law in its general form is $F = \dfrac{\Delta p}{\Delta t}$. For a force acting for a time $\Delta t$, rearranging gives the change of momentum."),
        D(r"The **impulse** of a force is the force multiplied by the time for which it acts. It equals the change in momentum of the body it acts on. For a variable force, the impulse is the **area under the force-time graph**.", title="Impulse",
          formula=r"J = F\,\Delta t = \Delta p = mv - mu",
          note="Unit: N s, the same as kg m s⁻¹."),
        EX("Worked example 1: kicking a ball", intro=r"A football of mass $0.45\ \mathrm{kg}$ arrives at a player at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and is kicked straight back at $25\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The contact lasts $0.020\ \mathrm{s}$. Find the impulse and the average force on the ball.", steps=[
            S("Take the direction of the kick (outgoing) as positive. Initial velocity is $-20$, final velocity is $+25$", math=r"\Delta p = m(v - u) = 0.45\,[25 - (-20)] = 20.25\ \mathrm{N\,s}"),
            S("Average force", math=r"F = \frac{\Delta p}{\Delta t} = \frac{20.25}{0.020} = 1010\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"1.0\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{N} \approx 225 \text{ times the ball's weight (4.5 N)}"),
        H("The conservation of linear momentum"),
        D(r"The total linear momentum of an **isolated system** (no resultant external force acts on it) remains constant. In a collision or explosion, the total momentum just before equals the total momentum just after.", title="Principle of conservation of linear momentum",
          formula=r"m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = m_1v_1 + m_2v_2"),
        T(r"**Why it is true.** Two bodies A and B interact for a time $\Delta t$. By Newton's third law the force on B is equal and opposite to the force on A, and the time is the same, so the impulses are equal and opposite: $\Delta p_A = -\Delta p_B$. Then $\Delta p_A + \Delta p_B = 0$ and the total momentum is unchanged."),
        KEY("How to use it", "Momentum is a vector: signs matter.", cards=[
            ["1. Positive direction", "Choose a direction (say to the right). Velocities the other way are negative."],
            ["2. Before and after", "Write total momentum before = total momentum after, with every body included."],
            ["3. Solve", "Solve for the unknown. A negative answer means the body moves in the negative direction."],
            ["4. Energy is separate", "Momentum is conserved in **every** collision of an isolated system. Kinetic energy is not (see the next lesson)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: trolleys that stick together", intro=r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley moving at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ collides with a stationary $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley. They stick together. Find their common velocity, and the kinetic energy lost.", steps=[
            S("Momentum before (right positive)", math=r"2.0(3.0) + 1.0(0) = 6.0\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Momentum after (joined, mass 3.0 kg)", math=r"3.0\,v = 6.0 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Kinetic energy before and after", math=r"\tfrac12(2.0)(3.0)^2 = 9.0\ \mathrm{J}, \qquad \tfrac12(3.0)(2.0)^2 = 6.0\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Energy lost (as heat and sound)", math=r"9.0 - 6.0 = 3.0\ \mathrm{J}"),
        ]),
        H("Recoil and explosions"),
        T(r"If bodies are initially at rest, the total momentum is zero, so after they push apart the momenta must still add to zero. The bodies move in opposite directions with momenta of equal size. The lighter body gets the higher speed."),
        EX("Worked example 3: recoil of a gun", intro=r"A rifle of mass $4.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ fires a bullet of mass $0.020\ \mathrm{kg}$ at $400\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the recoil speed of the rifle, and compare the kinetic energies.", steps=[
            S("Momentum before $= 0$. Bullet direction positive", math=r"0 = 0.020(400) + 4.0\,V"),
            S("Solve", math=r"V = -\frac{8.0}{4.0} = -2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} \quad (\text{opposite to the bullet})"),
            S("Kinetic energies", math=r"\text{bullet: } \tfrac12(0.020)(400)^2 = 1600\ \mathrm{J}, \qquad \text{rifle: } \tfrac12(4.0)(2.0)^2 = 8.0\ \mathrm{J}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The momenta are equal in size ($8.0\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$) but the kinetic energies are very different, because kinetic energy $= p^2/2m$ and the bullet is much lighter."),
        EX("Worked example 4: jumping from a canoe", intro=r"A $60\ \mathrm{kg}$ man in a $120\ \mathrm{kg}$ canoe at rest on calm water jumps off horizontally at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ relative to the water. Find the speed of the canoe (ignore water resistance).", steps=[
            S("Total momentum before is zero", math=r"0 = 60(3.0) + 120\,V"),
            S("Solve", math=r"V = -1.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("State", text="The canoe moves backwards at $1.5\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, opposite to the man."),
        ]),
        H("Safety: stretch the time"),
        T(r"The same change of momentum can be produced by a large force over a short time or a small force over a long time, since $F = \Delta p/\Delta t$. A car of mass $1200\ \mathrm{kg}$ moving at $15\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ has momentum $18\,000\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$. Stopping in $0.10\ \mathrm{s}$ needs an average force of $180\,000\ \mathrm{N}$, but stopping in $0.50\ \mathrm{s}$ (crumple zone, seat belt stretching, airbag) needs only $36\,000\ \mathrm{N}$. This is why motorcycle helmets and seat belts save lives on our roads."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Ignoring signs", r"If two bodies move towards each other, one velocity is negative. Forgetting this gives a wrong total momentum."),
        MISTAKE("Using kinetic energy conservation for every collision", r"Momentum is conserved in all collisions of an isolated system. Kinetic energy is conserved only in **elastic** collisions."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong mass after sticking", r"When bodies join, the mass after the collision is the **sum** of the masses."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing impulse with force", r"Impulse is force times time (N s). A small force acting for a long time can give the same impulse as a large force for a short time."),
        QUIZ(r"A $0.50\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball moving at $4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ hits a wall and rebounds at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ along the same line. What is the change in momentum of the ball (size)?",
             [r"$0.50\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$2.0\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$3.5\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$7.0\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$"], 2,
             r"Take the initial direction as positive: $\Delta p = 0.50(-3.0) - 0.50(4.0) = -3.5\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$, a change of size $3.5$.",
             hint="The velocity reverses, so the two momenta add in size.",
             feedback=["You subtracted the speeds without reversing the direction (4.0 − 3.0 = 1.0).", "You used only the initial momentum.", "", "You doubled the correct answer."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two skaters stand at rest on smooth ice and push each other apart. Skater A has twice the mass of skater B. Which is correct just after the push?",
             ["A moves twice as fast as B", "A and B have equal speeds", "B moves twice as fast as A", "A and B move in the same direction"], 2,
             r"Total momentum stays zero, so $m_Av_A = m_Bv_B$. With $m_A = 2m_B$ we get $v_B = 2v_A$.",
             hint="The momenta are equal in size and opposite in direction.",
             feedback=["The heavier skater moves slower.", "Equal speeds need equal masses.", "", "Momentum can only add to zero if they move in opposite directions."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A bullet of mass $10\ \mathrm{g}$ is fired horizontally into a block of wood of mass $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ suspended on strings (a ballistic pendulum) and stays inside. The block swings up through a height of $0.12\ \mathrm{m}$. Find the speed of the bullet.",
           r"After the impact, the block and bullet ($2.01\ \mathrm{kg}$) rise $0.12\ \mathrm{m}$: $v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2 \times 10 \times 0.12} = 1.55\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Momentum conservation in the impact: $0.010\,u = 2.01 \times 1.55$, so $u = 311\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. (Mechanical energy is conserved only after the impact, not during it.)"),
        PQ(r"A tennis ball of mass $0.15\ \mathrm{kg}$ is hit from rest by a racket. The force on the ball rises linearly from zero to $600\ \mathrm{N}$ and falls back to zero in a total time of $0.010\ \mathrm{s}$. Use the force-time graph to find the impulse and the speed of the ball.",
           r"The graph is a triangle: impulse $= \tfrac12 \times 600 \times 0.010 = 3.0\ \mathrm{N\,s}$. $\Delta p = mv$, so $v = 3.0/0.15 = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"Two skaters of masses $60\ \mathrm{kg}$ and $40\ \mathrm{kg}$ are at rest on smooth ice. They push apart and the $60\ \mathrm{kg}$ skater moves at $2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the speed and direction of the other skater, and the total kinetic energy gained.",
           r"$0 = 60(2.0) + 40V$, so $V = -3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$: the $40\ \mathrm{kg}$ skater moves at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ in the opposite direction. KE $= \tfrac12(60)(4.0) + \tfrac12(40)(9.0) = 120 + 180 = 300\ \mathrm{J}$ (from the chemical energy of their muscles)."),
        PQ(r"Explain, using $F = \Delta p/\Delta t$, why an airbag reduces the injury to a driver in a collision.",
           r"The driver's momentum must change from its initial value to zero. This change, $\Delta p$, is fixed. The airbag (and seat belt and crumple zone) increases the time $\Delta t$ over which the driver stops. Since $F = \Delta p/\Delta t$, a longer time means a smaller average force on the driver, so less injury."),
        TIP("In the exam: conservation of momentum is a standard Paper 2 topic (sticking, recoil, explosion, ballistic pendulum) and a Paper 1 favourite. State 'total momentum before = total momentum after' and define your positive direction."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Momentum $p = mv$ (vector). Impulse $F\Delta t = \Delta p$; the area under a force-time graph is the impulse.",
            "Total momentum of an isolated system is conserved: it follows from Newton's second and third laws.",
            "Choose a positive direction and keep the signs; bodies that stick have the combined mass.",
            "In recoil and explosions from rest, momenta are equal and opposite: the lighter body moves faster.",
            r"Longer stopping time means smaller force for the same $\Delta p$: the reason for seat belts and crumple zones."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Elastic and inelastic collisions 1: head-on
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Elastic and inelastic collisions",
    title="Elastic and inelastic collisions in one dimension",
    slug="phl1-elastic-inelastic-collisions-1d",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Classify head-on collisions by what happens to kinetic energy, use the relative-speed result for elastic collisions together with momentum conservation, and calculate the energy lost in inelastic collisions.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish elastic, inelastic and perfectly inelastic collisions in terms of kinetic energy",
        "Use conservation of momentum and the equal relative speeds of approach and separation to solve elastic collisions",
        "Calculate the kinetic energy lost in an inelastic collision",
        "Describe the special cases: equal masses, a heavy body hitting a light one and a ball hitting a wall",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Elastic collision", "Inelastic collision", "Perfectly inelastic collision", "Relative speed of approach and separation", "Kinetic energy lost"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"When two bodies collide, their total momentum is conserved (if no outside force acts). But what happens to their total **kinetic energy**? Two snooker balls bounce apart with almost no loss of kinetic energy. A taxi that runs into the back of a stationary pick-up and locks onto it loses much of its kinetic energy as sound, heat and bending metal. The type of collision decides which equations can be used."),
        CODE("""Type of collision        Momentum    Kinetic energy
elastic                 conserved   conserved
inelastic               conserved   some lost (as heat, sound...)
perfectly inelastic     conserved   maximum lost; bodies move together""", "text", title="Types of collision"),
        D(r"In an **elastic collision** the total kinetic energy before equals the total kinetic energy after. In an **inelastic collision** some kinetic energy is changed to other forms. In a **perfectly inelastic** collision the bodies stick together and move with a common velocity.", title="Elastic and inelastic collisions",
          note="Perfectly elastic collisions happen between atoms and molecules; collisions between everyday objects are nearly elastic at best. Momentum is conserved in all of them."),
        H("Solving an elastic collision"),
        T(r"Two bodies of masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ move along the same line with velocities $u_1$ and $u_2$ before the collision and $v_1$ and $v_2$ after it. Two equations are needed:"),
        F(r"m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = m_1v_1 + m_2v_2 \qquad (\text{momentum})"),
        F(r"\tfrac12m_1u_1^2 + \tfrac12m_2u_2^2 = \tfrac12m_1v_1^2 + \tfrac12m_2v_2^2 \qquad (\text{kinetic energy})"),
        T(r"Solving two such equations (one quadratic) directly is untidy. There is a neat shortcut. Write both equations as differences: $m_1(u_1 - v_1) = m_2(v_2 - u_2)$ from momentum, and $m_1(u_1^2 - v_1^2) = m_2(v_2^2 - u_2^2)$ from energy. Divide the second by the first: $u_1 + v_1 = v_2 + u_2$. Rearranging:"),
        D(r"In an **elastic** collision along a line, the relative speed of approach equals the relative speed of separation.", title="Relative-speed rule (elastic collisions only)",
          formula=r"u_1 - u_2 = v_2 - v_1",
          note="This is a linear equation, so combined with conservation of momentum it gives v1 and v2 easily. Do not use it for inelastic collisions."),
        T(r"Solving the momentum equation together with the relative-speed rule gives the standard results:"),
        F(r"v_1 = \frac{(m_1 - m_2)u_1 + 2m_2u_2}{m_1 + m_2}, \qquad v_2 = \frac{(m_2 - m_1)u_2 + 2m_1u_1}{m_1 + m_2}"),
        IMG("al-phl1-collision-1d.svg", alt="Before and after a head-on elastic collision: a 2 kg trolley moving at 6 m/s hits a stationary 4 kg trolley; afterwards the 2 kg trolley moves back at 2 m/s and the 4 kg trolley forward at 4 m/s",
            caption="Figure 1. An elastic collision between a 2 kg trolley and a stationary 4 kg trolley."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a light trolley hits a heavy one", intro=r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley moving at $6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ hits a stationary $4.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley in an elastic collision. Find their velocities afterwards.", steps=[
            S("Relative-speed rule (right positive)", math=r"6.0 - 0 = v_2 - v_1 \;\Rightarrow\; v_2 = v_1 + 6.0"),
            S("Momentum", math=r"2.0(6.0) = 2.0v_1 + 4.0v_2 \;\Rightarrow\; 12 = 2.0v_1 + 4.0(v_1 + 6.0)"),
            S("Solve for $v_1$", math=r"12 = 6.0v_1 + 24 \;\Rightarrow\; v_1 = -2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Then $v_2$", math=r"v_2 = -2.0 + 6.0 = 4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("State", text="The light trolley rebounds at $2.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and the heavy one moves forward at $4.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$."),
        ], check=r"\text{KE before: } \tfrac12(2)(36) = 36\ \mathrm{J}; \quad \text{after: } \tfrac12(2)(4) + \tfrac12(4)(16) = 4 + 32 = 36\ \mathrm{J}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: head-on, both moving", intro=r"A $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ body moving at $+4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ collides head-on and elastically with a $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ body moving at $-2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the velocities after the collision.", steps=[
            S("Relative-speed rule", math=r"4.0 - (-2.0) = v_2 - v_1 \;\Rightarrow\; v_2 = v_1 + 6.0"),
            S("Momentum", math=r"3.0(4.0) + 1.0(-2.0) = 3.0v_1 + 1.0v_2 \;\Rightarrow\; 10 = 3.0v_1 + v_1 + 6.0"),
            S("Solve", math=r"4.0v_1 = 4.0 \;\Rightarrow\; v_1 = 1.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}, \quad v_2 = 7.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"\text{KE before: } 24 + 2 = 26\ \mathrm{J}; \ \text{after: } 1.5 + 24.5 = 26\ \mathrm{J}"),
        H("Special cases of elastic collisions"),
        KEY("Three results worth remembering", "These come straight from the formulas (target initially at rest).", cards=[
            ["Equal masses", r"The moving body stops and the target moves off with the original speed: the velocities are exchanged (Newton's cradle)."],
            ["Heavy body hits a light body", r"The heavy body keeps moving, almost unchanged, and the light body flies off at about $2u$."],
            ["Light body hits a heavy body", r"The light body rebounds with almost its original speed and the heavy body hardly moves (a ball bouncing off a wall)."],
        ]),
        H("Inelastic collisions and energy lost"),
        T(r"In an inelastic collision only the momentum equation is valid. For a **perfectly inelastic** collision the two bodies move together, so $m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = (m_1 + m_2)v$. The kinetic energy lost is $\Delta E_k = E_{k,\text{before}} - E_{k,\text{after}}$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a collision on the road", intro=r"A taxi of mass $1200\ \mathrm{kg}$ moving at $15\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ runs into the back of a stationary pick-up of mass $1800\ \mathrm{kg}$. The two vehicles lock together. Find their speed just after the collision and the kinetic energy lost.", steps=[
            S("Momentum conservation with a common velocity", math=r"1200(15) = (1200 + 1800)\,v \;\Rightarrow\; v = \frac{18\,000}{3000} = 6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Kinetic energy before", math=r"\tfrac12(1200)(15)^2 = 135\,000\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Kinetic energy after", math=r"\tfrac12(3000)(6.0)^2 = 54\,000\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Energy lost", math=r"135\,000 - 54\,000 = 81\,000\ \mathrm{J} \ (60\%)"),
        ], check=r"\frac{m_2}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{1800}{3000} = 0.60"),
        T(r"For a stationary target the fraction of kinetic energy lost in a perfectly inelastic collision is $\dfrac{m_2}{m_1 + m_2}$: the heavier the target compared with the moving body, the more energy is lost."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using kinetic energy conservation for an inelastic collision", r"If the bodies stick together or crumple, use only momentum. Kinetic energy is lost."),
        MISTAKE("Using the relative-speed rule with a wrong sign", r"The relative speed of approach is $u_1 - u_2$ (with signs), and the relative speed of separation is $v_2 - v_1$. Both are positive when body 1 is behind body 2 and approaching it."),
        MISTAKE("Assuming momentum is lost when kinetic energy is lost", r"Momentum is conserved in **all** collisions of an isolated system; only kinetic energy changes."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking 'elastic' means the bodies are elastic", r"The word describes what happens to the kinetic energy: none is lost. A steel ball bearing is a much better elastic colliding body than a rubber ball."),
        QUIZ(r"A moving trolley of mass $m$ collides elastically and head-on with a stationary trolley of the **same** mass. What happens?",
             ["Both trolleys move off together at half the original speed", "The first trolley stops and the second moves off with the original speed", "The first trolley rebounds and the second moves off at half the speed", "Both trolleys stop"], 1,
             r"With $m_1 = m_2$ the formulas give $v_1 = 0$ and $v_2 = u_1$: the velocities are exchanged.",
             hint="Use the relative-speed rule together with momentum.",
             feedback=["That is a perfectly inelastic collision (they stick together).", "", "That would not conserve momentum.", "That would violate conservation of momentum."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two bodies collide. Before: $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ at $4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ at rest. After: $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ at $2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ at $6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. What type of collision was it?",
             ["Perfectly inelastic", "Inelastic", "Elastic", "Impossible, as momentum is not conserved"], 2,
             r"Momentum: before $12$, after $6 + 6 = 12$. Kinetic energy: before $\tfrac12(3)(16) = 24\ \mathrm{J}$, after $\tfrac12(3)(4) + \tfrac12(1)(36) = 6 + 18 = 24\ \mathrm{J}$. Both conserved: elastic.",
             hint="Calculate the total kinetic energy before and after.",
             feedback=["The bodies move at different speeds afterwards, so they have not stuck together.", "The kinetic energy before and after is the same, 24 J.", "", "Momentum is conserved: 12 before, 12 after."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A $0.50\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley moving at $2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ collides elastically with a stationary trolley of the same mass. State the velocities afterwards and explain how you know.",
           r"Equal masses in an elastic collision exchange velocities: the first trolley stops ($v_1 = 0$) and the second moves at $2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Check: momentum $1.0$ before and after; kinetic energy $1.0\ \mathrm{J}$ before and after."),
        PQ(r"A $5.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley at $4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ catches up with a $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley moving the same way at $2.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. They stick together. Find the common velocity and the kinetic energy lost.",
           r"$v = (5.0 \times 4.0 + 3.0 \times 2.0)/8.0 = 3.25\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. KE before $= 40 + 6 = 46\ \mathrm{J}$; after $= \tfrac12(8.0)(3.25)^2 = 42.25\ \mathrm{J}$. Energy lost $= 3.75\ \mathrm{J}$."),
        PQ(r"A $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley at $4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ hits a stationary $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley. Afterwards the $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley moves back at $1.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the speed of the other trolley and decide whether the collision is elastic.",
           r"Momentum: $4.0 = 1.0(-1.0) + 3.0V$, so $V = 1.67\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. KE before $= 8.0\ \mathrm{J}$. KE after $= 0.5 + \tfrac12(3.0)(1.67)^2 = 0.5 + 4.17 = 4.67\ \mathrm{J}$. Kinetic energy has been lost ($3.33\ \mathrm{J}$), so the collision is inelastic. The relative speed of separation ($2.67$) is less than that of approach ($4.0$)."),
        PQ(r"Show that when a very light ball of mass $m$ hits a very heavy stationary body of mass $M$ ($M \gg m$) elastically at speed $u$, the ball rebounds at nearly speed $u$.",
           r"For $u_2 = 0$: $v_1 = \dfrac{(m - M)u}{m + M}$. When $M \gg m$ this tends to $\dfrac{-Mu}{M} = -u$: the ball rebounds at nearly its original speed, and the heavy body, with $v_2 = \dfrac{2mu}{m + M}$ close to $0$, hardly moves."),
        TIP("In the exam: you may be asked to show that a collision is elastic or inelastic by calculating kinetic energy before and after (Paper 2) or to choose the correct statement about momentum and energy (Paper 1). Show both totals."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Momentum is conserved in every collision of an isolated system; kinetic energy is conserved only in elastic collisions.",
            r"Elastic, head-on: relative speed of approach = relative speed of separation, $u_1 - u_2 = v_2 - v_1$.",
            r"Equal masses exchange velocities; a light body bounces back off a heavy one with about its own speed.",
            "Perfectly inelastic: the bodies move together, and the maximum kinetic energy is lost.",
            "To classify a collision, compare the total kinetic energy before and after."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M2 / Elastic and inelastic collisions 2: two dimensions
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Elastic and inelastic collisions",
    title="Collisions in two dimensions",
    slug="phl1-collisions-in-two-dimensions",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Apply conservation of momentum separately along two perpendicular axes, solve oblique collisions, show that equal masses in an elastic collision with a stationary target separate at 90 degrees, and handle sticking and explosion problems.",
    objectives=[
        "Resolve momenta into perpendicular components and apply conservation of momentum to each",
        "Solve oblique collisions for unknown speeds and angles, and test whether they are elastic",
        "Show that after an elastic collision of equal masses (one initially at rest) the bodies move apart at 90 degrees",
        "Solve a sticking collision between bodies moving at right angles, and a two-fragment explosion",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Oblique collision", "Components of momentum", "Momentum triangle", "90 degree rule", "Perpendicular axes"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Not every collision is head-on. A pool ball hits another off-centre and they fly apart at angles. Two cars meet at a junction in Ngaoundéré, one travelling east and one north. Momentum is a **vector**, so conservation of momentum must hold in every direction. We apply it separately along two perpendicular axes."),
        D(r"In a collision of an isolated system, the total momentum is conserved. Resolve along perpendicular axes $x$ and $y$:", title="Conservation of momentum in two dimensions",
          formula=r"\sum p_x(\text{before}) = \sum p_x(\text{after}), \qquad \sum p_y(\text{before}) = \sum p_y(\text{after})"),
        KEY("Method", "Treat each axis as a separate one-dimensional problem.", cards=[
            ["1. Axes", "Choose $x$ along the initial motion of the moving body, $y$ perpendicular to it."],
            ["2. Resolve", r"Write the components of every velocity before and after, using $v\cos\theta$ and $v\sin\theta$ and the signs of the directions."],
            ["3. Equations", r"Write one momentum equation for $x$ and one for $y$."],
            ["4. Solve and recombine", r"Find the unknown components, then speed $=\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2}$ and angle $=\tan^{-1}(v_y/v_x)$."],
            ["5. Energy check", "If the problem needs it, compare the total kinetic energy before and after to decide whether the collision is elastic."],
        ]),
        IMG("al-phl1-collision-2d.svg", alt="Momentum vectors for a two-dimensional collision: the momentum before, 10 kg m/s along x, equals the vector sum of the two momenta afterwards",
            caption="Figure 1. The two momenta after the collision add as vectors to the momentum before, forming a closed triangle with it."),
        EX("Worked example 1: an oblique collision", intro=r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball moving at $5.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ along $+x$ hits a stationary $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball. After the collision the $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball moves at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $30^\circ$ above the $x$-axis. Find the velocity of the $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball, and decide whether the collision is elastic.", steps=[
            S("$x$-momentum: before $=$ after", math=r"2.0(5.0) = 2.0(3.0\cos30^\circ) + 3.0V_x \;\Rightarrow\; 10 = 5.196 + 3.0V_x \;\Rightarrow\; V_x = 1.60\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("$y$-momentum: before $= 0$", math=r"0 = 2.0(3.0\sin30^\circ) + 3.0V_y \;\Rightarrow\; V_y = -1.00\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Speed of the $3.0$ kg ball", math=r"V = \sqrt{1.60^2 + 1.00^2} = 1.89\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Direction", math=r"\theta = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{-1.00}{1.60}\right) = -32.0^\circ \ (32^\circ \text{ below the } x\text{-axis})"),
            S("Kinetic energy before and after", math=r"\tfrac12(2.0)(5.0)^2 = 25\ \mathrm{J}; \quad \tfrac12(2.0)(3.0)^2 + \tfrac12(3.0)(1.89)^2 = 9.0 + 5.35 = 14.3\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Conclude", text="Kinetic energy is lost (about $10.7\\ \\mathrm{J}$), so the collision is inelastic."),
        ]),
        H("The 90 degree rule for equal masses"),
        D(r"If a body of mass $m$ collides **elastically** with an identical stationary body, and both then move, their paths after the collision are **at right angles** to each other.", title="Equal-mass elastic collisions"),
        T(r"**Proof.** Let the incoming velocity be $\mathbf{u}$ and the velocities after be $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_2$. Momentum conservation gives $m\mathbf{u} = m\mathbf{v}_1 + m\mathbf{v}_2$, so $\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{v}_1 + \mathbf{v}_2$. Squaring (a scalar product): $u^2 = v_1^2 + v_2^2 + 2\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2$. Kinetic energy conservation gives $\tfrac12mu^2 = \tfrac12mv_1^2 + \tfrac12mv_2^2$, that is $u^2 = v_1^2 + v_2^2$. Comparing the two, $\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2 = 0$. The two velocities are perpendicular, unless one is zero (a head-on collision)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: two pool balls", intro=r"A cue ball of mass $0.50\ \mathrm{kg}$ moving at $4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ hits an identical stationary ball elastically. The cue ball is deflected through $35^\circ$. Find the speeds and directions of both balls afterwards.", steps=[
            S("The paths are at $90^\\circ$ to each other", text="So the other ball leaves at $90^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 55^\\circ$ on the opposite side of the original line."),
            S("The velocity vectors form a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse $u = 4.0$", math=r"v_1 = 4.0\cos35^\circ = 3.28\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}, \qquad v_2 = 4.0\sin35^\circ = 2.29\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"\tfrac12(0.50)(3.28^2 + 2.29^2) = 4.0\ \mathrm{J} = \tfrac12(0.50)(4.0)^2"),
        H("Sticking collisions at an angle"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a collision at a junction", intro=r"A car of mass $1200\ \mathrm{kg}$ travelling east at $15\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ collides at a junction with a van of mass $1000\ \mathrm{kg}$ travelling north at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. They lock together. Find their velocity just after the collision. (Invented figures for teaching.)", steps=[
            S("East momentum (all from the car)", math=r"p_x = 1200(15) = 18\,000\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("North momentum (all from the van)", math=r"p_y = 1000(20) = 20\,000\ \mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Combined mass $2200$ kg", math=r"V_x = \frac{18\,000}{2200} = 8.18\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}, \qquad V_y = \frac{20\,000}{2200} = 9.09\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Speed", math=r"V = \sqrt{8.18^2 + 9.09^2} = 12.2\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Direction north of east", math=r"\theta = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{9.09}{8.18}\right) = 48.0^\circ"),
        ], check=r"\text{KE before } 335\,000\ \mathrm{J}, \ \text{after } 164\,500\ \mathrm{J}: \text{about } 170\,000\ \mathrm{J} \text{ lost}"),
        T(r"The kinetic energy lost shows the damage. This is why a crash at a junction is dangerous even when each vehicle was not travelling very fast."),
        H("Explosions in two dimensions"),
        T(r"For an explosion or fragmentation, the total momentum just after equals the total momentum just before. If a moving shell breaks into two parts, the momentum of the pieces must add (as vectors) to the momentum of the shell. See practice question 1 below."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Adding speeds instead of components", r"Velocities are vectors. A car going east at $15$ and a van going north at $20$ do not make $35$: combine the components with Pythagoras."),
        MISTAKE("Using the 90 degree rule when it does not apply", r"It needs **equal masses**, an **elastic** collision and a **stationary target**. It fails for unequal masses or for inelastic collisions."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the sign of $y$-components", r"A ball going below the $x$-axis has a negative $y$-velocity. Check that the $y$-momenta add to the (usually zero) initial value."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the other body when finding the missing velocity", r"Write the momentum equation for the whole system: unknown components appear only in the term for the unknown body."),
        QUIZ(r"A $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball at $6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ along $+x$ hits a stationary $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball. After the collision the $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball moves at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ along $-y$ (at right angles). What is the $y$-component of the velocity of the $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball?",
             [r"$-1.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$+1.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$+3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$", r"$0$"], 1,
             r"$y$-momentum: $0 = 1.0(-3.0) + 2.0V_y$, so $V_y = +1.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ (the other ball goes the opposite way in $y$).",
             hint="The total y-momentum must stay zero.",
             feedback=["That has the same sign as the first ball, so the total $y$-momentum would not be zero.", "", "You forgot to divide by the mass of the $2.0\\ \\mathrm{kg}$ ball.", "Then the $y$-momentum after would be $-3.0$, not zero."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two identical ice-hockey pucks collide elastically; one is initially at rest. After the collision both move. The angle between their directions of motion is",
             [r"$45^\circ$", r"$60^\circ$", r"$90^\circ$", r"$180^\circ$"], 2,
             r"This is the 90 degree rule: for equal masses and an elastic collision with a stationary target, the velocities after are perpendicular.",
             hint="Combine momentum conservation with energy conservation.",
             feedback=["There is no such rule for $45^\\circ$.", "There is no such rule for $60^\\circ$.", "", "That would be a head-on collision with one puck stopping, in which only one puck moves after."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A shell of mass $6.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ moving east at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ explodes into two fragments. A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ fragment moves north at $30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the velocity of the $4.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ fragment.",
           r"East: $6.0(20) = 120 = 4.0V_x$, so $V_x = 30\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. North: $0 = 2.0(30) + 4.0V_y$, so $V_y = -15\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ (south). Speed $= \sqrt{30^2 + 15^2} = 33.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $\tan^{-1}(15/30) = 26.6^\circ$ south of east."),
        PQ(r"A puck moving at $5.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ strikes an identical stationary puck elastically and is deflected by $30^\circ$. Find the speeds of the two pucks afterwards and the angle of the second puck.",
           r"The pucks move apart at $90^\circ$. First puck: $5.0\cos30^\circ = 4.33\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $30^\circ$. Second puck: $5.0\sin30^\circ = 2.5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $60^\circ$ on the other side of the original line. (Check: $4.33^2 + 2.5^2 = 25 = 5.0^2$.)"),
        PQ(r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ body moving east at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ collides with a $1.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ body moving north at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and they stick together. Find the speed and direction of the combined body.",
           r"East: $2.0(3.0) = 6.0 = 3.0V_x$, $V_x = 2.0$. North: $1.0(3.0) = 3.0 = 3.0V_y$, $V_y = 1.0$. Speed $= \sqrt{2.0^2 + 1.0^2} = 2.24\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at $\tan^{-1}(1.0/2.0) = 26.6^\circ$ north of east."),
        PQ(r"Two equal masses collide elastically with one initially at rest. State three conditions needed for the 90 degree rule and give the key step in the proof.",
           r"Conditions: equal masses, an elastic collision, one body initially at rest (and both move afterwards). Key step: $\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{v}_1 + \mathbf{v}_2$ from momentum, so $u^2 = v_1^2 + v_2^2 + 2\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2$; energy gives $u^2 = v_1^2 + v_2^2$. Hence $\mathbf{v}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_2 = 0$, so the velocities are perpendicular."),
        TIP("In the exam: two-dimensional collisions appear in Paper 2 as a structured question with a diagram. Draw the momentum triangle or resolve along two axes, and finish with a kinetic energy check if asked about the type of collision."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Momentum is conserved separately along two perpendicular axes (equivalently, the momentum vectors form a closed triangle).",
            "Resolve each velocity into components, write one equation per axis, and recombine with Pythagoras and tangent.",
            "A collision is elastic only if the total kinetic energy is unchanged: calculate it before and after.",
            "Equal masses, elastic, stationary target: the two bodies move apart at $90^\\circ$ (derived from the dot product).",
            "Sticking collisions at right angles: add the momentum components to find the combined velocity."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Coplanar forces and equilibrium
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Coplanar forces and equilibrium",
    title="Coplanar forces: resultants, the triangle of forces and equilibrium",
    slug="phl1-coplanar-forces-equilibrium",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Find the resultant of several forces in one plane by resolving into components, use the closed force polygon, and solve equilibrium problems with cables, slopes and friction.",
    objectives=[
        "Resolve a force into perpendicular components and add forces by components",
        "State the conditions for a particle to be in equilibrium under coplanar forces",
        "Use the triangle of forces and component equations to find unknown forces and angles",
        "Solve equilibrium problems on a hanging load, a body on a slope and a body pulled at constant velocity",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Coplanar forces", "Resolving a force", "Resultant", "Equilibrium", "Equilibrant", "Triangle of forces"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A street lamp in Douala hangs from two cables fixed to poles. A tent stays up because the ropes, the pole and the ground balance each other. In every case several forces act in one plane on an object that does **not** accelerate. We need two skills: adding forces as vectors, and using the fact that the resultant is zero in equilibrium."),
        D(r"Forces are **coplanar** if they all lie in the same plane. The **resultant** of several forces is the single force with the same effect as all of them together.", title="Coplanar forces and the resultant"),
        H("Resolving forces"),
        T(r"A force $F$ at an angle $\theta$ to the $x$-axis has components along the axes:"),
        F(r"F_x = F\cos\theta, \qquad F_y = F\sin\theta"),
        T(r"To find the resultant of several forces, add all the $x$-components and all the $y$-components separately, then combine them:"),
        F(r"R_x = \sum F_x, \quad R_y = \sum F_y, \quad R = \sqrt{R_x^2 + R_y^2}, \quad \tan\alpha = \frac{R_y}{R_x}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: resultant of three forces", intro=r"Three forces act at a point: $10\ \mathrm{N}$ at $0^\circ$, $8.0\ \mathrm{N}$ at $60^\circ$ and $6.0\ \mathrm{N}$ at $150^\circ$ (angles measured anticlockwise from the $x$-axis). Find the resultant and the force that would balance them (the equilibrant).", steps=[
            S("$x$-components", math=r"R_x = 10 + 8.0\cos60^\circ + 6.0\cos150^\circ = 10 + 4.0 - 5.20 = 8.80\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("$y$-components", math=r"R_y = 0 + 8.0\sin60^\circ + 6.0\sin150^\circ = 6.93 + 3.00 = 9.93\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Magnitude", math=r"R = \sqrt{8.80^2 + 9.93^2} = 13.3\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Direction", math=r"\alpha = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{9.93}{8.80}\right) = 48.4^\circ \text{ above the } x\text{-axis}"),
            S("The equilibrant has the same size and the opposite direction", math=r"13.3\ \mathrm{N} \text{ at } 48.4^\circ + 180^\circ = 228.4^\circ"),
        ]),
        H("Equilibrium"),
        D(r"A particle (or a set of forces acting at one point) is in **equilibrium** when the resultant force is zero. Then the acceleration is zero: the body is at rest or moves with constant velocity. In components:", title="Equilibrium of coplanar forces",
          formula=r"\sum F_x = 0, \qquad \sum F_y = 0",
          note="For forces that do not act at a single point, there is a third condition: the sum of moments must also be zero (next lesson)."),
        T(r"**The triangle of forces.** If three forces acting at a point are in equilibrium, drawing them head to tail gives a **closed triangle**. The sides are proportional to the sizes of the forces. This lets you use the sine rule, or Pythagoras when the triangle is right-angled. Three coplanar forces in equilibrium always act through a single point."),
        IMG("al-phl1-three-forces.svg", alt="A 12 kg lamp hung from two cables at 30 degrees and 60 degrees to the horizontal, and the closed right-angled triangle of forces formed by the weight and the two tensions",
            caption="Figure 1. A lamp of weight 120 N hung from two cables. The three forces form a closed triangle."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a lamp on two cables", intro=r"A $12\ \mathrm{kg}$ lamp (weight $120\ \mathrm{N}$) hangs at rest from two cables that make angles of $30^\circ$ and $60^\circ$ with the horizontal ceiling, as in Figure 1. Find the tensions $T_1$ (at $30^\circ$) and $T_2$ (at $60^\circ$).", steps=[
            S("Horizontal forces balance", math=r"T_1\cos30^\circ = T_2\cos60^\circ \;\Rightarrow\; T_2 = 1.732\,T_1"),
            S("Vertical forces balance", math=r"T_1\sin30^\circ + T_2\sin60^\circ = 120"),
            S("Substitute for $T_2$", math=r"0.5\,T_1 + 1.732(0.866)\,T_1 = 120 \;\Rightarrow\; 2.0\,T_1 = 120"),
            S("Solve", math=r"T_1 = 60\ \mathrm{N}, \qquad T_2 = 1.732 \times 60 = 104\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"\text{Right-angled triangle: } T_1 = 120\sin30^\circ = 60\ \mathrm{N}, \ T_2 = 120\cos30^\circ = 104\ \mathrm{N}"),
        T(r"Notice that the cable at the smaller angle to the horizontal does **not** carry the larger tension here: the steeper cable (at $60^\circ$) carries more, because it supports more of the weight."),
        EX("Worked example 3: pulling a sledge at constant velocity", intro=r"A boy pulls a loaded sledge of weight $400\ \mathrm{N}$ at constant velocity along level ground with a rope at $20^\circ$ above the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction is $0.30$. Find the tension in the rope.", steps=[
            S("Constant velocity means equilibrium. Vertical balance", math=r"N + T\sin20^\circ = 400 \;\Rightarrow\; N = 400 - 0.342\,T"),
            S("Horizontal balance (friction $= \\mu N$)", math=r"T\cos20^\circ = 0.30\,N = 0.30(400 - 0.342\,T)"),
            S("Collect terms in $T$", math=r"0.940\,T + 0.103\,T = 120 \;\Rightarrow\; T = \frac{120}{1.042} = 115\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Normal reaction, for checking", math=r"N = 400 - 0.342(115) = 361\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"T\cos20^\circ = 108.2\ \mathrm{N}, \quad \mu N = 0.30 \times 360.7 = 108.2\ \mathrm{N}"),
        H("A body at rest on a slope"),
        T(r"A $10\ \mathrm{kg}$ block rests on a rough slope inclined at $25^\circ$. Take axes along and perpendicular to the slope. Along the slope, the static friction balances the component of weight: $F = mg\sin\theta = 100\sin25^\circ = 42.3\ \mathrm{N}$. Perpendicular to the slope: $N = mg\cos\theta = 90.6\ \mathrm{N}$. For the block not to slip, $F \le \mu_sN$, which needs $\mu_s \ge \tan25^\circ = 0.466$."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Swapping sine and cosine", r"Draw the angle on your diagram and ask: is the component **adjacent** to the angle (cosine) or **opposite** it (sine)? Test with a very small or very large angle."),
        MISTAKE("Assuming equal tensions", r"Two cables supporting a load carry equal tensions only if they make equal angles with the vertical."),
        MISTAKE("Dropping forces from the free-body diagram", r"Include weight, normal reaction, friction, tension and every push. A missing force makes the sums of components fail."),
        MISTAKE("Using $N = mg$ when there is a vertical component of another force", r"Find $N$ from the vertical balance. A rope pulling upwards reduces it."),
        QUIZ(r"Forces of $6\ \mathrm{N}$ east and $8\ \mathrm{N}$ north act on a particle. What third force makes the particle stay at rest?",
             [r"$10\ \mathrm{N}$ towards the south-west (opposite to the resultant)", r"$14\ \mathrm{N}$ towards the north-east", r"$2\ \mathrm{N}$ towards the west", r"$10\ \mathrm{N}$ towards the north-east"], 0,
             r"The resultant of $6$ east and $8$ north is $\sqrt{36 + 64} = 10\ \mathrm{N}$ towards the north-east. The equilibrant is the same size and opposite: $10\ \mathrm{N}$ towards the south-west.",
             hint="First find the resultant, then reverse it.",
             feedback=["", "You added the sizes of the forces as numbers.", "You subtracted the sizes. East and north are perpendicular.", "That is the resultant, in the wrong sense. A balancing force must point the other way."]),
        QUIZ(r"A $50\ \mathrm{N}$ picture hangs from a hook on a wire whose two halves each make $30^\circ$ with the horizontal. What is the tension in each half?",
             [r"$25\ \mathrm{N}$", r"$50\ \mathrm{N}$", r"$100\ \mathrm{N}$", r"$43\ \mathrm{N}$"], 1,
             r"The vertical components of the two tensions support the weight: $2T\sin30^\circ = 50$, so $T = 50\ \mathrm{N}$ in each half. A flatter wire has a larger tension.",
             hint="Balance the vertical components.",
             feedback=[r"You shared the weight equally and ignored the angle: only the vertical component of each tension supports the weight.", "", r"You used $2T = 50 \times 2$, or forgot that $\sin30^\circ = 0.5$ appears once in each half.", r"That is $50\cos30^\circ$: the wrong component."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Forces of $5.0\ \mathrm{N}$ and $12\ \mathrm{N}$ act at right angles on a body. Find the size and direction of the resultant relative to the $12\ \mathrm{N}$ force.",
           r"$R = \sqrt{5.0^2 + 12^2} = 13\ \mathrm{N}$. The angle with the $12\ \mathrm{N}$ force is $\tan^{-1}(5.0/12) = 22.6^\circ$."),
        PQ(r"A lamp of mass $5.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ hangs from a string. It is pulled aside by a horizontal force $F$ until the string makes $30^\circ$ with the vertical, and held there. Find $F$ and the tension $T$.",
           r"Vertical: $T\cos30^\circ = mg = 50\ \mathrm{N}$, so $T = 57.7\ \mathrm{N}$. Horizontal: $F = T\sin30^\circ = 28.9\ \mathrm{N}$ (or $F = mg\tan30^\circ$)."),
        PQ(r"A $5.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ block is held at rest on a smooth slope of $30^\circ$ by a rope parallel to the slope. Find the tension in the rope and the normal reaction.",
           r"Along the slope: $T = mg\sin30^\circ = 25\ \mathrm{N}$. Perpendicular: $N = mg\cos30^\circ = 43.3\ \mathrm{N}$."),
        PQ(r"Three forces of $6.0\ \mathrm{N}$, $8.0\ \mathrm{N}$ and $F$ act on a body in equilibrium. The $6.0\ \mathrm{N}$ and $8.0\ \mathrm{N}$ forces are at right angles. Find $F$ and say how the three forces could be drawn to show equilibrium.",
           r"$F$ balances the resultant of the other two: $F = \sqrt{6.0^2 + 8.0^2} = 10\ \mathrm{N}$, opposite in direction to that resultant. Drawn head to tail, the three forces form a closed right-angled triangle with sides $6.0$, $8.0$ and $10$ units."),
        TIP("In the exam: equilibrium and triangle-of-forces problems appear in Paper 2 and as quick checks in Paper 1. Draw a large free-body diagram, mark the angles clearly and write both component equations."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Resolve: $F_x = F\cos\theta$, $F_y = F\sin\theta$. Add components separately to find a resultant.",
            r"Equilibrium of a particle: $\sum F_x = 0$ and $\sum F_y = 0$; the force polygon closes.",
            "Three forces in equilibrium form a closed triangle and are concurrent (act through one point).",
            "For a body on a slope, resolve along and perpendicular to the slope; without sliding, friction $= mg\\sin\\theta$ and $N = mg\\cos\\theta$.",
            "Constant velocity is equilibrium too: friction balances the horizontal pull."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Torque and moments
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Torque and moments",
    title="Moments, couples and the equilibrium of rigid bodies",
    slug="phl1-torque-moments-couples",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Define the moment of a force and a couple, state the principle of moments, and solve beam, seesaw and ladder problems using the three conditions for the equilibrium of a rigid body.",
    objectives=[
        "Define the moment (torque) of a force about a point and calculate it using the perpendicular distance",
        "Define a couple and calculate its torque",
        "State the conditions for equilibrium of a rigid body and the principle of moments",
        "Solve problems on beams, supports and a ladder against a wall",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Moment of a force", "Torque", "Couple", "Principle of moments", "Centre of gravity", "Rigid-body equilibrium"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"It is easy to open a door by pushing near the handle, and very hard by pushing near the hinge. A long spanner loosens a stubborn wheel nut on a lorry that a short one cannot. A force does not only push or pull: it can also **turn** something. The turning effect of a force is called its **moment** or **torque**."),
        D(r"The **moment** of a force about a point (or axis) is the force multiplied by the **perpendicular distance** from the point to the line of action of the force.", title="Moment of a force",
          formula=r"\tau = F\,d_\perp = F\,r\sin\theta",
          note="Unit: N m. A moment is clockwise or anticlockwise: choose one as positive. Do not confuse with joules, which are also N m but are a different quantity."),
        T(r"Here $r$ is the distance from the pivot to the point where the force acts and $\theta$ is the angle between the force and the line from the pivot to that point. If the force acts at $90^\circ$ to the spanner, $\sin\theta = 1$ and the moment is largest. A force along the line through the pivot has no moment."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a spanner", intro=r"A force of $50\ \mathrm{N}$ is applied to the end of a $0.40\ \mathrm{m}$ spanner at $30^\circ$ to the spanner. Find the moment about the nut.", steps=[
            S("Perpendicular component of the force", math=r"F_\perp = 50\sin30^\circ = 25\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Moment", math=r"\tau = F_\perp \times r = 25 \times 0.40 = 10\ \mathrm{N\,m}"),
        ]),
        H("Couples"),
        D(r"A **couple** is a pair of equal and opposite parallel forces whose lines of action are different. A couple has a turning effect but no resultant force. Its torque is the **force times the perpendicular distance between the lines of action**.", title="Couple",
          formula=r"\tau = F\,d",
          note="The torque of a couple is the same about every point."),
        T(r"Turning a tap, a steering wheel or a key uses a couple. If a driver pulls with $15\ \mathrm{N}$ on one side of a steering wheel and pushes with $15\ \mathrm{N}$ on the other side, with the hands $0.40\ \mathrm{m}$ apart, the torque is $15 \times 0.40 = 6.0\ \mathrm{N\,m}$."),
        H("Equilibrium of a rigid body"),
        D(r"A rigid body is in **equilibrium** when (1) the resultant force is zero, and (2) the resultant moment about any point is zero. The second condition is the **principle of moments**: the sum of the clockwise moments about any point equals the sum of the anticlockwise moments about that point.", title="Conditions for equilibrium",
          formula=r"\sum \mathbf{F} = 0, \qquad \sum \tau = 0"),
        KEY("A good method", "Choose the pivot cleverly.", cards=[
            ["1. Draw", "Draw the body and all the forces at their points of action, with the weight of a uniform body at its centre."],
            ["2. Pick a pivot", "Take moments about a point where an unknown force acts. That force then has zero moment and drops out."],
            ["3. Moments", "Write clockwise = anticlockwise."],
            ["4. Forces", "Then balance forces vertically and horizontally to find the rest."],
        ]),
        T(r"The **centre of gravity** of a body is the point through which its whole weight appears to act. For a uniform rod, plank or beam it is at the middle."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a plank on two supports", intro=r"A uniform plank of length $4.0\ \mathrm{m}$ and mass $20\ \mathrm{kg}$ rests on two supports $A$ and $B$, $0.50\ \mathrm{m}$ from the left and right ends respectively. A $30\ \mathrm{kg}$ sack stands on it $1.0\ \mathrm{m}$ from the left end. Find the support forces.", steps=[
            S("Distances from $A$ (at $0.50$ m from the left end): sack $0.50$ m, plank centre $1.5$ m, support $B$ $3.0$ m", text="Weights: sack $300\\ \\mathrm{N}$, plank $200\\ \\mathrm{N}$."),
            S("Moments about $A$ (the force at $A$ has no moment)", math=r"R_B \times 3.0 = 300(0.50) + 200(1.5) = 450 \;\Rightarrow\; R_B = 150\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Balance vertical forces", math=r"R_A + R_B = 500 \;\Rightarrow\; R_A = 350\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"\text{Moments about } B:\ R_A \times 3.0 = 200(1.5) + 300(2.5) = 1050 \Rightarrow R_A = 350\ \mathrm{N}"),
        H("A ladder against a wall"),
        IMG("al-phl1-ladder.svg", alt="A uniform ladder at 60 degrees against a smooth wall and a rough floor, with weight W at the middle, wall reaction R horizontal, normal reaction N and friction F at the floor",
            caption="Figure 1. A uniform ladder, 5.0 m long and of weight 200 N, against a smooth wall."),
        T(r"The wall is smooth, so its reaction $R$ is horizontal (no friction at the wall). The floor is rough, so it gives a normal reaction $N$ upwards and a friction force $F$ towards the wall. The ladder is about to slip if $F$ reaches the limiting value $\mu N$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: will the ladder slip?", intro=r"The $5.0\ \mathrm{m}$ uniform ladder of weight $200\ \mathrm{N}$ in Figure 1 makes $60^\circ$ with the floor. Find the forces at the wall and floor, and the smallest coefficient of friction at the floor that keeps it from slipping.", steps=[
            S("Take moments about the foot of the ladder. Weight acts at the middle: horizontal distance $2.5\\cos60^\\circ$. Wall force $R$ acts at height $5.0\\sin60^\\circ$", math=r"R(5.0\sin60^\circ) = 200(2.5\cos60^\circ)"),
            S("Solve", math=r"R = \frac{200 \times 1.25}{4.33} = 57.7\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Vertical forces", math=r"N = 200\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Horizontal forces", math=r"F = R = 57.7\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Need $F \\le \\mu N$", math=r"\mu \ge \frac{57.7}{200} = 0.29"),
        ]),
        TIP("The ladder is more likely to slip when it is flatter or when someone stands high up on it. Try it: a person of 70 kg standing 3.0 m up the ladder makes R about 300 N, so the friction needed is about 0.33 of the total vertical force."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong distance", r"The moment uses the **perpendicular** distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force, not the distance along the body unless the force is perpendicular to it."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the weight of the beam", r"A uniform beam's weight acts at its centre and has a moment about the pivot. Leaving it out is the most common error."),
        MISTAKE("Taking moments about a point and forgetting a force there", r"A force through the pivot has no moment. But all other forces must be included, including the one at the other support."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a couple has a resultant force", r"A couple has zero resultant force. It cannot be balanced by a single force: only by another couple."),
        QUIZ(r"A uniform metre rule balances at the $50\ \mathrm{cm}$ mark. A $100\ \mathrm{g}$ mass is hung at the $20\ \mathrm{cm}$ mark. Where must a $60\ \mathrm{g}$ mass be hung to restore the balance?",
             ["At the $80\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ mark", "At the $100\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ mark", "At the $70\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ mark", "At the $75\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ mark"], 1,
             r"Moments about $50$ cm: $100 \times 30 = 60 \times d$, so $d = 50\ \mathrm{cm}$ to the right, at the $100\ \mathrm{cm}$ mark.",
             hint="Equate the moments about the 50 cm mark.",
             feedback=["That would balance a 100 g mass at 20 cm only with a 100 g mass at 80 cm.", "", "That distance is 20 cm, which is too small.", "That distance is 25 cm, which is too small."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two forces of $12\ \mathrm{N}$ act in opposite directions along parallel lines $0.30\ \mathrm{m}$ apart. What are the resultant force and the torque?",
             [r"$24\ \mathrm{N}$ and $7.2\ \mathrm{N\,m}$", r"$0$ and $3.6\ \mathrm{N\,m}$", r"$0$ and $0$", r"$12\ \mathrm{N}$ and $3.6\ \mathrm{N\,m}$"], 1,
             r"This is a couple: the resultant force is zero and the torque is $Fd = 12 \times 0.30 = 3.6\ \mathrm{N\,m}$.",
             hint="Equal and opposite parallel forces form a couple.",
             feedback=["You added the sizes of the forces: they are opposite and cancel.", "", "The forces cancel, but the torque does not.", "The forces cancel; there is no resultant."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A child of mass $30\ \mathrm{kg}$ sits $2.0\ \mathrm{m}$ from the pivot of a light seesaw. How far from the pivot, on the other side, must a $40\ \mathrm{kg}$ child sit for the seesaw to balance?",
           r"Moments: $30g \times 2.0 = 40g \times d$, so $d = 60/40 = 1.5\ \mathrm{m}$."),
        PQ(r"A wheelbarrow carries a load of $40\ \mathrm{kg}$ whose centre of gravity is $0.40\ \mathrm{m}$ horizontally from the wheel axle. The handles are held $1.2\ \mathrm{m}$ from the axle. Ignoring the weight of the barrow, find the upward force needed on the handles.",
           r"Moments about the axle: $F \times 1.2 = 400 \times 0.40$, so $F = 160/1.2 = 133\ \mathrm{N}$."),
        PQ(r"A uniform rectangular crate of mass $60\ \mathrm{kg}$, $0.60\ \mathrm{m}$ wide and $1.0\ \mathrm{m}$ tall, stands on rough floor. A horizontal force $P$ is applied at the top edge. Find the value of $P$ at which the crate is just about to tip about its lower edge (assume no slipping).",
           r"At the point of tipping, the normal reaction acts at the lower edge. Moments about that edge: $P \times 1.0 = 600 \times 0.30$ (the weight acts at the centre, $0.30\ \mathrm{m}$ horizontally from the edge). So $P = 180\ \mathrm{N}$."),
        PQ(r"The ladder of Worked example 3 is climbed by a $70\ \mathrm{kg}$ man who stands $3.0\ \mathrm{m}$ along the ladder from its foot. Find the force on the wall and the smallest coefficient of friction needed.",
           r"Moments about the foot: $R(5.0\sin60^\circ) = 200(2.5\cos60^\circ) + 700(3.0\cos60^\circ) = 250 + 1050 = 1300$, so $R = 300\ \mathrm{N}$. Vertical: $N = 900\ \mathrm{N}$. $F = R = 300\ \mathrm{N}$, so $\mu \ge 300/900 = 0.33$."),
        TIP("In the exam: moments questions are common in Paper 2 (beams, ladders, hinged rods) and Paper 1. Say 'taking moments about X' and name the pivot. Choosing the pivot at an unknown force saves a lot of algebra."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Moment $= F \times$ perpendicular distance $= Fr\sin\theta$ (unit N m).",
            r"A couple: two equal, opposite, parallel forces; torque $= F \times$ separation; no resultant force.",
            "Equilibrium of a rigid body: resultant force zero and total moment about any point zero (clockwise = anticlockwise).",
            "The weight of a uniform body acts at its centre; take moments about a point where an unknown force acts.",
            r"For a ladder against a smooth wall: $R$ from moments about the foot, then $N = W$ and $F = R$, and $\mu \ge F/N$."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Work, energy and power
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Work, energy and power",
    title="Work, kinetic and potential energy, conservation of energy and power",
    slug="phl1-work-energy-power",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Define work as force times displacement in the direction of the force, find work from a force-displacement graph, use the work-energy theorem and conservation of energy, and calculate power and efficiency.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate work done by a constant force at an angle, and by a variable force from the area under a graph",
        "State and use the work-energy theorem and the formulas for kinetic, gravitational and elastic potential energy",
        "Apply conservation of energy, including problems with friction",
        "Calculate power as W/t and as Fv, and efficiency",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Work", "Kinetic energy", "Gravitational potential energy", "Work-energy theorem", "Conservation of energy", "Power", "Efficiency"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A water pump lifts water from a borehole into a tank, a lorry climbs the hill out of Bamenda, a child slides down a slide. In each case energy is transferred. In physics, **work** is the amount of energy transferred by a force, and **power** says how fast it is transferred."),
        D(r"The **work done** by a force is the product of the force and the displacement in the direction of the force. For a constant force $F$ at an angle $\theta$ to the displacement $s$:", title="Work",
          formula=r"W = F\,s\cos\theta",
          note="Unit: the joule (J), 1 J = 1 N m. Work is a scalar and can be negative (for example, friction or braking, where the force opposes the displacement)."),
        T(r"If $\theta = 90^\circ$ the force does no work, as for the normal reaction on a body sliding along a surface, or the tension in a string on a body moving in a circle."),
        EX("Worked example 1: pulling a trolley", intro=r"A rope pulls a trolley $50\ \mathrm{m}$ along level ground. The rope tension is $400\ \mathrm{N}$ and the rope makes $25^\circ$ with the ground. How much work does the rope do?", steps=[
            S("Component of force along the displacement", math=r"F\cos25^\circ = 400 \times 0.906 = 362.5\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Work", math=r"W = 362.5 \times 50 = 1.81\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{J} = 18.1\ \mathrm{kJ}"),
        ]),
        H("Work from a force-displacement graph"),
        T(r"When the force varies, the work done is the **area under the force-displacement graph**. In the figure a force rises from $0$ to $40\ \mathrm{N}$ over the first $2\ \mathrm{m}$, stays at $40\ \mathrm{N}$ for the next $3\ \mathrm{m}$ and falls to zero over the last $1\ \mathrm{m}$. The area is $40 + 120 + 20 = 180\ \mathrm{J}$."),
        IMG("al-phl1-force-displacement.svg", alt="Graph of force against displacement: a trapezium with areas 40 J, 120 J and 20 J, total 180 joules",
            caption="Figure 1. The area under a force-displacement graph is the work done: 180 J."),
        H("Energy"),
        D(r"**Kinetic energy** is the energy a body has because of its motion. **Gravitational potential energy** near the Earth's surface is the energy due to height. **Elastic potential energy** is stored in a stretched or compressed spring.", title="Forms of energy",
          formula=r"E_k = \tfrac12mv^2, \qquad E_p = mgh, \qquad E_{\text{elastic}} = \tfrac12kx^2"),
        D(r"The **work-energy theorem**: the net work done on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy.", title="Work-energy theorem",
          formula=r"W_{\text{net}} = \tfrac12mv^2 - \tfrac12mu^2",
          note="Derivation for a constant force: v² = u² + 2as and F = ma give Fs = ½mv² − ½mu²."),
        D(r"**Conservation of energy:** energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another. If only gravity (and spring forces) do work, mechanical energy $E_k + E_p$ is constant. Friction transfers mechanical energy to thermal energy.", title="Conservation of energy"),
        EX("Worked example 2: braking", intro=r"A car of mass $1000\ \mathrm{kg}$ is travelling at $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and brakes to rest with a constant braking force of $5000\ \mathrm{N}$. Find the stopping distance.", steps=[
            S("Work done by the brakes is negative: it removes the kinetic energy", math=r"-F s = 0 - \tfrac12mu^2"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"5000\,s = \tfrac12(1000)(20)^2 = 2.0\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Solve", math=r"s = 40\ \mathrm{m}"),
        ], check=r"a = F/m = 5.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}, \ s = u^2/2a = 40\ \mathrm{m}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a slide", intro=r"A $25\ \mathrm{kg}$ child slides down a slide from a height of $3.2\ \mathrm{m}$, starting from rest. (a) How fast is she moving at the bottom if there is no friction? (b) Actually her speed at the bottom is $6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. How much work is done against friction?", steps=[
            S("(a) Energy conservation", math=r"mgh = \tfrac12mv^2 \;\Rightarrow\; v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2(10)(3.2)} = 8.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("(b) Potential energy lost", math=r"mgh = 25(10)(3.2) = 800\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Kinetic energy gained", math=r"\tfrac12(25)(6.0)^2 = 450\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("The difference is the work done against friction (thermal energy)", math=r"800 - 450 = 350\ \mathrm{J}"),
        ]),
        H("Power and efficiency"),
        D(r"**Power** is the rate of doing work (or transferring energy). When a force $F$ moves a body at constant speed $v$ along the line of the force, the power is $Fv$. **Efficiency** is the ratio of useful output to total input.", title="Power and efficiency",
          formula=r"P = \frac{W}{t} = Fv, \qquad \text{efficiency} = \frac{\text{useful output energy (or power)}}{\text{total input energy (or power)}}",
          note="Unit of power: the watt, 1 W = 1 J s⁻¹. Efficiency has no unit and is often given as a percentage."),
        EX("Worked example 4: a lorry on a hill", intro=r"A lorry of mass $2000\ \mathrm{kg}$ climbs a slope of $1$ in $10$ ($\sin\theta = 0.1$) at a constant speed of $10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The resistive forces total $500\ \mathrm{N}$. Find the power of the engine.", steps=[
            S("Constant speed means zero resultant force along the slope. Component of weight down the slope", math=r"mg\sin\theta = 2000(10)(0.1) = 2000\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Driving force", math=r"F = 500 + 2000 = 2500\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Power", math=r"P = Fv = 2500 \times 10 = 25\,000\ \mathrm{W} = 25\ \mathrm{kW}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 5: a water pump", intro=r"A pump raises $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ of water every second through a height of $12\ \mathrm{m}$. Find the useful power output and, if the pump is $60\%$ efficient, the input power.", steps=[
            S("Useful work per second", math=r"P_{\text{out}} = \frac{mgh}{t} = 3.0(10)(12) = 360\ \mathrm{W}"),
            S("Input power", math=r"P_{\text{in}} = \frac{360}{0.60} = 600\ \mathrm{W}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Ignoring the angle", r"Only the component of force along the displacement does work. Use $F\cos\theta$."),
        MISTAKE("Saying 'work is done' when you hold a heavy load still", r"With no displacement, $W = Fs = 0$ in physics, even though your muscles get tired."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that energy is not conserved when friction acts", r"Mechanical energy is lost; the total energy (including thermal energy) is conserved. Account for friction work."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing power and energy", r"A kilowatt-hour is a unit of **energy** ($3.6 \times 10^6\ \mathrm{J}$). A kilowatt is a unit of power."),
        QUIZ(r"A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball is thrown upwards at $10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Ignoring air resistance, what maximum height does it reach?",
             [r"$5.0\ \mathrm{m}$", r"$10\ \mathrm{m}$", r"$20\ \mathrm{m}$", r"$2.5\ \mathrm{m}$"], 0,
             r"$\tfrac12mu^2 = mgh$, so $h = u^2/2g = 100/20 = 5.0\ \mathrm{m}$. The mass cancels.",
             hint="Equate kinetic energy at the start to potential energy at the top.",
             feedback=["", "You used $h = u^2/g$, missing the factor 2.", "You used $h = u^2/(g/2)$, an algebra slip.", "You halved the correct answer."]),
        QUIZ(r"A force of $20\ \mathrm{N}$ acts at $90^\circ$ to the direction a body moves, over a distance of $5.0\ \mathrm{m}$. How much work does the force do?",
             [r"$100\ \mathrm{J}$", r"$20\ \mathrm{J}$", r"$0$", r"$4.0\ \mathrm{J}$"], 2,
             r"$W = Fs\cos90^\circ = 0$. A force perpendicular to the motion does no work.",
             hint="Use W = F s cos(theta).",
             feedback=["You ignored the angle.", "You used the force alone.", "", "You divided the distance by the force."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Use the force-displacement graph of Figure 1 to find the speed of a $10\ \mathrm{kg}$ trolley, initially at rest on a smooth surface, after the force has acted for the full $6\ \mathrm{m}$.",
           r"Work done $= 180\ \mathrm{J} = $ gain in kinetic energy: $\tfrac12(10)v^2 = 180$, so $v = 6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A spring of stiffness $200\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$ is compressed by $0.10\ \mathrm{m}$ and then used to launch a $0.050\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball along a smooth horizontal track. Find the elastic energy stored and the launch speed.",
           r"$E = \tfrac12kx^2 = \tfrac12(200)(0.10)^2 = 1.0\ \mathrm{J}$. All becomes kinetic energy: $\tfrac12(0.050)v^2 = 1.0$, so $v = \sqrt{40} = 6.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A motor of input power $1.5\ \mathrm{kW}$ lifts a $200\ \mathrm{kg}$ load at a steady $0.50\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the useful power output and the efficiency.",
           r"Useful power $= Fv = 2000 \times 0.50 = 1000\ \mathrm{W}$. Efficiency $= 1000/1500 = 0.67$ (67 %)."),
        PQ(r"A car of mass $800\ \mathrm{kg}$ accelerates from $10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ to $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ in $4.0\ \mathrm{s}$ on level ground. Find the net work done and the average net power.",
           r"$W = \tfrac12(800)(20^2 - 10^2) = 1.2\times10^5\ \mathrm{J}$. Average power $= W/t = 1.2\times10^5/4.0 = 3.0\times10^4\ \mathrm{W} = 30\ \mathrm{kW}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: work, energy and power appear in Paper 2 (often combined with slopes and friction) and in Paper 1. State the principle you use (conservation of energy or work-energy theorem) and account for each energy form."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Work $W = Fs\cos\theta$; the area under a force-displacement graph is the work done.",
            r"$E_k = \tfrac12mv^2$, $E_p = mgh$, $E_{\text{elastic}} = \tfrac12kx^2$; net work $=$ change in kinetic energy.",
            "Energy is conserved overall; friction changes mechanical energy to thermal energy.",
            r"Power $P = W/t = Fv$; efficiency $=$ useful output / total input.",
            "A force perpendicular to the displacement does no work."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Circular motion and centripetal acceleration
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Circular motion and centripetal acceleration",
    title="Circular motion: angular speed and centripetal acceleration",
    slug="phl1-circular-motion-centripetal",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Describe circular motion with angle in radians, angular speed and period; derive a = v^2/r = omega^2 r towards the centre and use F = mv^2/r to find the centripetal force provided by tension, friction or gravity.",
    objectives=[
        "Convert between degrees, radians and revolutions, and relate v, omega, f and T",
        "Explain why a body moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating",
        "Derive and use a = v^2/r = omega^2 r and F = mv^2/r",
        "Identify what provides the centripetal force in different situations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Radian", "Angular speed", "Period and frequency", "Centripetal acceleration", "Centripetal force"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A taxi takes a bend on the road out of Kribi, a washing machine spins your clothes, the Earth turns once a day. All these things move in circles. Even at a **constant speed**, a body moving in a circle is **accelerating**, because its velocity keeps changing direction. This lesson explains that acceleration and the force that causes it."),
        H("Angles in radians"),
        D(r"The **radian** is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius. An arc of length $s$ on a circle of radius $r$ subtends an angle $\theta = s/r$ radians.", title="Radian",
          formula=r"\theta = \frac{s}{r}, \qquad 2\pi\ \mathrm{rad} = 360^\circ = 1\ \text{revolution}",
          note="The radian is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no dimensions."),
        D(r"The **angular speed** $\omega$ is the rate of change of angle. For one revolution in a time $T$ (the period), or $f$ revolutions per second:", title="Angular speed, period, frequency",
          formula=r"\omega = \frac{\Delta\theta}{\Delta t} = \frac{2\pi}{T} = 2\pi f, \qquad v = r\omega",
          note="Unit of ω: rad s⁻¹. Frequency f in hertz (revolutions per second). 60 revolutions per minute = 1 Hz."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a bicycle wheel", intro=r"A point on the rim of a bicycle wheel of radius $0.35\ \mathrm{m}$ turns at $4.0$ revolutions per second. Find its angular speed, its speed and its acceleration.", steps=[
            S("Angular speed", math=r"\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi(4.0) = 25.1\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Speed", math=r"v = r\omega = 0.35 \times 25.1 = 8.80\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Acceleration (derived below)", math=r"a = \omega^2 r = (25.1)^2(0.35) = 221\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
        ], check=r"v^2/r = 8.80^2/0.35 = 221\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
        H("Centripetal acceleration"),
        IMG("al-phl1-circular-motion.svg", alt="A particle moving in a circle with velocity along the tangent and acceleration towards the centre, shown at three positions",
            caption="Figure 1. The velocity is along the tangent; the acceleration points to the centre."),
        T(r"**Why the acceleration points to the centre.** In a short time $\Delta t$ the particle moves through a small angle $\Delta\theta$. Its velocity (constant size $v$) turns through the same angle. The change in velocity $\Delta\mathbf{v}$ is the base of a thin isosceles triangle with two sides of length $v$ and a small apex angle $\Delta\theta$, so its size is $v\,\Delta\theta$, and it points towards the centre. The acceleration is therefore"),
        F(r"a = \frac{\Delta v}{\Delta t} = v\,\frac{\Delta\theta}{\Delta t} = v\omega = \frac{v^2}{r} = \omega^2 r"),
        D(r"A body moving in a circle of radius $r$ at speed $v$ has an acceleration of $v^2/r = \omega^2 r$ directed towards the centre: the **centripetal acceleration**. By Newton's second law there must be a resultant force towards the centre, the **centripetal force**.", title="Centripetal acceleration and force",
          formula=r"F = ma = \frac{mv^2}{r} = m\omega^2 r"),
        KEY("Centripetal force is not a new force", "It is the name for the resultant force towards the centre. Always say what provides it.", cards=[
            ["Whirling a stone", "The tension in the string."],
            ["Car on a flat bend", "The friction between the tyres and the road."],
            ["A planet or satellite", "Gravity."],
            ["Banked track or loop", "A component of the normal reaction (and weight)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a stone on a string", intro=r"A $0.25\ \mathrm{kg}$ stone is whirled in a horizontal circle of radius $0.60\ \mathrm{m}$ on a smooth table at $2.0$ revolutions per second. Find the tension in the string.", steps=[
            S("Angular speed", math=r"\omega = 2\pi(2.0) = 12.57\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Tension provides the centripetal force", math=r"T = m\omega^2 r = 0.25 \times 12.57^2 \times 0.60 = 23.7\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: the fastest safe speed on a flat bend", intro=r"A car goes round a flat circular bend of radius $50\ \mathrm{m}$. The coefficient of friction between the tyres and the road is $0.60$. Find the greatest speed at which it can take the bend without skidding.", steps=[
            S("Friction provides the centripetal force. Maximum friction", math=r"F_{\max} = \mu N = \mu mg"),
            S("Set equal to the centripetal force", math=r"\mu mg = \frac{mv^2}{r} \;\Rightarrow\; v^2 = \mu g r"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v = \sqrt{0.60 \times 10 \times 50} = 17.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} \approx 62\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The mass cancels, so a heavy lorry and a light car have the same limiting speed on the same road. On a wet road $\mu$ is smaller, and so is the safe speed: a good reason to slow down in the rainy season."),
        H("What happens when the force vanishes?"),
        T(r"If the string breaks or the friction fails, there is no centripetal force and the body moves in a **straight line along the tangent** with its velocity at that instant (Newton's first law). It does not fly outwards radially. A passenger in a car turning left seems pushed to the right: really, the passenger tends to carry on in a straight line while the car turns."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Adding a 'centrifugal force' to the free-body diagram", r"In an inertial frame there is no outward force. Draw only the real forces (tension, friction, weight, normal reaction); their resultant towards the centre is $mv^2/r$."),
        MISTAKE("Saying constant speed means no acceleration", r"Acceleration is a change of velocity, which includes a change of direction. A body in uniform circular motion has an acceleration $v^2/r$ even though its speed is constant."),
        MISTAKE(r"Using degrees or revolutions in $v = r\omega$", r"The relation $v = r\omega$ needs $\omega$ in **radians** per second. Convert revolutions per minute: $\omega = 2\pi \times \text{rpm}/60$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong radius", r"$r$ is the radius of the circle the body moves in, not the diameter and not the size of the object."),
        QUIZ(r"A body moves in a circle at constant speed. Which statement about its acceleration is correct?",
             ["It is zero because the speed is constant", "It is directed along the tangent, in the direction of motion", "It is directed towards the centre and has magnitude $v^2/r$", "It is directed away from the centre"], 2,
             r"The velocity changes direction, and the change is towards the centre. The size is $v^2/r$.",
             hint="Which way does the change of velocity point?",
             feedback=["Acceleration includes a change of direction.", "A tangential acceleration would change the speed, which is constant.", "", "That describes the (fictitious) centrifugal effect, not a real acceleration."]),
        QUIZ(r"A car takes a flat circular bend at speed $v$ without skidding. The speed is now doubled on the same bend. By what factor does the required friction force increase?",
             [r"$2$", r"$4$", r"$8$", r"$\sqrt{2}$"], 1,
             r"The force needed is $mv^2/r$, proportional to $v^2$. Doubling $v$ multiplies the force by $4$.",
             hint="Look at how F depends on v.",
             feedback=["That would be so if F were proportional to $v$.", "", "That would be a $v^3$ dependence.", "That would be a square-root dependence."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A car wheel of radius $0.30\ \mathrm{m}$ rotates at $900$ revolutions per minute. Find $\omega$, the speed of a point on the tyre and its centripetal acceleration.",
           r"$f = 900/60 = 15\ \mathrm{Hz}$. $\omega = 2\pi(15) = 94.2\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$. $v = 0.30 \times 94.2 = 28.3\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. $a = \omega^2r = 94.2^2(0.30) = 2.66\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$."),
        PQ(r"A $1500\ \mathrm{kg}$ car goes round a bend of radius $40\ \mathrm{m}$ at $12\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. What is the centripetal force, and what provides it?",
           r"$F = mv^2/r = 1500 \times 144/40 = 5400\ \mathrm{N}$. It is provided by the sideways friction between the tyres and the road."),
        PQ(r"The maximum friction coefficient between tyres and a wet road is $0.40$. Find the greatest speed on a bend of radius $80\ \mathrm{m}$, in $\mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$.",
           r"$v = \sqrt{\mu gr} = \sqrt{0.40 \times 10 \times 80} = 17.9\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} = 64\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A student whirls a $0.20\ \mathrm{kg}$ ball on a string of length $0.80\ \mathrm{m}$ in a horizontal circle (ignore gravity's effect on the circle) at a constant speed of $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the tension. Then explain what happens if the string suddenly breaks.",
           r"$T = mv^2/r = 0.20 \times 9.0/0.80 = 2.25\ \mathrm{N}$. If the string breaks, there is no centripetal force, so by Newton's first law the ball moves off in a straight line along the tangent at $3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ (and then falls under gravity)."),
        TIP("In the exam: circular motion appears in Paper 1 (what provides the centripetal force, scaling with v and r) and Paper 2 (calculations with friction, tension and satellites). Say what provides the centripetal force before using $mv^2/r$."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$\omega = \Delta\theta/\Delta t = 2\pi/T = 2\pi f$ (rad s⁻¹) and $v = r\omega$.",
            r"Centripetal acceleration $a = v^2/r = \omega^2 r$ is directed towards the centre.",
            r"The resultant force towards the centre is $F = mv^2/r = m\omega^2 r$: it is provided by real forces such as tension, friction or gravity.",
            "A body moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating: the velocity changes direction.",
            "If the centripetal force disappears, the body moves along the tangent."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Banked tracks and vertical circles 1: banked tracks
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Banked tracks and vertical circles",
    title="Banked tracks, aircraft turns and the conical pendulum",
    slug="phl1-banked-tracks-conical-pendulum",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Find the ideal speed v = sqrt(r g tan theta) on a banked track without friction, extend to limiting speeds with friction, and apply the same method to an aircraft in a banked turn and a conical pendulum.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain how banking provides a horizontal component of the normal reaction",
        "Derive the ideal speed and banking angle: tan(theta) = v^2/(rg)",
        "Include friction to find the maximum speed on a banked track",
        "Apply the same method to an aircraft banking and a conical pendulum",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Banked track", "Ideal speed", "Banking angle", "Normal reaction components", "Conical pendulum"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"On a flat bend, only friction keeps a car on the road, and a wet road can be dangerous. Road and racetrack engineers **bank** the bend: they tilt the road surface so that its outer edge is higher. Then the road pushes the car **inwards** as well as upwards, and the car can take the bend safely even if the surface is icy or oily. Cyclists and aircraft do the same by leaning or banking."),
        IMG("al-phl1-banked-track.svg", alt="Cross-section of a banked track: normal reaction N tilted towards the centre, weight W downwards, and the horizontal resultant that provides the centripetal force",
            caption="Figure 1. The normal reaction N is perpendicular to the track surface. Its horizontal component provides the centripetal force."),
        H("The ideal banking: no friction needed"),
        T(r"A car of mass $m$ moves at speed $v$ round a track of radius $r$ banked at angle $\theta$ to the horizontal. Assume no friction. Two forces act: the weight $mg$ downwards and the normal reaction $N$ perpendicular to the surface, tilted at $\theta$ from the vertical towards the centre."),
        T(r"**Vertical** (no vertical acceleration): $N\cos\theta = mg$. **Horizontal** (towards the centre of the circle): $N\sin\theta = \dfrac{mv^2}{r}$."),
        T(r"Divide the second equation by the first: the mass $m$ and the unknown $N$ cancel."),
        D(r"For a car on a frictionless banked track of angle $\theta$ and radius $r$, there is one speed (the **ideal speed**, or design speed) at which no friction is needed:", title="Ideal speed on a banked track",
          formula=r"\tan\theta = \frac{v^2}{rg} \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad v = \sqrt{rg\tan\theta}",
          note="It does not depend on the mass of the car. At other speeds friction is needed: down the slope if the car is too fast, up the slope if it is too slow (see below)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the design speed", intro=r"A circular track of radius $80\ \mathrm{m}$ is banked at $15^\circ$. Find the speed at which a car needs no friction.", steps=[
            S("Ideal speed", math=r"v = \sqrt{rg\tan\theta} = \sqrt{80 \times 10 \times \tan15^\circ}"),
            S("Evaluate ($\\tan15^\\circ = 0.268$)", math=r"v = \sqrt{214.4} = 14.6\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} \approx 53\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        H("With friction: the range of safe speeds"),
        T(r"At speeds above the ideal speed the car tends to slide **up** the bank, so friction acts **down** the slope; at lower speeds it tends to slide down, and friction acts up the slope. For maximum speed, resolve the normal reaction $N$ and limiting friction $\mu N$ (down the slope):"),
        F(r"N\cos\theta - \mu N\sin\theta = mg, \qquad N\sin\theta + \mu N\cos\theta = \frac{mv^2}{r}"),
        F(r"v_{\max} = \sqrt{rg\,\frac{\tan\theta + \mu}{1 - \mu\tan\theta}}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: with friction", intro=r"The $80\ \mathrm{m}$ radius track banked at $15^\circ$ has a friction coefficient of $0.30$ between tyres and road. Find the greatest speed at which a car can take it without skidding.", steps=[
            S("Substitute into the formula", math=r"v_{\max} = \sqrt{80 \times 10 \times \frac{0.268 + 0.30}{1 - 0.30 \times 0.268}}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"v_{\max} = \sqrt{80 \times 10 \times 0.618} = \sqrt{494} = 22.2\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} \approx 80\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        T(r"Compare with $14.6\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ for the frictionless design speed: friction allows a much higher speed. Since $\mu = 0.30$ is larger than $\tan15^\circ = 0.268$, a car could even stop on the bank without sliding down."),
        H("Other examples of the same physics"),
        KEY("Aircraft and cyclists", "The sideways 'push' comes from a different force, but the equations are the same.", cards=[
            ["Banked turn of an aircraft", r"The lift $L$ is perpendicular to the wings. $L\cos\theta = mg$ and $L\sin\theta = mv^2/r$, so $\tan\theta = v^2/(rg)$."],
            ["Cyclist", r"The resultant of the ground's normal and friction forces passes through the centre of mass, and the cyclist leans at $\tan\theta = v^2/(rg)$."],
            ["Conical pendulum", r"A bob on a string moves in a horizontal circle. $T\cos\theta = mg$ and $T\sin\theta = mv^2/r$."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: an aircraft turn", intro=r"An aircraft flies a horizontal circle of radius $2000\ \mathrm{m}$ at $100\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the angle of bank and the lift as a multiple of the weight.", steps=[
            S("Banking angle", math=r"\tan\theta = \frac{v^2}{rg} = \frac{10\,000}{20\,000} = 0.50 \;\Rightarrow\; \theta = 26.6^\circ"),
            S("Lift from the vertical equation", math=r"L = \frac{mg}{\cos\theta} = 1.12\,mg"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 4: a conical pendulum", intro=r"A small bob on a string of length $1.0\ \mathrm{m}$ moves in a horizontal circle with the string at $30^\circ$ to the vertical. Find the speed of the bob and the time for one revolution.", steps=[
            S("Radius of the circle", math=r"r = l\sin30^\circ = 0.50\ \mathrm{m}"),
            S("Speed (the same equations with $T$ in place of $N$)", math=r"v = \sqrt{rg\tan\theta} = \sqrt{0.50 \times 10 \times 0.577} = 1.70\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Period", math=r"T = \frac{2\pi r}{v} = \frac{2\pi(0.50)}{1.70} = 1.85\ \mathrm{s}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Taking the normal reaction as $mg$", r"On a banked track $N = mg/\cos\theta$, which is larger than $mg$ (without friction)."),
        MISTAKE(r"Using $mg\sin\theta$ as the centripetal force", r"The centripetal force is the **horizontal** component of $N$ (with friction), pointing to the centre of the circle, which is horizontal. It is not along the slope."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong direction of friction", r"Above the ideal speed, friction acts down the slope (the car tends to slide outwards and up). Below it, friction acts up the slope."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the ideal speed depends on the mass", r"The mass cancels, so a lorry and a car have the same ideal speed."),
        QUIZ(r"The banking angle of a frictionless curved track of radius $r$ is designed for speed $v$. If the same track were designed for a speed of $2v$ (same radius), what would happen to $\tan\theta$?",
             ["It would double", "It would quadruple", "It would be unchanged", "It would halve"], 1,
             r"$\tan\theta = v^2/(rg)$, so doubling $v$ multiplies $\tan\theta$ by $4$.",
             hint="Look at how tan(theta) depends on v.",
             feedback=["That would be so if $\\tan\\theta$ were proportional to $v$.", "", "It depends on $v^2$.", "A faster design speed needs steeper banking."]),
        QUIZ(r"A car moves round a banked track at **exactly** the ideal speed. Which statement is correct?",
             ["The friction force is at its maximum value, up the slope", "No friction force is needed", "The car has no centripetal acceleration", "The normal reaction is equal to the weight"], 1,
             r"At the ideal speed the horizontal component of $N$ alone supplies the centripetal force. Friction is not needed.",
             hint="Recall how the formula was derived.",
             feedback=["That is for a car about to slide down.", "", "The car moves in a circle, so it has centripetal acceleration $v^2/r$.", "$N = mg/\\cos\\theta$, which is greater than $mg$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A bend of radius $60\ \mathrm{m}$ is banked at $20^\circ$. Find the speed at which no friction is needed.",
           r"$v = \sqrt{rg\tan\theta} = \sqrt{60 \times 10 \times 0.364} = 14.8\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ ($53\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$)."),
        PQ(r"At what angle should a bend of radius $150\ \mathrm{m}$ be banked so that cars can take it at $25\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ without friction?",
           r"$\tan\theta = v^2/(rg) = 625/1500 = 0.417$, so $\theta = 22.6^\circ$."),
        PQ(r"A car of mass $1000\ \mathrm{kg}$ moves at the ideal speed on a track banked at $15^\circ$. Find the normal reaction and the horizontal force towards the centre.",
           r"$N = mg/\cos15^\circ = 10\,000/0.966 = 1.04\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{N}$. The horizontal component $N\sin15^\circ = mg\tan15^\circ = 2.68\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{N}$ is the centripetal force."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a cyclist leans inwards when going round a bend, and say what happens to the angle of lean if the speed is increased at the same radius.",
           r"The ground gives a resultant force on the cycle through the centre of mass, with a horizontal component towards the centre that provides the centripetal force. For no turning about the centre of mass, this resultant must act along the line from the ground to the centre of mass, so the cyclist leans at $\tan\theta = v^2/(rg)$ (with friction as the horizontal force). Increasing $v$ increases $\tan\theta$: the cyclist must lean more."),
        TIP(r"In the exam: banked tracks and aircraft turns are Paper 2 favourites. Always resolve vertically ($N\cos\theta = mg$) and horizontally ($N\sin\theta = mv^2/r$) and divide."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"On a banked track the horizontal component of the normal reaction provides the centripetal force: $N\sin\theta = mv^2/r$, $N\cos\theta = mg$.",
            r"Ideal speed: $v = \sqrt{rg\tan\theta}$, independent of the mass; $\tan\theta = v^2/(rg)$.",
            r"With friction, the maximum speed is $\sqrt{rg(\tan\theta + \mu)/(1 - \mu\tan\theta)}$.",
            "Aircraft banking and the conical pendulum have the same equations with lift or tension in place of the normal reaction.",
            "Faster design speed or tighter bend means steeper banking."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Banked tracks and vertical circles 2: vertical circles
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Banked tracks and vertical circles",
    title="Motion in a vertical circle",
    slug="phl1-vertical-circles",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Combine F = mv^2/r with conservation of energy to find tension or reaction at the top and bottom of a vertical circle, the minimum speed to complete the circle, and when a car leaves a hump-back bridge.",
    objectives=[
        "Write the equation of motion towards the centre at the top and bottom of a vertical circle",
        "Use conservation of energy to relate speeds at different heights",
        "Derive the minimum speeds sqrt(gr) at the top and sqrt(5gr) at the bottom",
        "Analyse a car on a hump-back bridge and a rider in a loop",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Vertical circle", "Tension at top and bottom", "Minimum speed at the top", "Energy conservation in a circle", "Hump-back bridge"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A child swings a bucket of water in a vertical circle and no water spills, even at the top when the bucket is upside down. A car crests a hump-back bridge and for a moment feels light. A roller coaster rider goes through a loop. In a **vertical** circle, gravity is not always at right angles to the motion, so the speed changes, and so does the force needed. We use two tools: $F = mv^2/r$ towards the centre, and conservation of energy."),
        IMG("al-phl1-vertical-circle.svg", alt="A ball on a string in a vertical circle: at the top tension and weight both point down towards the centre; at the bottom the tension points up and the weight down",
            caption="Figure 1. At the top, tension and weight both point towards the centre; at the bottom tension points to the centre and the weight points away from it."),
        H("Forces at the top and at the bottom"),
        T(r"For a ball of mass $m$ on a string of length $r$, take the direction **towards the centre** as positive."),
        T(r"**At the bottom:** tension $T$ is towards the centre (up), weight $mg$ is away from it (down). The resultant towards the centre is $T - mg$."),
        F(r"T_{\text{bottom}} - mg = \frac{mv_b^2}{r} \;\Rightarrow\; T_{\text{bottom}} = \frac{mv_b^2}{r} + mg"),
        T(r"**At the top:** tension (down) and weight (down) are both towards the centre. The resultant is $T + mg$."),
        F(r"T_{\text{top}} + mg = \frac{mv_t^2}{r} \;\Rightarrow\; T_{\text{top}} = \frac{mv_t^2}{r} - mg"),
        T(r"The tension is greatest at the bottom, where the string must both support the weight and provide the centripetal force."),
        H("Energy links the speeds"),
        D(r"With no friction, mechanical energy is conserved as the ball moves round. The top is a height $2r$ above the bottom, so:", title="Speeds at top and bottom",
          formula=r"\tfrac12mv_b^2 = \tfrac12mv_t^2 + mg(2r) \;\Rightarrow\; v_b^2 = v_t^2 + 4gr"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a stone on a string", intro=r"A $0.40\ \mathrm{kg}$ stone on a string of length $0.90\ \mathrm{m}$ moves in a vertical circle. At the bottom its speed is $8.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the tension at the bottom, the speed at the top and the tension at the top.", steps=[
            S("Tension at the bottom", math=r"T_b = \frac{mv_b^2}{r} + mg = \frac{0.40 \times 64}{0.90} + 4.0 = 28.4 + 4.0 = 32.4\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("Speed at the top from energy", math=r"v_t^2 = v_b^2 - 4gr = 64 - 4(10)(0.90) = 28 \;\Rightarrow\; v_t = 5.29\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Tension at the top", math=r"T_t = \frac{mv_t^2}{r} - mg = \frac{0.40 \times 28}{0.90} - 4.0 = 12.4 - 4.0 = 8.4\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ]),
        H("The minimum speed to complete the circle"),
        T(r"A string cannot push, so the tension cannot be negative. At the top, the smallest possible tension is zero: then **gravity alone** provides the centripetal force."),
        D(r"To keep moving in a circle (string taut) the speed at the top must satisfy $v_t^2 \ge gr$. The minimum speeds are:", title="Minimum speeds for a complete vertical circle",
          formula=r"v_{t,\min} = \sqrt{gr}, \qquad v_{b,\min} = \sqrt{v_{t,\min}^2 + 4gr} = \sqrt{5gr}",
          note="With the minimum speed at the bottom, the tension at the bottom is mv²/r + mg = 5mg + mg = 6mg."),
        EX("Worked example 2: can the stone complete the circle?", intro=r"For the stone above ($r = 0.90\ \mathrm{m}$), find the minimum speed at the top and at the bottom for a complete circle. Would a speed of $6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at the bottom be enough?", steps=[
            S("Minimum speed at the top", math=r"v_{t,\min} = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{10 \times 0.90} = 3.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Minimum speed at the bottom", math=r"v_{b,\min} = \sqrt{5gr} = \sqrt{45} = 6.71\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Compare", text="$6.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}} < 6.71\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, so it is **not** enough: the string would go slack before the top and the stone would fall in a curved path."),
        ]),
        H("Hump-back bridges and loops"),
        T(r"The same method applies when the object rests on a surface instead of a string. For a car on the top of a hump-back bridge of radius $r$, the centre of the circle is **below** the car. Weight $mg$ is towards the centre and the normal reaction $R$ is away from it:"),
        F(r"mg - R = \frac{mv^2}{r} \;\Rightarrow\; R = m\left(g - \frac{v^2}{r}\right)"),
        EX("Worked example 3: the hump-back bridge", intro=r"A $1200\ \mathrm{kg}$ car crosses the top of a hump-back bridge of radius of curvature $40\ \mathrm{m}$ at $15\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Find the reaction of the road on the car, and the speed at which the car would lose contact with the road.", steps=[
            S("Reaction at the top", math=r"R = 1200\left(10 - \frac{15^2}{40}\right) = 1200(10 - 5.625) = 5250\ \mathrm{N}"),
            S("The car leaves the road when $R = 0$", math=r"g = \frac{v^2}{r} \;\Rightarrow\; v = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{400} = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The reaction is $5250\ \mathrm{N}$, less than the weight ($12\,000\ \mathrm{N}$): the driver feels lighter. At $20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ ($72\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$) the car would become momentarily weightless and lose contact."),
        T(r"For a loop-the-loop, the rider at the bottom feels a force $N = mg + mv^2/r$ from the seat, larger than the weight. At the top of a loop the track pushes the rider **down** with $N = mv^2/r - mg$, and the rider stays on the track as long as $v^2 \ge gr$."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using $T = mg$ at the top or bottom", r"Always write the equation towards the centre: $T \pm mg = mv^2/r$. The sign of $mg$ depends on whether weight points towards or away from the centre."),
        MISTAKE("Using the same speed at the top and bottom", r"The speed is lower at the top. Use energy: $v_b^2 = v_t^2 + 4gr$."),
        MISTAKE(r"Thinking the minimum speed at the bottom is $\sqrt{gr}$", r"$\sqrt{gr}$ is the minimum at the **top**. At the bottom it is $\sqrt{5gr}$."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that a string cannot push", r"If the calculation gives a negative tension, the string has gone slack: the circle cannot be completed at that speed."),
        QUIZ(r"A ball moves in a vertical circle on a string at the minimum speed that keeps the string taut at the top. What is the tension at the top?",
             [r"$mg$", r"$2mg$", r"$0$", r"$6mg$"], 2,
             r"At the minimum speed, gravity alone provides the centripetal force at the top: $mg = mv^2/r$, so $T = 0$.",
             hint="Write T + mg = mv squared over r with the smallest allowed T.",
             feedback=["That would be the tension in a ball hanging at rest.", "Nothing requires twice the weight.", "", "That is the tension at the bottom for the minimum speed."]),
        QUIZ(r"A car passes the top of a hump-back bridge faster and faster. What happens to the normal reaction of the road on the car?",
             ["It increases", "It decreases", "It stays at $mg$", "It becomes greater than $mg$"], 1,
             r"$R = m(g - v^2/r)$ decreases as $v$ increases, becoming zero at $v = \sqrt{gr}$.",
             hint="Write the equation of motion towards the centre, which is below the car.",
             feedback=["That happens at the bottom of a dip, not the top of a hump.", "", "$R = mg$ only at zero speed.", "On a hump $R$ is always less than $mg$ while moving."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A ball of mass $0.20\ \mathrm{kg}$ on a string of length $0.50\ \mathrm{m}$ moves in a vertical circle with a speed of $4.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at the top. Find the tension at the top, and the speed and tension at the bottom.",
           r"$T_t = mv_t^2/r - mg = 0.20(16)/0.50 - 2.0 = 6.4 - 2.0 = 4.4\ \mathrm{N}$. $v_b^2 = v_t^2 + 4gr = 16 + 4(10)(0.50) = 36$, so $v_b = 6.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. $T_b = mv_b^2/r + mg = 0.20(36)/0.50 + 2.0 = 14.4 + 2.0 = 16.4\ \mathrm{N}$."),
        PQ(r"What is the minimum speed at the bottom of a vertical circle of radius $1.5\ \mathrm{m}$ for an object on a string to complete the circle?",
           r"$v_{b,\min} = \sqrt{5gr} = \sqrt{5 \times 10 \times 1.5} = \sqrt{75} = 8.66\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ (and $3.87\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ at the top)."),
        PQ(r"A hump-back bridge has a radius of curvature of $50\ \mathrm{m}$. Find the speed at which cars lose contact with the road at the top, in $\mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$.",
           r"$v = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{500} = 22.4\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}} = 80.5\ \mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A roller coaster loop has a radius of $8.0\ \mathrm{m}$. Find the minimum speed at the top for the passengers to stay in their seats without restraints, and the force from the seat on a passenger of mass $m$ at the bottom if the speed there is the minimum speed needed to complete the loop.",
           r"$v_{t,\min} = \sqrt{gr} = 8.9\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Minimum speed at the bottom $= \sqrt{5gr} = 20\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Force at the bottom: $N = mg + mv^2/r = mg + 5mg = 6mg$, six times the weight."),
        TIP("In the exam: vertical circles appear in Paper 2 with energy conservation and tension at two points. Draw both positions, write the equation towards the centre for each and link them with $v_b^2 = v_t^2 + 4gr$."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Towards the centre: at the bottom $T - mg = mv^2/r$; at the top $T + mg = mv^2/r$.",
            r"Energy: $v_b^2 = v_t^2 + 4gr$ (the top is $2r$ higher).",
            r"Minimum speeds for a complete circle (string taut): $\sqrt{gr}$ at the top and $\sqrt{5gr}$ at the bottom.",
            r"Hump-back bridge: $R = m(g - v^2/r)$; the car loses contact at $v = \sqrt{gr}$.",
            "The tension or reaction is largest at the bottom."),
    ]))

# ================================================================ M3 / Moment of inertia and rotational motion
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Moment of inertia and rotational motion",
    title="Moment of inertia, torque, angular momentum and rolling",
    slug="phl1-moment-of-inertia-rotation",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Define moment of inertia, use torque = I alpha and rotational kinetic energy, apply conservation of angular momentum, and compare rolling and sliding down a slope.",
    objectives=[
        "Define the moment of inertia I = sum of m r^2 and use the standard results for common shapes",
        "Apply torque = I alpha and the equations of rotational motion with constant angular acceleration",
        "Calculate rotational kinetic energy and use energy conservation for a rolling body",
        "State and apply the conservation of angular momentum",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Moment of inertia", "Torque and angular acceleration", "Rotational kinetic energy", "Angular momentum", "Rolling without slipping"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A potter in Foumban sets a heavy wheel spinning and it keeps turning for a long time. A figure skater pulls in her arms and spins faster. A solid ball and a hollow ball released from the top of a slope do not reach the bottom together. In each case a body **rotates**, and what matters is not only its mass but **how that mass is distributed** about the axis. This is described by the **moment of inertia**."),
        H("Moment of inertia"),
        D(r"The **moment of inertia** $I$ of a body about an axis is the sum of the products of each particle's mass and the square of its perpendicular distance from the axis. It is the rotational equivalent of mass: a measure of how hard it is to change the body's rotation.", title="Moment of inertia",
          formula=r"I = \sum m_i r_i^2",
          note="Unit: kg m². The same body has different values of I about different axes."),
        T(r"Mass far from the axis counts much more, because of the $r^2$. For a ring (hoop) of mass $M$ and radius $R$ all the mass is at distance $R$, so $I = MR^2$. For a solid disc the mass is spread inwards, so $I = \tfrac12MR^2$ (by integration)."),
        IMG("al-phl1-inertia-shapes.svg", alt="Moments of inertia of five uniform shapes: hoop, solid disc, solid sphere, rod about its centre and rod about one end",
            caption="Figure 1. Standard moments of inertia (check your textbook for the list you must memorise: they are often given in an exam)."),
        D(r"**Parallel-axis theorem:** the moment of inertia about any axis is $I = I_{\text{cm}} + Md^2$, where $I_{\text{cm}}$ is that about a parallel axis through the centre of mass and $d$ is the separation of the axes.", title="Parallel axis theorem",
          note="Check: a rod about its end: ML²/12 + M(L/2)² = ML²/3. Whether the theorem is in your syllabus varies; check your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a system of masses", intro=r"Two $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ masses are fixed at the ends of a light rod of length $1.0\ \mathrm{m}$ that rotates about its centre. Find $I$.", steps=[
            S("Each mass is $0.50$ m from the axis", math=r"I = \sum mr^2 = 2.0(0.50)^2 + 2.0(0.50)^2 = 1.0\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}"),
        ]),
        H("Rotational dynamics"),
        T(r"Rotational motion follows the same patterns as linear motion with replacements: displacement $s \to$ angle $\theta$, velocity $v \to$ angular speed $\omega$, acceleration $a \to$ angular acceleration $\alpha$, mass $m \to I$, force $F \to$ torque $\tau$."),
        CODE("""Linear                      Rotational (axis fixed)
s, v, a                     theta, omega, alpha
v = u + at                  w = w0 + alpha t
s = ut + at^2/2             theta = w0 t + alpha t^2/2
v^2 = u^2 + 2as             w^2 = w0^2 + 2 alpha theta
F = ma                      torque = I alpha
KE = mv^2/2                 KE = I w^2/2
work = F s                  work = torque x theta
power = F v                 power = torque x w
p = mv                      L = I w""", "text", title="Linear and rotational quantities"),
        D(r"The resultant torque on a body about a fixed axis equals its moment of inertia times its angular acceleration. Rotational kinetic energy is $\tfrac12I\omega^2$.", title="Torque and rotational kinetic energy",
          formula=r"\tau = I\alpha, \qquad E_k = \tfrac12I\omega^2",
          note="Derivation: a particle at radius r has F = ma = m r α, so its torque is m r² α. Summing over all particles gives τ = (Σ m r²) α = Iα."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a flywheel", intro=r"A flywheel is a uniform disc of mass $20\ \mathrm{kg}$ and radius $0.30\ \mathrm{m}$. A constant torque of $18\ \mathrm{N\,m}$ acts on it from rest. Find $I$, the angular acceleration, and the angular speed and kinetic energy after $5.0\ \mathrm{s}$.", steps=[
            S("Moment of inertia of a disc", math=r"I = \tfrac12MR^2 = \tfrac12(20)(0.30)^2 = 0.90\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}"),
            S("Angular acceleration", math=r"\alpha = \frac{\tau}{I} = \frac{18}{0.90} = 20\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Angular speed after $5.0$ s", math=r"\omega = \alpha t = 100\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Kinetic energy", math=r"E_k = \tfrac12I\omega^2 = \tfrac12(0.90)(100)^2 = 4500\ \mathrm{J}"),
        ], check=r"\theta = \tfrac12\alpha t^2 = 250\ \mathrm{rad},\ W = \tau\theta = 18 \times 250 = 4500\ \mathrm{J}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a mass on a pulley", intro=r"A cord is wrapped round a uniform disc pulley of mass $4.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ and radius $0.20\ \mathrm{m}$ that turns on a frictionless axle. A $2.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ mass hangs from the cord and is released. Find its acceleration and the tension in the cord.", steps=[
            S("Hanging mass (down positive)", math=r"mg - T = ma"),
            S("Disc: torque from the tension, with $a = R\\alpha$ and $I = \\tfrac12MR^2$", math=r"TR = I\alpha = \tfrac12MR^2\,\frac{a}{R} \;\Rightarrow\; T = \tfrac12Ma"),
            S("Add the two equations", math=r"mg = (m + \tfrac12M)a \;\Rightarrow\; a = \frac{20}{2.0 + 2.0} = 5.0\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
            S("Tension", math=r"T = \tfrac12(4.0)(5.0) = 10\ \mathrm{N}"),
        ], check=r"\text{Without the pulley's inertia } a \text{ would be } g = 10\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}"),
        H("Angular momentum"),
        D(r"The **angular momentum** of a body rotating about a fixed axis is $L = I\omega$ (unit kg m² s⁻¹). The torque equals its rate of change, $\tau = \Delta L/\Delta t$. If there is no resultant external torque, the total angular momentum stays constant: the **conservation of angular momentum**.", title="Angular momentum",
          formula=r"L = I\omega, \qquad I_1\omega_1 = I_2\omega_2 \ (\text{no external torque})"),
        EX("Worked example 4: a spinning skater", intro=r"A skater spins at $1.0$ revolution per second with her arms out, with $I = 3.6\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. She pulls her arms in, and $I$ falls to $1.2\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. Find her new rate of rotation and the change in kinetic energy.", steps=[
            S("Initial angular speed", math=r"\omega_1 = 2\pi(1.0) = 6.28\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Conservation of angular momentum", math=r"\omega_2 = \frac{I_1\omega_1}{I_2} = \frac{3.6 \times 6.28}{1.2} = 18.8\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}, \ \text{that is } 3.0 \text{ rev/s}"),
            S("Kinetic energy before and after", math=r"\tfrac12(3.6)(6.28)^2 = 71\ \mathrm{J}, \qquad \tfrac12(1.2)(18.8)^2 = 213\ \mathrm{J}"),
            S("Comment", text="The kinetic energy increases by 142 J: this is the work the skater does pulling her arms in against the 'centrifugal' tendency to fly out."),
        ]),
        H("Rolling down a slope"),
        T(r"A body that **rolls without slipping** has two kinds of kinetic energy: translational $\tfrac12mv^2$ and rotational $\tfrac12I\omega^2$, with $\omega = v/r$. Starting from rest at height $h$, energy conservation gives:"),
        F(r"mgh = \tfrac12mv^2 + \tfrac12I\frac{v^2}{r^2}"),
        EX("Worked example 5: rolling versus sliding", intro=r"A uniform solid sphere ($I = \tfrac25mr^2$) rolls from rest down a slope through a vertical height of $1.4\ \mathrm{m}$ without slipping. Find its speed at the bottom and compare with a frictionless block sliding down the same slope.", steps=[
            S("Energy conservation", math=r"mgh = \tfrac12mv^2 + \tfrac12\left(\tfrac25mr^2\right)\frac{v^2}{r^2} = \tfrac12mv^2 + \tfrac15mv^2 = \tfrac{7}{10}mv^2"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v = \sqrt{\frac{10gh}{7}} = \sqrt{\frac{10 \times 10 \times 1.4}{7}} = 4.47\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Sliding block (no rotation)", math=r"v = \sqrt{2gh} = 5.29\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The rolling body is slower because part of the energy goes into rotation. A hoop ($I = mr^2$) reaches the bottom with $v = \sqrt{gh} = 3.74\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, a disc ($I = \tfrac12mr^2$) with $\sqrt{4gh/3} = 4.32\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and the sphere $4.47\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. So the order of arrival is sphere, disc, hoop: the smaller $I/mr^2$, the faster. The speed does not depend on mass or radius."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the same $I$ for every axis", r"$I$ depends on the axis. A rod has $\tfrac1{12}ML^2$ about its centre but $\tfrac13ML^2$ about its end."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting rotational energy for a rolling body", r"A rolling body's total kinetic energy is $\tfrac12mv^2 + \tfrac12I\omega^2$. Using only $\tfrac12mv^2$ gives a speed that is too large."),
        MISTAKE(r"Using $\tau = I\alpha$ with torque and $I$ about different axes", r"Use the same axis for both."),
        MISTAKE("Using degrees or rev/min", r"In $\tau = I\alpha$ and $E_k = \tfrac12I\omega^2$, $\omega$ must be in rad s⁻¹ and $\alpha$ in rad s⁻²."),
        QUIZ(r"A hoop and a solid disc of the same mass and radius are released together from rest at the top of a slope and roll without slipping. Which reaches the bottom first?",
             ["The hoop", "The disc", "They arrive together", "It depends on the mass"], 1,
             r"The disc has the smaller moment of inertia ($\tfrac12mr^2$ against $mr^2$), so less of its energy goes into rotation and it moves faster.",
             hint="Which has the smaller moment of inertia?",
             feedback=["The hoop has more of its mass far from the axis, so it needs more rotational energy and is slower.", "", "Only a sliding (non-rolling) body is independent of its shape.", "Mass cancels in the energy equation."]),
        QUIZ(r"A skater spinning with arms outstretched pulls her arms in. Which quantity stays constant (assuming no external torque)?",
             ["Her angular speed", "Her rotational kinetic energy", "Her moment of inertia", "Her angular momentum"], 3,
             r"With no external torque, angular momentum $I\omega$ is conserved. $I$ falls, so $\omega$ rises, and kinetic energy increases (the skater does work).",
             hint="Which quantity is conserved when no external torque acts?",
             feedback=["It increases because $I$ decreases.", "It increases: the skater does work pulling her arms in.", "It decreases when the arms are pulled in.", ""]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A uniform rod of mass $3.0\ \mathrm{kg}$ and length $1.2\ \mathrm{m}$ rotates about an axis through one end. Find $I$ using the standard result and verify with the parallel-axis theorem.",
           r"$I = \tfrac13ML^2 = \tfrac13(3.0)(1.2)^2 = 1.44\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. Check: $I_{\text{cm}} = \tfrac1{12}ML^2 = 0.36$; $Md^2 = 3.0(0.60)^2 = 1.08$; the sum is $1.44\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$."),
        PQ(r"A flywheel with $I = 2.0\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$ rotates at $300$ revolutions per minute. Find its kinetic energy. How long would a constant braking torque of $20\ \mathrm{N\,m}$ take to stop it?",
           r"$\omega = 300 \times 2\pi/60 = 31.4\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$. $E_k = \tfrac12(2.0)(31.4)^2 = 987\ \mathrm{J}$. $\alpha = \tau/I = 10\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-2}}$, so $t = \omega/\alpha = 3.14\ \mathrm{s}$."),
        PQ(r"A turntable has $I = 0.50\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$ and turns at $1.2\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$. A $0.20\ \mathrm{kg}$ lump of clay is dropped onto the rim, $0.40\ \mathrm{m}$ from the axis, and sticks. Find the new angular speed.",
           r"Added moment of inertia $= mr^2 = 0.20(0.40)^2 = 0.032$, so $I_2 = 0.532\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. $\omega_2 = I_1\omega_1/I_2 = 0.50 \times 1.2/0.532 = 1.13\ \mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A uniform hoop and a uniform solid sphere, each of mass $m$ and radius $r$, roll from rest down the same slope through a height $h$. Show that the sphere reaches the bottom faster and find the ratio of their speeds.",
           r"Hoop: $mgh = \tfrac12mv^2 + \tfrac12mr^2(v/r)^2 = mv^2$, so $v = \sqrt{gh}$. Sphere: $v = \sqrt{10gh/7}$ (shown above). Ratio $v_{\text{sphere}}/v_{\text{hoop}} = \sqrt{10/7} = 1.20$. The sphere is faster, since less of its energy goes into rotation."),
        TIP("In the exam: rotational motion appears in Paper 2 (flywheels, pulleys, rolling, conservation of angular momentum); the moments of inertia of common shapes are usually given. Use the linear-rotational analogy to recall equations."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$I = \sum mr^2$ depends on the mass and its distance from the axis; standard values: hoop $MR^2$, disc $\tfrac12MR^2$, sphere $\tfrac25MR^2$.",
            r"$\tau = I\alpha$; rotational energy $\tfrac12I\omega^2$; the rotational equations parallel the linear ones.",
            r"Angular momentum $L = I\omega$ is conserved when no external torque acts.",
            r"A rolling body has $\tfrac12mv^2 + \tfrac12I\omega^2$ with $\omega = v/r$; the smaller $I$, the faster it rolls down a slope.",
            "Use the same axis for torque and moment of inertia."),
    ]))
